var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#Eka-Materials-Selection:-MVP-Task-Details","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Prepared 11 October 2026; updated 13 October 2026. Owner for all tasks: Joshua Corbett, with Codex assisting. AGENTS.md is the authoritative local board for task status, dependencies, and agent handoffs. This file holds each task's scope, artifact target, completion criteria, and eventual evidence.\n\nJoshua chose repository-local tracking instead of Linear. There is no pending publication or browser-access prerequisite. No Linear project or issues were created. Artifact targets below are proposed paths, not completed deliverables.\n\nThe charter and decision log are prepared; the research checkpoint is committed and the product branch exists. Deferred pilot/reviewer/budget tasks are not blockers.\n\nAdd dated evidence, real artifact links, verification results, and limitations to the relevant section before marking its task Done in AGENTS.md. Keep statuses and dependencies only in that board to avoid divergent copies.","title":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-01:-Define-three-demonstration-briefs","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Create self-authored examples that exercise different tradeoffs. Include an underspecified brief and an intentionally unsatisfied constraint. Do not present them as customer cases or validated engineering answers.\n\nArtifact target: docs/materials-selection-demo-briefs.md.\n\nDone when: three briefs state supplied facts, unresolved questions, intended comparison behavior, and what evidence would be needed. Choose exact applications with MVP-02; the bracket/enclosure/insulator ideas are suggestions, not fixed scope.\n\nCompletion evidence, 2026-10-13: three demonstration briefs cover a bracket, an underspecified cover, and a no-supported-match heat-spreader screen. Each separates supplied inputs, questions, evidence needs, and acceptance cases. Local links, all 14 task dependencies, three brief IDs, whitespace, and release audit passed. These are self-authored cases, not executed demos or customer validation; end-to-end checks remain MVP-11.","title":"MVP-01: Define three demonstration briefs"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-02:-Inventory-permitted-sources-and-sample-grade-coverage","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Find manufacturer documentation sufficient for the examples. Record access, intended use, dates, grade identities, available properties/conditions, and gaps. Start with sources that can be used without a new paid agreement.\n\nArtifact target: docs/materials-selection-sources.md; permitted private source files, if needed, under data/local/materials-selection/.\n\nDone when: each demo has a credible evidence route or an explicitly revised scope. Manually inspect representative records before choosing a catalogue size. Do not represent generic family pages as exact commercial grade evidence.\n\nCompletion evidence, 2026-10-13: source inventory records four inspected grade/alloy references, revision dates, property context, access failures, sourcing gaps, and manufacturer terms. An additional ceramic lead is explicitly excluded. No real source is approved for commercial reuse; the immediate scope is revised to synthetic fixtures while permissions and visual table checks remain prerequisites for real-data admission in MVP-04. Inventory links and repository release audit passed; no paid access or outreach occurred.","title":"MVP-02: Inventory permitted sources and sample grade coverage"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-03:-Establish-the-service-and-material-evidence-schema","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Confirm a small implementation stack and isolate it from the Julia research package. Model family, grade, variant, property observation, source, task, and offer identities. Preserve units, conditions, evidence type, and missing values.\n\nArtifact target: services/materials-selection/ and its schema documentation.\n\nDone when: synthetic examples round-trip through validated storage; distinct grades stay distinct; provenance survives normalization; dependencies are pinned. Record the stack decision and checks in the decision log.\n\nCompletion evidence, 2026-10-13: service README and schema, validated models, snapshot storage, and 29 focused tests. Python 3.14.6, Pydantic 2.14.1, Pint 0.26.1, and the standard-library SQLite client were tested in an isolated environment; all nine runtime packages are pinned.\n\nChecks: 29 service tests and 41 existing Python tests passed; dependency check, CLI validation/snapshot commands, release audit, and whitespace checks passed. Tests cover grade separation, dimensional conversions, original provenance, missing/conflicting values, malformed records, reference integrity, permission checks, immutable revisions, and read-only retrieval. The synthetic catalogue round-trip hash is 3b57f1e53e7889f7376a3670c499bb632ad7b0c02e12d56b2511241c79d64c70.\n\nLimits: only synthetic fixtures are included. This is storage and validation, not application approval or a comparison engine. Categorical constraints, source ingestion, UI, model calls, and MCP are not implemented. Julia code was unchanged; its test suite was not rerun in this session. See decision D10.","title":"MVP-03: Establish the service and material evidence schema"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-04:-Populate-a-small-traceable-catalogue","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Ingest and manually check enough grades for the demos. Start from the four inspected reference candidates; expand only when a demo needs more coverage. Preserve conflicting observations and revisions; keep source context available. Use synthetic fixtures for public tests and only permitted real data in the demo.\n\nArtifact target: versioned catalogue under data/local/materials-selection/ plus ingestion code and a coverage manifest in the service.\n\nDone when: every decisive value in the demos has exact source evidence, units, conditions, and a grade identity; repeat ingestion is reproducible.\n\nHandoff, 2026-10-13: begin with the four reference candidates in the source inventory, not a fixed 15-30-grade quota. Resolve an adequate rights basis and visually verify source context before admission. No real records are currently approved or ingested. This gap does not block MVP-05's synthetic implementation, but it does block real recommendations.","title":"MVP-04: Populate a small traceable catalogue"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-05:-Implement-constraint-checks-and-tradeoff-comparison","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Implement deterministic pass/fail/unknown/conflict checks and explicit preference comparisons. Validate observation applicability before numerical comparison. Keep supported matches and provisional alternatives distinct.\n\nArtifact target: service constraint/ranking functions and focused tests.\n\nDone when: equivalent units agree, unknown never becomes pass, hard failures cannot be offset by preferences, and supported alternatives expose their tradeoffs. Check typical-versus-guaranteed values and no-match behavior.\n\nProgress, 2026-11-24: the material-index worksheet compares manually screened candidates using one explicitly derived power-product index. The fictional example includes overlapping ranges and an unresolved screen. All 38 service tests passed, including nine index checks. The CLI example ranked two candidates above a third and withheld the unresolved candidate. Automatic hard checks on catalogue observations and comparisons across preferences remain open.","title":"MVP-05: Implement constraint checks and tradeoff comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-06:-Attach-exact-grade-sourcing-links","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Map grades or variants to manufacturer/distributor product pages. Capture known form, geography, date, quantity basis, and availability without inventing offers.\n\nArtifact target: service sourcing records and lookup function.\n\nDone when: every demo candidate has an exact sourcing route or an explicit gap. Generic family pages are labelled; unverified price/stock/volume capacity remain unknown. This issue does not authorize contacting suppliers or placing orders.","title":"MVP-06: Attach exact grade sourcing links"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-07:-Translate-briefs-into-editable-requirements","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Extract supplied requirements, preferences, assumptions, and open questions into the task schema. Ask high-value clarifications and let the engineer correct them. Keep any numerical derivations explicit; vague adjectives are not numeric limits.\n\nArtifact target: service requirements adapter and versioned demo prompts.\n\nDone when: all three briefs produce inspectable specifications with field origins, and user corrections reach the comparison engine. Malformed model output and unsupported inferences are rejected or exposed.","title":"MVP-07: Translate briefs into editable requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-08:-Produce-evidence-backed-shortlist-reports","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Combine requirements, comparisons, and sourcing into a report showing advantages, disadvantages, evidence, assumptions, missing facts, and actual search coverage.\n\nArtifact target: service report function and Markdown/JSON exports.\n\nDone when: three to five options appear when supported, fewer when justified, every decisive numerical claim cites a matching observation, and insufficient evidence is an explicit result. Record catalogue and task versions.","title":"MVP-08: Produce evidence-backed shortlist reports"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-09:-Build-the-local-comparison-interface","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Create a brief input, editable requirements, comparison table, and source detail view. Let users change assumptions, compare again, and export the result.\n\nArtifact target: local application in the service and setup instructions.\n\nDone when: the entire workflow works without editing files; values/conditions and evidence are inspectable; loading, error, empty, and narrow-screen states work. The first screen is the usable tool. The design does not fix the final product form.","title":"MVP-09: Build the local comparison interface"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-10:-Expose-core-operations-through-MCP","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Use an official SDK with a tested release for local stdio tools: validate a specification, search, inspect grades, compare, and retrieve evidence/sourcing. Use the same core logic as the interface, with typed inputs/outputs and limits.\n\nArtifact target: service MCP adapter, configuration example, integration checks.\n\nDone when: one real MCP host completes a demonstration and matches the core's decisions; invalid inputs, timeouts, cancellation, and absent evidence remain explicit. A host-supplied specification does not require a duplicate model call.","title":"MVP-10: Expose core operations through MCP"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-11:-Verify-the-complete-demonstration-workflow","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Exercise realistic failures involving units, grade identity, conditions, source conflicts, unsupported explanation claims, incomplete requirements, and no match. Treat source-embedded instructions as untrusted content. Verify UI and MCP paths.\n\nArtifact target: focused regression suite and a local demo verification report.\n\nDone when: the three demo briefs complete end to end, decisive claims trace to sources, known gaps are visible, and cost/latency/manual work are recorded. Results are labelled software verification, not independent engineering validation.","title":"MVP-11: Verify the complete demonstration workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-12:-Package-a-runnable-first-user-demo","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Prepare a stable local build with clear setup, example briefs, synthetic fixtures, and instructions for acquiring/loading any permitted private catalogue inputs.\n\nArtifact target: service README, reproducible dependency setup, demo bundle.\n\nDone when: setup works from a clean environment; an engineer can inspect a result, change a requirement, and compare again; restricted data and credentials are excluded from public packaging. Public hosting is optional.","title":"MVP-12: Package a runnable first-user demo"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-13:-Observe-one-engineer-using-a-real-task","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Joshua approaches an engineer with the working MVP. Observe a task and record useful suggestions, disputed comparisons, missing information, and usability friction. Get permission before retaining private project information.\n\nArtifact target: private first-user notes and a de-identified findings summary.\n\nDone when: one engineer tries a real task and provides concrete observations. Do not count a demonstration or interest alone as validated accuracy or demand. This task is future owner outreach, not a message sent by this planning turn.","title":"MVP-13: Observe one engineer using a real task"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#MVP-14:-Turn-first-user-feedback-into-the-next-iteration","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"Prioritize observed failures and useful improvements. Reconsider the initial application, sources, interface, and whether specialist review or a formal pilot now answers a concrete need. Re-estimate using actual implementation experience.\n\nArtifact target: updated backlog and dated decision-log entries.\n\nDone when: the next small iteration is justified by observed use, with clear completion criteria; deferred work stays deferred unless evidence warrants it.","title":"MVP-14: Turn first-user feedback into the next iteration"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-backlog/#Tracking-decision","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP Task Details","text":"On 12 October 2026, Joshua replaced the planned Linear setup with a local AGENTS.md task board. See decision D07. External tracker setup is not part of the build sequence. Update, 2026-10-13: MVP-01 through MVP-03 are complete; the current next-work handoff is in AGENTS.md.\n\n","title":"Tracking decision"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Materials-Selection:-Demonstration-Briefs","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Version 1, 13 October 2026. Self-authored software demonstrations, not customer cases, validated engineering answers, or evidence of market demand. All thresholds below are invented task inputs, not recommendations or derived design limits.\n\nThe source inventory separates inspected manufacturer references from approved catalogue inputs. Start implementation with fictional fixtures. Do not attach real grade names to fictional property values.","title":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#DEMO-01:-Bolted-laboratory-sensor-bracket","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"","title":"DEMO-01: Bolted laboratory sensor bracket"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Brief","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"\"Compare existing materials for a non-safety-critical sensor bracket machined from 10 mm extruded stock. It is bolted, not welded, and used indoors near room temperature. For this preliminary screen, I want a documented minimum tensile yield strength of at least 200 MPa. Prefer lower mass at unchanged geometry, and show machining and sourcing considerations. Do not approve the finished design.\"","title":"Brief"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Supplied-facts-and-limits","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Function: support a laboratory sensor; commercial stock, followed by machining.\nProcess: extrusion, machining, bolting; no welding or further heat treatment.\nStock section thickness: 10 mm, not a statement of final part dimensions.\nMandatory evidence: a published minimum for tensile yield strength. A typical tensile strength, ultimate strength, or flexural strength is not a substitute.\nPreliminary threshold: at least 200 MPa. Room temperature is qualitative; record the source's temperature or its absence, without inventing 23 degrees C.\nPreferences: lower density at identical geometry, machinability, sourcing route. No numeric cost, mass, delivery-date, or regional requirement is supplied.","title":"Supplied facts and limits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Questions-and-assumptions","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Ask about loads, geometry, fatigue, tolerable deflection, environment, fasteners, surface treatment, tolerances, and quantity before claiming design suitability. Confirm whether a datasheet limit is sufficient for this preliminary screen or whether a purchase specification/certificate is required. The demo does not infer a yield limit from a load, or turn this screen into structural verification.","title":"Questions and assumptions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Evidence-route-and-intended-behavior","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Hydro 6061-T6 and 6082-T6 extrusions are inspection candidates S-H6061 and S-H6082, not approved data imports. Their tables distinguish thickness-dependent minima from typical physical properties. Preserve each alloy, temper, source revision, and original units. Do not borrow a T6511 or special-temper claim just because it appears on the same sheet. The referenced sheets are North American sources, not evidence of stock in a user's country. See the linked source inventory.\n\nShow each requirement's supporting observation and any unresolved applicability. If the engineer raises the threshold, a published lower bound below that threshold does not prove the material's actual strength is below it: report insufficient evidence unless an applicable upper bound or other evidence establishes failure. Equivalent units must produce equivalent checks. Unknown density cannot win a minimum-mass comparison. Fewer than three options is an acceptable result.","title":"Evidence route and intended behavior"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Acceptance-cases","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Preserve the supplied 200 MPa limit and 10 mm stock thickness as supplied inputs.\nReject substitutions of ultimate/flexural strength for tensile yield strength.\nWith synthetic evidence, distinguish a sufficient lower bound, an insufficient lower bound, an incompatible thickness, and missing test conditions.\nChanging to a welded part invalidates any automatic transfer of unwelded limits.\nReport sourcing gaps and missing price/stock, without contacting suppliers.","title":"Acceptance cases"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#DEMO-02:-Hot-equipment-cover-with-incomplete-requirements","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"","title":"DEMO-02: Hot equipment cover with incomplete requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Brief-2","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"\"I need a lightweight plastic cover near a hot laboratory instrument. It should be tough, easy to machine, and ideally transparent. What materials should I use?\"","title":"Brief"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Supplied-facts-and-limits-2","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Function: equipment cover; plastic requested; machining intended.\nPreferences: low mass, toughness, machinability, transparency.\nNo numerical temperature, load, impact, thickness, chemical exposure, fire, electrical, cost, quantity, or geography limit has been supplied.","title":"Supplied facts and limits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Questions-and-assumptions-2","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Ask first for maximum continuous/peak material temperature and exposure duration; then whether transparency is mandatory, and what chemicals, impact, fire, or electrical requirements apply. Do not convert \"hot\" into a service-temperature limit, \"tough\" into a tensile-strength requirement, or \"lightweight\" into density.\n\nFor a repeatable follow-up path only, the fictional engineer replies: \"Maximum continuous exposure is 110 degrees C in dry air. Transparency is preferred, not mandatory. This is an initial comparison using guideline values, not a guarantee of performance. Chemical cleaning and impact requirements are still unknown.\" Mark these as supplied follow-up facts, not inferred facts from the initial brief.","title":"Questions and assumptions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Evidence-route-and-intended-behavior-2","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Ensinger TECANAT natural and TECAPEEK natural are reference candidates S-EPC and S-EPEEK. Their source warnings and test context matter: a service-temperature guideline is not proof of stiffness, creep life, or impact performance at that temperature. Do not substitute a melting point, glass transition, or heat distortion test for continuous-use evidence. Shape grades are not automatically interchangeable with resin grades or injection-moulded variants.\n\nInitially return clarification and provisional leads, not qualified matches. After the follow-up, keep absent environmental conditions and unverified application performance visible. An opaque candidate can remain an alternative while transparency is only a preference; making it mandatory must change the requirements without silently relaxing them. Source permissions are unresolved.","title":"Evidence route and intended behavior"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Acceptance-cases-2","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Initial extraction contains no invented numeric limits or compulsory transparency.\nUser corrections reach the saved specification, with provenance preserved.\nMissing/ambiguous service-temperature evidence stays unknown.\nGuidelines cannot pass a requirement for guaranteed performance.\nExplain tradeoffs without inventing impact performance, cost, or an exact offer.","title":"Acceptance cases"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#DEMO-03:-Heat-spreader-screen-with-no-supported-match","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"","title":"DEMO-03: Heat-spreader screen with no supported match"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Brief-3","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"\"Compare solid, uncoated stock materials for an electrically insulating heat spreader. For this preliminary reference screen, require thermal conductivity of at least 100 W/(mK) at 25 degrees C, density no greater than 2 g/cm^3, and volume resistivity of at least 10^10 ohmcm at 25 degrees C. Typical values are acceptable for screening only. Do not change the limits to make something fit.\"","title":"Brief"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Supplied-facts-and-limits-3","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Application: solid heat spreader; no coatings, foams, or assemblies in this demo.\nThree mandatory screening constraints, with units, temperatures, and acceptable evidence basis as stated above. Density temperature is not supplied.\nNo voltage, dielectric withstand, thickness, heat flux, joining process, humidity, operating life, or actual operating-temperature range is supplied.\nVolume resistivity is not dielectric strength, and neither alone qualifies electrical safety. No design approval is requested or provided.","title":"Supplied facts and limits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Questions-and-assumptions-3","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Ask for the operating environment, electrical requirements, geometry, interfaces, and whether any limit is negotiable. Do not presume that conductivity measured at 20 degrees C applies at 25 degrees C, or infer dielectric withstand from resistivity. Proposed changes require an explicit edited task version.","title":"Questions and assumptions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Evidence-route-and-intended-behavior-3","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"The initial four reference candidates span metals and polymers, not the entire materials space. S-CALN is a ceramic follow-up lead, excluded from initial numeric comparisons because its direct retrieval and unit context need checking. This omission must be visible, not disguised as an exhaustive search.\n\nThe deterministic no-match expectation belongs to the fictional fixture set: one candidate fails density, another fails conductivity, and a third lacks applicable resistivity evidence. Thus none is a supported match. This does not assert that no real material exists. Every failed/unknown constraint remains visible; a good preference score cannot rescue a failed mandatory requirement.","title":"Evidence route and intended behavior"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#Acceptance-cases-3","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Zero supported matches is a valid completed report, not a model error.\nSeparate known screening failures from unknown or conflicting evidence.\nAn out-of-coverage ceramic lead stays unranked until exact evidence is admitted.\nA subsequent relaxed-density task is a new explicit version, not a hidden fix.\nSurface a source's malformed unit for review; never silently repair it.","title":"Acceptance cases"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-demo-briefs/#First-implementation-slice","page":"Materials Selection: Demonstration Briefs","text":"Start with DEMO-01's structured inputs, synthetic grades and sources, and exact provenance. Establish storage round trips before implementing comparison. Carry all three briefs into later requirements, UI, and MCP checks. These acceptance cases describe expected software behavior; they have not yet been executed end to end. MVP-11 will record actual demonstration results.\n\n","title":"First implementation slice"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity/#Label-sensitivity-workflow-and-reproduction","page":"Label-sensitivity workflow and reproduction","text":"The label-sensitivity protocol was frozen on 31 August 2026 after the original pilot report and before alternative-policy results. The full local experiment and exact reproduction are complete. Scientific outputs remain ignored by Git under reports/local/mp-label-sensitivity-v1-2026-08-31/; start with its REPORT.md.\n\nThe original v1 protocol, scoring definitions and preserved run remain unchanged. An explicit ID-to-file-and-hash registry now supports both frozen protocols. The v1 checksum is still f64c1fb803da3cc57aff658341b299824e3d662cc48039586a8bc10410bab21f; the sensitivity checksum is f577444465292b6f1099e2650eec22106aebfb2ce3b970b86a5a037bc578a09f.","title":"Label-sensitivity workflow and reproduction"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity/#Entry-points","page":"Label-sensitivity workflow and reproduction","text":"The dedicated research runner reaches this module as EkaCompositions.Research.MPLabelSensitivity. Research modules are internal and unexported, and no research subcommand is wired into src/cli.jl, so no new mode or policy is silently added to v1's benchmark-pu CLI. It verifies the snapshot, audit and captured original pilot bundle before running anything new. Every original ranking, metric and raw report is recomputed and compared byte for byte as a compatibility check. Evaluation-only branches then consume the preserved original rankings, retaining their scores and relative order.\n\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. scripts/run_label_sensitivity.jl \\\n  data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot \\\n  reports/local/mp-ternary-audit \\\n  reports/local/mp-recovery-pilot-v1-2026-08-31/results \\\n  reports/local/new-label-sensitivity-results\npython3 scripts/analyze_label_sensitivity.py \\\n  reports/local/new-label-sensitivity-results \\\n  reports/local/new-label-sensitivity-analysis\n\nOutput directories must not exist. Explicit --synthetic is the only optional runner argument; the input snapshot and original pilot must both be synthetic. Seeds and budgets come from the verified original bundle, with all real v1 seeds/budgets mandatory. There are no real-data overrides for selecting a subset.\n\nSynthetic end-to-end tests use budgets 1 and 2, because the full excluded-policy fixture has fewer candidates than the original fixture. The old synthetic v1 example's budget 4 deliberately cannot be silently truncated to fit that branch. An infeasible declared branch rejects the whole experiment before new rankings.","title":"Entry points"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity/#Output-contract","page":"Label-sensitivity workflow and reproduction","text":"Six branches combine evaluation_only / full_pipeline with original, exclude_mixed, and unlabel_mixed. All real branches have 20 splits, three methods and four budgets: 1,440 metric rows in total, with 360 complete rankings and 480 membership files. The original controls deliberately duplicate v1.\n\nevaluation_only keeps original mixed training positives. Exclusion removes mixed candidates and compacts ranks; every row retains its original rank and metrics record original depth at k. Unlabelling changes evaluation labels only.\nfull_pipeline reconstructs labels and splits, removes mixed training positives under both alternatives, and recomputes scores. Alternatives share training/holdouts but have different candidate populations. Common-candidate scores and relative ordering agree, as required by the ranking definitions.\nmetrics.tsv records populations, mixed membership, excluded groups, policy counts, every metric denominator, seeds and eligible-budget semantics.\nconfig.toml hashes all deterministic outputs, copied source inputs, baseline files, implementation and dependencies. runtime.tsv is separate.\nanalysis/ contains independent validation, all paired differences and changes from original, all budgets/methods, population tables, and the descriptive report.\n\nThe independent Python analyzer reconstructs policy memberships and checks ranks, fixed-score/order/depth preservation, common-candidate invariance, random keys, popularity scores, complete metric grids and exact denominators. Full similarity recomputation is established by the separate Julia rerun, not by pretending the Python analyzer implements another independent cosine engine.","title":"Output contract"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity/#Freeze-and-restore","page":"Label-sensitivity workflow and reproduction","text":"The experiment used a dirty working tree based on commit 568ed605c131edfa40695afc53781015c9739f6c. It did not claim that commit alone contains the sensitivity implementation. The local implementation-freeze.json records every captured tracked/untracked source hash, exact source archive, binary Git patch, Julia/Python versions, and Project/Manifest hashes. The earlier design-freeze.json records the prospective protocol and original pilot identity.\n\nTo restore, create a fresh checkout at the base commit, overlay the captured environment/source-tree.tar, copy its environment/Manifest.toml, then use Julia 1.12.6 and Pkg.instantiate() without updating or resolving dependencies. Verify every captured source hash and ensure the manifest bytes remain unchanged. The local REPRODUCE.md and reproduction-commands.json give the exact workflow. The archive contains project sources, not the original MP snapshot or pilot: those retained local inputs are still required, with their recorded hashes.\n\nThe separate restore used the same macOS arm64 platform and installed dependency depot. It is not an empty-depot/network-free installation or a cross-platform bitwise guarantee. All 925 deterministic output files, the config, 1,440 metric rows and seven analysis files matched exactly. Original v1's sealed evidence inventory was verified unchanged too. Runtime is intentionally excluded.","title":"Freeze and restore"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity/#Verification-and-remaining-work","page":"Label-sensitivity workflow and reproduction","text":"julia --startup-file=no --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test(; allow_reresolve=false)'\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_mp_export.py' -v\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_pu_analysis.py' -v\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_label_sensitivity_analysis.py' -v\n\nAll 4,061 Julia checks, 10 exporter tests, five pilot-analysis tests and four sensitivity-analysis tests passed before the real run. The restored checkout also passed the full Julia suite and sensitivity-analysis tests. Tests include hand-calculated filtering/depth metrics, policy membership, training isolation, deterministic reruns, original controls, tampering with rewritten checksums, infeasible budgets and overwrite refusal. CI includes the synthetic workflows; remote CI status should be checked for the relevant commit.\n\nThe subsequent system-holdout study and element-pair comparator are separately frozen and reproduced; neither changes this sensitivity design or its preserved results. Interpretation and the next policy-carry-forward decision are in the local report, separate from the frozen design. Publication permissions remain open; the supplied MP terms now support preparation of attributed results and covered data. MIT applies separately to original code; final release review remains distinct.\n\n","title":"Verification and remaining work"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-data-provenance-review/#MP-pilot:-data-and-provenance-review","page":"MP pilot: data and provenance review","text":"Reviewed 31 August 2026 for the recovery protocol. Decision: continue the local pilot; redistribution is not cleared. This is a research handling record, not a legal opinion or blanket licence grant.\n\nFollow-up on 31 August 2026: the owner supplied the MP terms, including the CC BY 4.0 Content grant and permission for attributed processed results. The terms evidence review and publication-permissions register supersede this initial review's missing-text status. Preparation is supported for covered content; final artifact review and specific exceptions remain separate. The initial access failures below are historical evidence, not an absent grant.","title":"MP pilot: data and provenance review"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-data-provenance-review/#Provenance-interpretation","page":"MP pilot: data and provenance review","text":"The installed emmet-core 0.87.2 emmet/core/provenance.py and the upstream main file retrieved on this date are byte-identical, SHA-256 1bdc53348b95c93091559426881d7d39ab174cdae34b77a923414587abe8bb75. In ProvenanceDoc.from_SNLs, experimental history is aggregated with any, and theoretical is its negation; database identifiers are collected separately. This supports preserving explicit flags rather than deriving a label from an identifier's presence. This file comparison does not establish the server's builder version or verify the source literature. Source implementation.\n\nDay 1 verified preserved JSONL, normalized TSV, group membership, labels, counts, and source-ID serialization. The predetermined sample uses five positive-only, five unlabelled, and five mixed-flag groups, hash-sampled with seed 20260831. Source records and findings remain in the ignored local review folder. This is a consistency review, not a manual literature check or certification of synthesis. No label rule was changed and no ranking performance was inspected.","title":"Provenance interpretation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-data-provenance-review/#Separate-software,-data,-and-source-rights","page":"MP pilot: data and provenance review","text":"Area Evidence / limitation Handling decision\nEka code Following owner confirmation, MIT is applied to original code in LICENSE. Reused examples and dependencies retain third-party notices. Software licensing is resolved separately; it grants no rights over input datasets or their derivatives.\nMP terms The MP-managed AWS registry points to MP Terms of Use. Direct retrieval of the current terms page failed, including an HTTP 403 response through the web reader. Record the gap. Do not mark all exported fields or derived outputs cleared.\nMP attribution The MP-managed registry links the Jain et al. Materials Project paper. The official legacy API documentation lists the Ong et al. API paper, but is not current API terms. Preserve MP source, database version, retrieval time, processing changes, and relevant citations. Verify current citation requirements before publication.\nGNoME MP release notes explicitly identify a BY-NC licence and access acceptance for GNoME data. The snapshot query excludes GNoME. Preserve the exclusion; it does not establish uniform rights for all remaining sources.\nExternal database IDs database_IDs can name third-party sources. Retaining an MP-provided ID does not grant access to, or redistribution rights over, its source database. Do not download or republish ICSD/Pauling source records under assumed MP rights. Specific identifier/field and derivative permissions remain unresolved.\nCC BY 4.0 Where applicable, the CC deed requires credit, a licence link, and an indication of changes, without implying endorsement; other rights may remain. These conditions do not establish that every field in this snapshot is covered. Apply only after coverage is confirmed.\n\nSources consulted:\n\nMP-managed data registry: terms link and project citation. This pilot used the API, not AWS; do not claim an AWS retrieval.\nMP Terms of Use: current text could not be retrieved; still requires review.\nMP database release notes: GNoME restrictions, notably the v2024.12.18 entry. Search-accessible content was inspected; direct local retrieval failed.\nOfficial legacy API documentation: API citation, explicitly an older API reference.\nCC BY 4.0 deed: general attribution conditions, not dataset-specific clearance.\n\nDo not substitute an old snapshot licence, a search snippet, a website footer, or a software licence for the applicable current dataset/source terms. A direct source-terms review for ICSD and Pauling remains open; no assertion is made that their direct-service contracts automatically govern this MP export.","title":"Separate software, data, and source rights"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-data-provenance-review/#Field-handling-and-publication-gate","page":"MP pilot: data and provenance review","text":"The export retains material_id, composition, formula_pretty, theoretical, database_IDs, and deprecated. Only canonical composition is a candidate feature; training membership comes from the frozen grouping rule. IDs and flags are for provenance/evaluation, never predictive features. No original crystal structures or bibliographic abstracts were requested by this export.\n\nFor now none of the exported fields is cleared for redistribution by this review. Preserve the frozen originals locally; keep record-level files, samples, coverage tables, splits, rankings, and derived results in ignored directories. Original code, synthetic fixtures, and methodological documentation are covered by MIT; reused Seko example pairs retain their upstream notice. Source code has been committed and pushed separately. No MP dataset or data-derived release has been cleared, and no clarification request has been sent to source providers.\n\nBefore sharing data or a data-derived summary:\n\nRetrieve and review the current MP terms and any applicable contribution terms; record review date and the exact field/output classes covered.\nResolve the handling of external source identifiers and any source restrictions, distinguishing MP-provided metadata from original third-party records.\nRecord approved attribution, licence links, source version/date, and a statement of canonicalization, grouping, splitting, and other modifications.\nPreserve Eka's MIT licence and third-party notices; review intended files for credentials, raw data, local environments, and unreviewed derivatives.\n\nUntil then, the release fallback is a prepared code/synthetic-input package with no unreviewed data derivatives. These open distribution questions do not require new MP API access or a change to the scientific label rule.\n\n","title":"Field handling and publication gate"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-learned-decision/#Learned-comparator-feasibility-decision","page":"Learned-comparator feasibility decision","text":"Decision recorded 31 August 2026, before real element-pair fitting/ranking. Go for one frozen evaluation of the fixed-compute element-pair model. This is not a decision that the model is scientifically useful or converged.\n\nThe specification defines the question, axes, objective, low-confidence missing-pair assumption, coverage fallback and training-only boundaries. The evaluation protocol freezes the model settings, 2,000-iteration cap and reporting obligations. No hyperparameter tuning or real recovery inspection informed these settings.\n\nSynthetic numerical checks passed, including finite-difference gradients and an analytical stationary point. Full workflow checks cover factor-derived scores, objective/residual consistency, membership, ranking, coverage, metrics and rejection of rehashed corruption. Scoring does not refit or mutate the model.\n\nOn this machine, the small synthetic case reached its projected-gradient criterion in 502 iterations. The artificial size check used 4,288 training and 9,293 candidate compositions and reached the 2,000-iteration cap. Its warm fit took about 0.15 seconds, allocated about 86 MiB cumulatively, and ranking took about 0.02 seconds. Allocation is not peak memory. All 9,293 candidates received finite scores, including 4,513 with the explicit unseen-element zero fallback. These generated cases are engineering checks, not estimates of real recovery.\n\nThe capped larger fit has not established convergence. The prospective evaluation therefore reports each fit's stop reason, full objective trace, residual, coverage and ties. It evaluates the fixed-compute state without claiming an optimum or increasing iterations after seeing results. A failed line search/nonfinite state will fail the experiment rather than silently dropping a branch.\n\nThe synthetic cost suggests roughly 20 seconds of fitting and scoring for 120 such cases, excluding input validation, file output, analysis, compilation and reproduction. Real convergence and end-to-end runtime must be measured separately. This replaces an unsupported 30-hour model-development commitment with one bounded benchmark; it is not an estimate for a tuned model or production deployment.\n\nThe prototype is deliberately limited: stoichiometries within a system tie; missing pairs are weak zero targets rather than verified negatives; cold elements receive zero; factors may encode research frequency. A favorable benchmark result would not remove these limitations. A negative result will be retained and will not automatically trigger model tuning or a larger implementation.\n\nSynthetic evidence is preserved locally under reports/local/element-pair-feasibility-2026-08-31/. Numerical and workflow tests are tracked; real scientific evidence is recorded separately.\n\n","title":"Learned-comparator feasibility decision"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/release-readiness/#Source-release-readiness","page":"Source release readiness","text":"Reviewed 7 September 2026. This gate covers a source archive generated by git archive; it does not cover a local evidence directory, MP snapshot, candidate-level ranking bundle, dependency environment or production Seko database.","title":"Source release readiness"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/release-readiness/#Ordered-pre-tag-checklist","page":"Source release readiness","text":"Run this checklist from the repository root. The candidate is the exact commit printed in step 1; do not tag it if HEAD changes, the working tree becomes dirty, or any later step fails. Start again from step 1 instead.\n\nFreeze and identify the candidate:\ngit status --short\ngit rev-parse HEAD\ngit branch --show-current\nRequire an empty status and the intended release branch. Record the full commit SHA in the release notes.\nRun the Julia package gate:\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\nRun the Python gate:\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_*.py'\nAudit the archive made from the candidate commit, retaining the audit outside the repository:\ncandidate=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\npython3 scripts/verify_release.py --archive \"$candidate\" --output /tmp/eka-release-audit.json\nRequire \"status\": \"pass\" and an empty problems list.\nPush that commit and require every job in its GitHub Actions run to pass, including Documentation. Confirm that the development manual is reachable at https://JoshCCorby.github.io/EkaCompositions.jl/dev/.\nImmediately before tagging, repeat step 1 and require the recorded SHA and clean status to be unchanged. The tag must point to that SHA.","title":"Ordered pre-tag checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/release-readiness/#Findings-from-the-6-September-2026-review","page":"Source release readiness","text":"The post-documentation candidate was clean before review. The three local gates passed:\n\nJulia 1.12.6: 4,756 of 4,756 assertions passed.\nPython 3.14.6: all 36 discovered tests passed.\nThe exact-commit archive audit passed with an empty problems list.\n\nGitHub Actions run 34040718639 passed for the same source tree: Julia 1.10 and current Julia passed on Ubuntu, current Julia passed on macOS and Windows, the analysis and exporter jobs passed on Python 3.11–3.14, and the Documentation job built and deployed the manual. GitHub Pages is configured to publish / from the gh-pages branch with HTTPS enforced.\n\nThese findings establish source-release readiness only. They do not create a tag or GitHub release, and they do not approve publication of any local research artifact.","title":"Findings from the 6 September 2026 review"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/release-readiness/#Candidate-contents","page":"Source release readiness","text":"The source candidate contains Eka's Julia and Python source, methodological documentation, synthetic examples, tests, the small noticed Seko-derived fixture, aggregate prose findings, MIT licence, third-party notices and citation metadata. Git archive attributes exclude local activity logs. Ignored local data and evidence directories are not members of the archive.\n\nThe aggregate findings identify Materials Project as the data source, database version 2026.04.13, retrieval time, transformations, GNoME exclusion, citation and non-endorsement limitation. No material IDs, candidate-level rankings, factors, MP snapshots, API keys or complete dependency environments are included.","title":"Candidate contents"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/release-readiness/#Archive-gate-details","page":"Source release readiness","text":"The audit builds the actual Git archive and fails on:\n\nlocal data, report or activity-log paths;\nunreviewed JSONL/SQLite/database files, except the documented tiny fixture;\nenvironment files and Manifest.toml;\nsymbolic links, hard links and other special archive members;\nprivate-key markers and common GitHub, AWS and literal MP API-key forms;\nmachine-specific home paths, files over the review limit, missing licence, notice/citation metadata, or absent MP/Seko attribution.\n\nThe JSON output records every regular archive member's type, mode, size and SHA-256 plus a manifest hash. It is a content-control aid, not a guarantee that arbitrary secrets or all legal issues can be recognized automatically. CI runs the audit and its focused tests on every push and pull request.","title":"Archive gate details"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/release-readiness/#Terms-and-decision","page":"Source release readiness","text":"The terms evidence now includes a read-only check of the live official MP page on 31 August 2026. That page confirms CC BY 4.0 for MP Content with credit, licence link and change indication, and separately assigns GNoME CC BY-NC 4.0. The project excluded GNoME. MP asks users to contact it for guidance before repackaging and publicly redistributing MP data.\n\nThe source-only archive is ready as a release candidate because it does not repackage record-level MP data and retains the relevant attribution and notices. No tag, GitHub release or data publication is performed by this review. A future MP data/results bundle must receive a separate file-by-file review and may warrant provider guidance based on its concrete contents.","title":"Terms and decision"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/release-readiness/#0.1.1-candidate-scope","page":"Source release readiness","text":"Version 0.1.1 applies this same ordered gate after adding reproducible code and synthetic tests for the subgroup, system-concentration and 20,000-iteration stability diagnostics. Its release notes explicitly describe a reproducible ranking benchmark rather than a validated discovery model. The tag and GitHub source-only release must identify the exact candidate SHA; that external record avoids the impossible self-reference of embedding a commit's own SHA in the commit. No data, rankings, factors, environment or other evidence asset is to be attached to the release.\n\n","title":"0.1.1 candidate scope"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#Recovery-project:-ordered-task-checklist","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"Prepared 31 August 2026 from the proposed post-pilot roadmap and its review.\n\nOrder: finish the frozen pilot → test label sensitivity and resolve sharing → evaluate chemical-system holdout → decide whether to add a learned comparator → package the findings.\n\nThis document adopts the supplied revised roadmap as the working plan. It does not amend the frozen v1 protocol. Checkboxes below record verified progress as of 31 August 2026; unmarked work remains pending. Dates and effort estimates are provisional; advance when each completion gate is met.","title":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#Progress-recorded-31-August-2026","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"The benchmark and evidence-packaging steps are complete locally: the original pilot and both label-sensitivity analyses validate and reproduce. Their distinct protocols were frozen before respective evaluation; v1's identity and artifacts remain unchanged. See pilot reproduction and label-sensitivity workflow.\n\nThe local evidence directories are reports/local/mp-recovery-pilot-v1-2026-08-31/ and reports/local/mp-label-sensitivity-v1-2026-08-31/. Scientific results, rankings, source records and unreviewed derivatives remain outside Git. The sensitivity report records the subsequent decision to carry all three label policies into the proposed system-holdout design; v2 was subsequently frozen, implemented and evaluated separately.\n\nStep 3 has a verified environment restore and an artifact-by-artifact permissions register. The owner supplied MP terms permitting attributed processed results and licensing Content under CC BY 4.0; the evidence review records the copy's checksum, current-version caveat and retained fields. Original code is MIT with scoped third-party notices. Final data-release review remains separate; there is no blanket provider-permission blocker for covered uses.\n\nThe system-holdout comparison and a subsequent fixed-compute learned comparator are complete. Steps 7–9 now have a feasibility decision, frozen learned protocol, complete evaluation and separate-checkout refit reproduction. The learned run contains 480 new metric rows and reproduced all 992 deterministic files, its full config and six analysis files.\n\nThe combined findings retain every method/design/policy, including unfavorable comparisons and the 101 capped fits. Code, synthetic tests, methods and the attributed aggregate summary form the sharing package; detailed records, rankings, factors and environments remain local. Final review of a future data bundle remains separate. No provider message or new API query was made.\n\nThe ordered benchmark work is complete locally. Further model tuning, convergence studies or label audits require a new question and prospective design; they are not automatically authorized by a favorable branch. No literature-based label changes were made. See execution and evidence for restore instructions.\n\nThe subsequent audit hardening compatibility check found no normalization mismatch in the frozen snapshot. Reaudited composition and exclusion files and all 81 v1 membership files matched exactly, so the frozen scientific runs remain unchanged.","title":"Progress recorded 31 August 2026"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#1.-Finish-the-existing-pilot-without-changing-its-design","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"Preserve the frozen v1 protocol. Do not fold the later experiments into the first run.\n\n[x] Record the implementation commit, protocol hash, input snapshot identifier/hash, and exact dependency environment.\n[x] Execute all 20 planned splits, all four budgets, and all three methods: random, popularity, and similarity.\n[x] Save the complete outputs in an immutable run directory (operational freeze: SHA-256 inventory and write protection; not WORM storage).\n[x] Validate split membership, rankings, metrics, and the expected set of outputs.\n[x] Reproduce one split in a separate directory and compare membership, rankings, and metrics exactly, as required by the existing plan.\n[x] Reproduce the required workflow from a clean checkout using the recorded environment.\n[x] Report every split's D_s = H_sim@100 − H_pop@100, its mean and median, and the positive/zero/negative counts.\n[x] Describe variation across splits as split sensitivity. Preserve v1's rule against confidence intervals and significance claims.\n[x] Write a short pilot report explaining the question, comparison, observed effect, and limitations.\n\nInterpretation: the pilot measures recovery of withheld provenance-labelled compositions under random composition holdout. A similarity advantage can be valid for that task. It does not establish synthesis success or broad discovery ability. Unlabelled candidates are not known failed syntheses.\n\nDone when: the planned run is complete, validates, reproduces, and has a report that separates measured results from interpretation.","title":"1. Finish the existing pilot without changing its design"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#2.-Correct-the-roadmap's-claims-and-record-the-next-decisions","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"Do this after the pilot report, before inspecting any new experimental rankings.\n\n[x] Replace “expect the margin to collapse” with three hypotheses: similarity's advantage shrinks, persists, or reverses under system holdout.\n[x] Remove claims that the pilot advantage is necessarily an artifact or that a particular result will be the most publishable.\n[x] Define system holdout as testing transfer to unseen element combinations, not necessarily to chemically distant materials.\n[x] Record the concrete limitation: BaTiO3 and SrTiO3 belong to different systems, so one can remain in training while the other is held out.\n[x] Remove the automatic rule that a similarity–popularity tie moves model development ahead of evaluation design.\n[x] If the pilot produces a tie, inspect baseline strength, score ties, representation, label composition, and implementation correctness before choosing the next experiment. Keep any exploratory diagnostics separate from frozen results.\n[x] Define the next analyses and their interpretation rules before running them; document subsequent changes as new versions.\n\nDone when: the roadmap allows all plausible outcomes and states exactly what each experiment can and cannot establish.","title":"2. Correct the roadmap's claims and record the next decisions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#3.-Resolve-reproducibility-and-sharing-requirements","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"Run this work alongside Step 4 where possible. Unresolved publication terms need not block permitted local analysis.\n\n[x] Choose and document how exact environments are restored: commit the appropriate environment lockfile or deliberately preserve a complete environment with each run.\n[x] Verify the chosen approach from a clean checkout and document the restore command/workflow.\n[x] Record owner confirmation and apply MIT to original code, with scoped notices for reused Seko examples and dependencies; third-party data permissions remain separate.\n[x] Record owner-supplied MP terms and their provenance limits; independently retrieved current-page applicability remains a final data-release check.\n[x] Record the supplied text's checksum, intended source, review date, unavailable version date, six retained fields and general-grant scope limits.\n[x] Create a publication-permissions table covering your code, dependencies, synthetic fixtures, aggregate results, composition records, provenance fields, and other derived artifacts.\n[x] For each category, record the basis for sharing, attribution requirements, restrictions, and unresolved questions.\n[x] Do not mark any category as definitely publishable or prohibited without establishing the applicable basis.\n\nDone when: an exact environment can be restored, the code-licensing decision is recorded, and every proposed publication artifact has a documented status. Unresolved items remain excluded from release.","title":"3. Resolve reproducibility and sharing requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#4.-Test-sensitivity-to-the-positive-label-rule","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"Do this before committing to a new model. The original rule is “at least one MP record has theoretical=false,” an experimental-provenance proxy rather than a direct synthesis claim.","title":"4. Test sensitivity to the positive-label rule"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#4.1-Freeze-the-sensitivity-design","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"[x] Confirm the number and distribution of composition groups with mixed flags; the original roadmap reports 1,343, which needs verification against the frozen snapshot.\n[x] Define three label policies: the original rule, mixed-flag groups excluded, and mixed-flag groups treated as unlabelled.\n[x] Distinguish the two analyses below and predefine their metrics, eligible populations, and denominators.\n[x] Preserve the original pilot outputs. Give each new policy and analysis its own identifier and reproducible configuration.\n[x] Before multiple frozen protocols coexist, generalise protocol pinning to a version-to-hash mapping, or an equivalent explicit versioned mechanism, without changing v1's identity.","title":"4.1 Freeze the sensitivity design"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#4.2-Run-an-evaluation-only-analysis","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"[x] Hold the original fitted state and rankings fixed while changing the evaluation label rule.\n[x] Specify whether excluded groups are filtered and ranks recomputed or handled through a separately defined eligible evaluation set; do not change the meaning of the budget silently.\n[x] Report how the measured effect changes and identify that original training labels were retained.\n[x] Describe this as sensitivity of evaluation to relabelling, not as a full run under the alternative training-label policy.","title":"4.2 Run an evaluation-only analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#4.3-Run-the-full-alternative-label-analyses","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"[x] Rebuild valid training and evaluation membership under each policy.\n[x] Ensure groups now classified as unlabelled are not retained as training positives.\n[x] Remove excluded groups from the relevant populations and recompute pool sizes and metric denominators.\n[x] Recompute all affected training-derived scores and rerun the methods. Reuse seeds or group assignments only where valid; do not assume the old splits remain valid unchanged.\n[x] Report training counts, candidate counts, eligible positive counts, and the sign and magnitude of the method differences under each policy.\n[x] If conclusions are label-sensitive, consider a small literature audit with its sampling rule and assessment criteria frozen before inspection.\n\nDone when: the report quantifies label sensitivity and clearly distinguishes changing evaluation labels from changing the full training/evaluation pipeline.","title":"4.3 Run the full alternative-label analyses"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#5.-Design-and-freeze-chemical-system-holdout-v2","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"[x] Specify a chemical system as a canonical unordered set of elements and document how it is constructed from a composition.\n[x] Define exactly how whole systems are assigned to training and candidate populations, including unlabelled records, eligibility rules, and edge cases.\n[x] Document how seeded system selection works and how it handles unequal system sizes.\n[x] Define the question narrowly: how does recovery change when candidate element combinations have no training positives?\n[x] Predefine how v1 and v2 will be compared on the same snapshot and with clearly specified label policies.\n[x] Require reporting of training-positive count, candidate count, held-out-positive count/prevalence, number of systems, and concentration of positives in large systems.\n[x] Include the random baseline and within-protocol method differences; do not interpret raw hits@100 changes alone as the effect of chemical separation.\n[x] Add a candidate-to-training similarity diagnostic under both protocols, using a documented representation and distance/similarity rule.\n[x] If making a causal claim about chemical separation, predefine a matched-size control to reduce confounding by training and pool sizes. Otherwise limit the conclusion to the observed protocol difference.\n[x] Freeze the v2 protocol, metrics, diagnostics, and any controls before looking at v2 rankings.\n\nDone when: v2 defines a reproducible experiment with explicit limits, comparison rules, and no assumed direction of the result.","title":"5. Design and freeze chemical-system holdout v2"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#6.-Implement,-validate,-and-run-v2","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"[x] Implement the versioned system-grouped split algorithm without altering v1 behavior.\n[x] Add meaningful checks for system separation, composition disjointness, deterministic membership, and isolation from evaluation labels.\n[x] Split src/mp_pu.jl only if the new implementation needs clearer boundaries between verification, ranking, metrics, and reporting; file length alone is not a requirement to refactor.\n[x] Run random, popularity, and similarity under v2 on the frozen snapshot.\n[x] Run the predefined diagnostics and any matched-size controls.\n[x] Reproduce the required outputs from the captured environment.\n[x] Report v1 and v2 side by side, including population differences and similarity distributions.\n[x] Explain whether the advantage shrinks, persists, or reverses, without assuming any change proves the removal of chemical analogues.\n[x] If label sensitivity was material, carry the relevant alternative policies into v2 or explicitly restrict the scope of the conclusion.\n\nDone when: the protocol difference is quantified, reproducible, and interpreted with its population and representation differences visible.","title":"6. Implement, validate, and run v2"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#7.-Decide-whether-a-learned-comparator-is-worth-building","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"Phase 03 is optional. Make this decision from the remaining research question, available time, and the results of Steps 4–6.\n\n[x] Write the specific question a learned comparator would answer beyond the existing methods.\n[x] Specify one initial matrix/tensor representation, its axes, objective, and regularisation.\n[x] State how unobserved entries are handled without silently treating them as verified negatives.\n[x] Specify how the model scores every eligible candidate, including systems or entities without a fitted latent factor.\n[x] Define hyperparameter selection using training information only, with any internal validation separated from final evaluation labels.\n[x] Build a small end-to-end feasibility demonstration with synthetic or training-only data.\n[x] Confirm coverage, runtime, reproducibility, and that the chosen representation supports the intended holdout task.\n[x] Make a written go/no-go decision and update the effort estimate. Do not commit to the original 30-hour estimate before this gate.\n\nDone when: a viable model specification and demonstration justify the work, or a documented decision defers it.","title":"7. Decide whether a learned comparator is worth building"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#8.-If-justified,-fit-and-evaluate-the-learned-comparator","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"[x] Explicitly amend the documented scope that currently excludes Tucker/CP/NMF/SVD training.\n[x] Fit independently within each outer split, using only permitted training information.\n[x] Isolate preprocessing, feature fitting, model selection, and caches as well as model fitting.\n[x] Verify that changing evaluation labels cannot change the fitted model or its scores when permitted training inputs are fixed.\n[x] Test the predefined behavior for unseen systems/entities and record scoring coverage; do not silently drop unscorable candidates.\n[x] Freeze the learned-method configuration before final evaluation.\n[x] Compare against random, popularity, and similarity under both holdout designs, preserving the same eligible candidate populations within each comparison.\n[x] Include relevant label-sensitivity analysis where needed to support the conclusions.\n[x] Describe the method as an implementation in the same model family, not a reproduction of or direct comparison with Seko's published model.\n\nDone when: the learned method runs reproducibly, passes isolation checks, and answers the stated additional question.","title":"8. If justified, fit and evaluate the learned comparator"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#9.-Package-the-evidence-and-reassess-the-next-commitment","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"[x] Assemble protocols, environment records, run identifiers, validation instructions, and results into a coherent report/package.\n[x] Separate frozen analyses, exploratory diagnostics, measured observations, and hypotheses.\n[x] State the limits of the provenance label, random composition holdout, and system holdout.\n[x] Include unfavourable, null, and contradictory results under the same reporting rules.\n[x] Release only artifacts cleared in Step 3; retain unresolved categories locally.\n[x] Replace provisional estimates with observed effort and runtime before planning further work.\n\nDone when: a reader can understand and reproduce the permitted evidence without inferring synthesis success or unsupported discovery claims.","title":"9. Package the evidence and reassess the next commitment"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#Rules-that-apply-throughout","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"If leakage or a normalisation defect is found, version the correction, preserve an audit trail, and rerun every affected method. Do not retain a favourable invalid result as the headline.\nDo not tune methods or redesign protocols to obtain an expected collapse or advantage. Treat changes after inspection as exploratory or freeze a new experiment.\nDo not infer first-publication dates from MP creation timestamps. Defer temporal evaluation until a defensible source exists.\nKeep the chemistry scope at oxygen-containing ternaries unless an explicit new protocol changes it; do not imply these are all validated oxides.\nDefer dashboards, unrelated CLI work, broader chemistry, and full reproduction of the external published model.\nIf time becomes scarce, prioritise the pilot, label sensitivity, the environment record, and the licensing/sharing decisions. Defer system holdout and model development explicitly.","title":"Rules that apply throughout"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-roadmap/#Supporting-reference","page":"Recovery project: ordered task checklist","text":"The distinction between a nominal chemistry holdout and extrapolation in representation space is discussed in Probing out-of-distribution generalization in machine learning for materials. It motivates the similarity diagnostic; it does not predict this project's result.\n\n","title":"Supporting reference"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity-protocol/#MP-positive-label-sensitivity-protocol","page":"MP positive-label sensitivity protocol","text":"Protocol ID: eka-mp-label-sensitivity-v1\n\nFrozen: 31 August 2026, after the v1 pilot report and before any alternative label-policy ranking or metric inspection. This is a prospective follow-on specification, not a pre-pilot preregistration. The original v1 protocol and its preserved outputs do not change.","title":"MP positive-label sensitivity protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity-protocol/#Question-and-fixed-choices","page":"MP positive-label sensitivity protocol","text":"How sensitive is similarity-minus-popularity recovery to the treatment of composition groups with both explicit theoretical=false and theoretical=true records? This examines an experimental-provenance proxy, not whether particular compositions can be synthesized.\n\nUse the identical source snapshot, audit, chemistry, canonicalization, and exclusions pinned by v1. No re-query, energy filter, external scores, learned model, system holdout or literature-based relabelling is permitted here. Original v1 results are a required input, identified by the captured config and deterministic-file hashes. Validate them against the original snapshot and recompute them for compatibility before using their saved rankings.\n\nPreserve methods random, popularity, similarity; budgets 20, 50, 100, 200; split seeds 0–19; ranking seeds 10000–10019; tie seed 20260901; exact Float64 scores and v1 hash/tie rules. The original positive count is P, unlabelled count U, and mixed-flag positive count M. A mixed group has strictly positive experimental-record and theoretical-record counts. Unknown flags alone do not make a group mixed. No mixed group may contradict its original positive label.\n\nBefore ranking, independently count mixed groups and their distribution across chemical systems, element membership, H/halogen membership and record counts. Store these counts locally. They are input diagnostics, not outcome-based scope selection. Neither mixed nor nonmixed status supplies a predictive feature.","title":"Question and fixed choices"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity-protocol/#Policy-identifiers","page":"MP positive-label sensitivity protocol","text":"Policy Positive groups Unlabelled groups Excluded groups\noriginal Original P Original U Original unresolved groups\nexclude_mixed P minus M Original U Original unresolved plus all M\nunlabel_mixed P minus M Original U plus all M Original unresolved\n\nKeep these IDs separate from analysis mode. An output is identified by protocol, mode (evaluation_only or full_pipeline), policy, split seed and method.","title":"Policy identifiers"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity-protocol/#Evaluation-only-analysis","page":"MP positive-label sensitivity protocol","text":"Use the original v1 training memberships, fitted scores and complete saved rankings. Do not add any originally training composition to the evaluation pool, refit a method, or change any retained score or relative ordering. Original mixed training positives remain in the fitted state under all evaluation-only policies. Report their count explicitly.\n\nLet Hs be the original held-out set, Cs the original candidate set, and ms = |Hs intersect M|. All candidate mixed groups must be original holdouts.\n\nPolicy Eligible candidates Evaluation positives N_s h_s\noriginal C_s H_s U + floor(P/5) floor(P/5)\nexclude_mixed C_s minus M H_s minus M U + floor(P/5) − m_s floor(P/5) − m_s\nunlabel_mixed C_s unchanged H_s minus M U + floor(P/5) floor(P/5) − m_s\n\nFor exclusion, filter each original full ranking, preserve every retained score and relative order, and assign contiguous eligible ranks starting at one. A budget k means k eligible candidates, not k original positions. Preserve the original rank of every retained row and report original rank depth at each budget. This can require examining more than k original positions; do not present it as the same original selection. For unlabelling there is no filtering, so every rank/score stays fixed and original depth equals k.\n\nReport this as evaluation sensitivity with original training labels retained. It is not an end-to-end experiment under the new training-label policy.","title":"Evaluation-only analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity-protocol/#Full-pipeline-analysis","page":"MP positive-label sensitivity protocol","text":"Rebuild grouped labels for each policy before split construction. For each policy with Pp positives and Up unlabelled groups, use the existing v1 formula-hash order to hold out hp = floor(Pp/5), train on Pp − hp positives, and rank those holdouts plus all U_p unlabelled groups. Reuse the declared seeds and hash domains to align selection where valid; never reuse old membership unchanged merely because seeds match.\n\nBoth alternatives share exactly the same eligible positives and therefore the same training/held-out membership. Their candidate pools differ by M: all mixed groups are excluded under exclude_mixed, and all are unlabelled candidates under unlabel_mixed. No mixed group is a training positive under either. Original unresolved groups remain excluded. Recompute all methods from each branch's training/candidate inputs; no fitted full-positive cache is allowed.\n\nfull_pipeline/original must reproduce every original membership, score, rank and metric. For the two alternatives, shared-candidate scores and relative rank order must agree because training, hash seeds and scoring definitions agree; additional unlabelled candidates may change absolute ranks and metrics.","title":"Full-pipeline analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity-protocol/#Metrics,-denominators-and-reporting","page":"MP positive-label sensitivity protocol","text":"For every mode, policy, split, method and budget, report training count (including mixed training count), candidate count, mixed candidate count, held-out-positive count, observed-positive prevalence and excluded-group count. Preserve full membership and labelled rankings locally, with labels attached only after scoring. Report the policy-wide positive/unlabelled counts separately from the actual training membership: evaluation-only training still follows the original policy.\n\nUse the v1 metric definitions with each branch's actual eligible N and h: H@k; H@k/k; H@k/h; (H@k/k)/(h/N); exact uniform-random expected hits k*h/N. Require h >= 1, training >= 1, U >= 1 and every budget <= N. Fail the whole run before any alternative ranking if a declared branch is infeasible; do not silently omit a split or truncate a budget. Unlabelled never means negative.\n\nPrimary comparison: Ds = Hsimilarity@100 − Hpopularity@100 within each identical branch and split. Report every Ds, mean, median, range, and positive/zero/negative counts for all six branches. Report changes from original D_s, while making visible that populations differ. All secondary budgets and all random realizations and expectations must be retained. Report per-method mean hits, recall and enrichment alongside population sizes; raw hit changes alone are not evidence of a causal training-label effect.\n\nThere are 6 branches × 20 splits × 3 methods × 4 budgets = 1,440 metric rows. All are required, even when original controls duplicate the pilot. Descriptive variation is split sensitivity, not independent experiments. No significance tests, confidence intervals, new tuning, subgroup winner selection, or causal claim isolating relabelling from changed populations is permitted.","title":"Metrics, denominators and reporting"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity-protocol/#Interpretation-and-subsequent-decisions","page":"MP positive-label sensitivity protocol","text":"Allow effects to shrink, persist, reverse, or become zero. A small change in sign around zero is reported as directional sensitivity, not proof of a robust scientific reversal. Report continuous magnitudes and the split distribution; do not impose a post-result threshold for a desired conclusion.\n\nIf either alternative changes the sign of the mean primary full-pipeline D relative to original, carry both alternative policies into any later system holdout design (or explicitly restrict its claims and document that decision). If only evaluation-only results reverse, retain them as evaluation diagnostics and discuss why the end-to-end conclusion differs. Even without reversal, report all magnitude changes; none demonstrates synthesis success or label validity. A literature audit may be proposed only as a separately frozen sampling and assessment plan, never used retroactively to replace these labels.\n\nModel development remains deferred. System holdout, if later designed, tests unseen element combinations, not necessarily chemically distant examples: BaTiO3 and SrTiO3 are separate systems. Similarity's advantage may shrink, persist or reverse; no direction is presumed desirable or more publishable.","title":"Interpretation and subsequent decisions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-label-sensitivity-protocol/#Software,-freeze-and-reproduction","page":"MP positive-label sensitivity protocol","text":"Maintain an explicit protocol-ID-to-document-and-hash registry. Keep v1's ID and hash unchanged; unknown IDs or altered protocol bytes fail. This protocol has its own pin. Preserve the exact implementation, dirty patch and untracked source bytes, Project/Manifest and Julia version before real sensitivity evaluation. Capture snapshot, audit, original pilot config/rankings and protocol hashes. The protocol checksum lives in code/run records, not in its own hashed text.\n\nSynthetic verification must cover policy membership, mixed training exclusion, valid seed reuse, filtering/depth/denominator semantics, label isolation, same-input score invariance, original-v1 compatibility, deterministic reruns, tampered baselines and no-overwrite behavior. Verify all original pilot outputs before consuming them. Run every branch, then reproduce all deterministic scientific outputs from the captured environment in a separate directory.\n\nKeep original pilot outputs untouched and use exclusively new output directories. Separate timings from deterministic artifacts. Results and all unreviewed data derivatives remain local; no licence, permission or publication is implied.\n\n","title":"Software, freeze and reproduction"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-charter/#Eka-Materials-Selection:-One-Page-Charter","page":"Eka Materials Selection: One-Page Charter","text":"11 October 2026. Owner: Joshua Corbett. Stage: solo-built MVP before first user.\n\nPurpose. Give engineers a quick point of reference for choosing among existing materials. Turn a design brief into a small set of plausible options with clear pros and cons, supporting evidence, and sourcing links. Make the same capability available to other engineering agents.\n\nUsers. Engineers in smaller firms and consultancies are the initial audience. Engineers and materials specialists in larger firms may use the same tool to accelerate reference searches and comparisons. The first individual user and industry are still to be identified; neither is a condition of starting.\n\nDecision supported. Which existing material families or commercial grades deserve consideration, what tradeoffs distinguish them, and what needs checking next? The engineer retains design and qualification decisions. Distinguish family-level exploration, grade-specific evidence, and purchasable products.\n\nApplication and geography. General materials selection across engineering is the ambition. No ceramics-only vertical or UK/EU market is committed. The MVP uses a small declared set of examples and sources chosen for feasibility. Region, manufacturing, and supply requirements can be task inputs; coverage remains visible.\n\nFirst experience. Describe a problem, inspect and correct the requirements Eka extracts, and receive approximately three to five options where evidence permits. Each option shows advantages, disadvantages, property values and conditions, citations, sourcing links, and unresolved questions. Adjust the requirements and compare again.\n\nProduct form. A comparison view and MCP interface are useful initial options. The eventual balance between a standalone application and a tool used by other agents is open. Evidence and comparison functions should serve both.\n\nMVP boundary. Build enough of this workflow to approach one engineer with something usable. Use a modest catalogue and truthful coverage. A funded pilot, hired reviewer, several recruited firms, and formal benchmarking come later if useful. Basic source, unit, grade-identity, and constraint checks belong in the MVP; they do not constitute independent engineering validation.\n\nExclusions. Novel material generation, predictions presented as measurements, autonomous design approval, exhaustive coverage claims, automatic supplier contact/purchasing, and enterprise deployment. Choosing existing-material selection does not require claiming that generative research is useful only in certain industries.\n\nSuccess now. A runnable demo handles a few representative briefs, produces traceable comparisons, exposes missing evidence, and lets an engineer challenge the result. Next, one engineer tries a real task and identifies what helps and what needs correction. Time savings, broad accuracy, and willingness to pay remain hypotheses.\n\nExecution: local task board, MVP plan, task details, and decision log.\n\n","title":"Eka Materials Selection: One-Page Charter"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"Every exported name, grouped by the stability tier it belongs to. The tiers, and what each one promises, are defined in API stability and enforced by test/test_api.jl. Anything not listed here is internal, regardless of visibility.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Stable","page":"API reference","text":"Available for the life of 0.1.x. Signatures and observable behaviour will not change incompatibly; new keyword arguments with defaults may be added.","title":"Stable"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Compositions","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Compositions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Ranking","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Ranking"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Databases","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Databases"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Stable-command-line-behaviour,-experimental-Julia-signature","page":"API reference","text":"These back the frozen command-line interface. The CLI behaviour is stable for 0.1.x. The Julia signatures are not: they may gain, lose or reorder arguments in a 0.1.x release, because they exist to serve the CLI and the research protocols rather than as a general-purpose library surface.","title":"Stable command-line behaviour, experimental Julia signature"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Experimental","page":"API reference","text":"Part of the research surface. These may change in any 0.1.x release; a change is recorded in the changelog rather than in the version number.","title":"Experimental"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internal","page":"API reference","text":"Everything under EkaCompositions.Research — MPLabelSensitivity, MPSystemHoldout, ElementPairModel, PairFrequencyModel, EvaluationSuite and MPElementPair — is internal. Nothing there is exported, no research subcommand is wired into the CLI, and interfaces may change between any two releases with no deprecation path. They live in the package so they are precompiled, tested and loaded once, not because they are a public API. They are deliberately absent from this reference.","title":"Internal"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API reference","text":"\n\n","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.EkaCompositions","page":"API reference","text":"Explore precomputed chemical-composition scores with validated, canonical formulas.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.EkaCompositions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.Composition","page":"API reference","text":"Composition(formula::AbstractString)\nComposition(pairs)\n\nAn immutable composition of valid element symbols and positive integer amounts. Repeated symbols are combined, amounts are reduced by their greatest common divisor, and symbols are sorted alphabetically. The stored pairs and strings are immutable. Only simple formulas are accepted (no parentheses, charges, or fractional amounts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.Composition"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.formula","page":"API reference","text":"Return the alphabetically ordered, reduced formula, including explicit ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.formula"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.species","page":"API reference","text":"Return an immutable tuple of the distinct element symbols.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.species"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.similarity","page":"API reference","text":"Cosine similarity in [0, 1]; this measures stoichiometric overlap, not chemical plausibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.similarity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.AbstractRankingMethod","page":"API reference","text":"Extend ranking_value(method, composition, stored_score) to add a ranking strategy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.AbstractRankingMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.ScoreRanking","page":"API reference","text":"Rank by the supplied database score (no model fitting).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.ScoreRanking"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.SimilarityRanking","page":"API reference","text":"Rank by cosine similarity of element-count vectors to a reference composition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.SimilarityRanking"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.ranking_value","page":"API reference","text":"ranking_value(method::AbstractRankingMethod, composition::Composition, stored_score::Real)\n\nReturn the value used to order composition under method; larger values rank first. Implement this to add a ranking strategy. The result must be finite, or rank_compositions throws. stored_score is the score held in the database, passed through unchanged, so a method may ignore it. Ties are broken by descending stored score and then canonical formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.ranking_value"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.rank_compositions","page":"API reference","text":"Return ranked copies of (Composition, stored_score) rows. Never mutate the input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.rank_compositions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.rank_by_score","page":"API reference","text":"rank_by_score(results)\n\nRank (Composition, stored_score) rows by stored score, descending. Shorthand for rank_compositions(results, ScoreRanking()). The input is not mutated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.rank_by_score"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.rank_by_similarity","page":"API reference","text":"rank_by_similarity(results, reference)\n\nRank (Composition, stored_score) rows by cosine similarity of element-count vectors to reference, given as a Composition or a formula string. Shorthand for rank_compositions(results, SimilarityRanking(reference)). Similarity measures stoichiometric overlap, not chemical plausibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.rank_by_similarity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.query_compositions","page":"API reference","text":"query_compositions(path; elements=nothing, nary=nothing, threshold=0.01,\n                   inclusive=true, ranking=ScoreRanking())\n\nQuery a standard composition/score table or the legacy data2/data3/data4ionic/ data5ionic layout. Require all requested elements. nary=nothing is unrestricted; an empty nary list matches nothing. Legacy arities 4 and 5 cover ionic records only. Thresholds apply to the stored score, including with similarity ranking. Set inclusive=false for the original script's strict score > threshold semantics. Return (Composition, Float64) tuples, retaining source scores and duplicate rows. Only selected candidate rows are validated; use validate_database for a full audit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.query_compositions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.database_info","page":"API reference","text":"Inspect supported tables and coverage without loading composition records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.database_info"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.validate_database","page":"API reference","text":"validate_database(path; strict=true, max_issues=20)\n\nRead-only SQLite quickcheck plus a streaming audit of every record, including legacy redundant element/count fields. With strict=false, continue after invalid rows and return counts, at most `maxissues` row errors, and bounded per-category summaries/examples. Unsupported symbols are reported, never silently converted. No filtering, scoring, or repair is performed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.validate_database"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.import_compositions","page":"API reference","text":"import_compositions(records, destination; source, duplicates=:error)\n\nStream (formula_or_Composition, precomputed_score) records into a new standard SQLite database. Normalize and validate every row. Canonical duplicates error by default; :keep preserves them without merging scores. Preserve original formula, source-row number, and import metadata. Publish only after transaction commit and validation; never overwrite a destination. The containing filesystem must support hard links. This rebuilds a query database, NOT scores or a tensor model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.import_compositions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.import_tsv","page":"API reference","text":"import_tsv(input, destination; source, duplicates=:error)\n\nImport UTF-8 tab-separated composition\tscore records, with an exact header. No CSV quoting, blank rows, or extra columns. LF and CRLF line endings are accepted. The SHA-256 of the exact input bytes is stored as source_sha256; a separate hash identifies canonical scored records. Scores must already exist in the source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.import_tsv"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.main","page":"API reference","text":"main(args=ARGS; out=stdout, err=stderr) -> Int\n\nRun the CLI, returning 0 on success/help or 2 for invalid input/database errors. The library does not exit the calling process; only bin/eka calls exit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.main"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.benchmark_rankings","page":"API reference","text":"benchmark_rankings(records, training; budgets=[25, 50, 100], seeds=[0, 1, 2],\n                   methods=[\"score\", \"random\", \"popularity\"])\n\nEvaluate explicit (composition, stored_score, outcome) records at fixed budgets. Outcomes must be integer 0 or 1: absent/unlabelled compounds are NOT negatives. Training is a separate collection of known-positive compositions. Both collections must be unique after canonicalization and disjoint. All methods share one pool. No model is trained; supplied scores must be generated without evaluation leakage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.benchmark_rankings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.benchmark_tsv","page":"API reference","text":"benchmark_tsv(input, training, output; source, kwargs...)\n\nRead strict labelled-candidate and training TSV files and create a new report directory. Preserve exact input snapshots and SHA-256 checksums. Existing output paths are never overwritten. source describes the data, labels, split, and scores.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.benchmark_tsv"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.audit_mp_snapshot","page":"API reference","text":"audit_mp_snapshot(snapshot, output)\n\nAudit a snapshot produced by scripts/exportmppilot.py. Group material records by canonical composition before any future split. Labels are positive (any explicit theoretical=false), unlabelled (all true), or unresolved (otherwise). Missing provenance never becomes a negative. No ranking or discovery claim is made.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.audit_mp_snapshot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.split_mp_recovery","page":"API reference","text":"split_mp_recovery(snapshot, audit, output; synthetic=false,\n                  seeds=0:19, budgets=[20, 50, 100, 200])\n\nVerify snapshot and audit provenance, then save all deterministic membership splits in a NEW directory. Real mode is pinned to the Day 1 input hashes, protocol, 20 seeds and four budgets. Explicit synthetic mode requires a synthetic snapshot and keeps its identity distinct; it does not certify real data provenance.\n\nSaved ranker inputs contain formulas only. Evaluation labels, holdouts, input and implementation hashes, counts, and manifest hashes are recorded separately. No existing directory (including a dangling symlink) is overwritten. On failure, remove only the new output directory reserved by this invocation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.split_mp_recovery"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.benchmark_pu","page":"API reference","text":"benchmark_pu(bundle, snapshot, audit, output; synthetic=false)\n\nVerify all saved splits before running the random, popularity and maximum training-similarity methods on each split and every declared budget. Rankers receive only formula vectors and declared seeds. Evaluator-owned holdouts are consulted only after ranking. Save complete rankings, raw metrics, input/implementation provenance and runtime in a NEW output directory. External scores are not a method here; see docs/mp-external-score-provenance.md.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.benchmark_pu"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.mp_recovery_splits","page":"API reference","text":"mp_recovery_splits(groups; seeds=0:19, budgets=[20, 50, 100, 200])\n\nPure membership calculation for (composition, label) rows, with labels positive, unlabelled, or unresolved. Canonical duplicates are errors, even across labels. Hold out floor(P/5) positive compositions using the frozen v1 SHA-256 ordering; exclude training positives and unresolved groups from the pool.\n\nReturns splits with inputs=(training, candidates) containing compositions only, and a separate evaluation=(heldout, labels) whose labels align with candidates. This helper cannot establish source provenance. Use split_mp_recovery for saved snapshot/audit inputs. Non-default seeds/budgets support synthetic fixtures only in the file-based API. No RNG, ranking, evaluation, or filesystem access occurs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.mp_recovery_splits"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.load_mp_recovery","page":"API reference","text":"load_mp_recovery(bundle, snapshot, audit; synthetic=false)\n\nLoad a schema-v1 split bundle only after checking its manifests, captured file hashes and exact expected memberships reconstructed from the verified original snapshot/audit. The same checks apply to every split before any ranking begins. No manifest-supplied path is executed or used outside fixed allowlisted filenames. Returns separate composition-only inputs and evaluator-owned labels plus captured provenance bytes. Preserved producer code is verified, never executed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.load_mp_recovery"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.pu_rank","page":"API reference","text":"pu_rank(training, candidates; method, ranking_seed=10000, tie_seed=20260901)\n\nComposition-only random, training-element-popularity, or maximum-training- similarity ranking. There is no argument for labels, source IDs, stored scores, or bundle paths. All counts and similarity references are refitted per call from unique training compositions. Returned scores are Float64 for popularity and similarity; random uses its exact SHA-256 key and has no numeric score.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.pu_rank"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.pu_metrics","page":"API reference","text":"pu_metrics(ranking, heldout; budgets)\n\nMetrics for a complete ordering of unique composition formulas and its observed held-out positive subset. Unlabelled entries are not confirmed failures. Returns hits, observed-label fraction, held-out recall, enrichment, and uniform-random expected hits as both Float64 and an exact numerator/denominator pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.pu_metrics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.recovery_metrics","page":"API reference","text":"recovery_metrics(ranking, relevant; budgets, scores=nothing)\n\nEvaluate a complete ordering of unique canonical compositions against a nonempty held-out relevant subset. The ranked item is a composition; its chemical system is the unordered set of its elements. For each budget, return composition hits, observed-label fraction, recall, enrichment, exact uniform-random expectation, binary DCG/NDCG, and system-concentration diagnostics.\n\nscores, when supplied, must be finite and aligned in descending order. Exact score equality is used to report the cutoff tie size, how many members of that tie are selected, and whether the tie crosses the cutoff. No score rounding is performed. This function evaluates an existing order: it does not rank, filter, fit, mutate inputs, or interpret unlabelled compositions as confirmed negatives. Invalid budgets, duplicates, empty relevance, or relevance outside the ranking are errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.recovery_metrics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EkaCompositions.write_synthetic_mp_snapshot","page":"API reference","text":"write_synthetic_mp_snapshot(documents, target; database_version)\n\nWrite an offline, explicitly synthetic Materials Project-shaped snapshot for testing Eka's audit and recovery workflows. The target must not exist. This function performs no network access and must not be used to label real records as synthetic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EkaCompositions.write_synthetic_mp_snapshot"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-sources/#Materials-Selection:-Source-Inventory","page":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory","text":"Inspected 13 October 2026. Scope: a small source-feasibility investigation, not a downloaded catalogue or legal opinion. No paid access, supplier contact, account creation, bulk scraping, or manufacturer-data redistribution was performed.","title":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-sources/#Admission-decision","page":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory","text":"Four exact alloy/grade candidates were inspected through manufacturer documents: Hydro 6061-T6 and 6082-T6 extrusions, Ensinger TECANAT natural, and Ensinger TECAPEEK natural. Begin with these four reference candidates rather than assuming 15-30 grades are necessary. This is two broad material classes, not broad market coverage. A CoorsTek ceramic page is an excluded follow-up lead.\n\nNone is currently approved by this project for commercial catalogue ingestion, redistribution, or public numeric display. Public access is not permission for those uses. The immediate software demo uses authored synthetic records and fictional grade names; these must never be presented as manufacturer data. An actual engineering shortlist still requires admitted real evidence.\n\nThe inventory below records document identity, inspection findings, and next actions. It deliberately does not reproduce property tables or source files. Links are research citations, not representations of an approved sourcing offer.","title":"Admission decision"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-sources/#S-H6061:-Hydro-6061-extrusion","page":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory","text":"Candidate: Hydro 6061-T6, extruded stock; keep T6511 and special tempers separate.\nSource: Alloy 6061 PDF.\nDiscovery/supply route: North America alloy index.\nDocument revision: 2019/01 (2/2019), shown on page 2; checked 2026-10-13.\nInspection: both pages' extracted text; page 2 mechanical/physical table and footnotes. The table includes tensile minima by temper/thickness and separately labels typical conductivity. The source warns about strength after welding.\nApplicability: extrusion, not rolled plate; section thickness is tied to the tension specimen. Test temperature/method is not fully supplied in the selected row. Do not infer these from unrelated thermal-property headings.\nStorage/display/redistribution: not approved; R-H below. No local source copy.\nGaps: visual table verification before ingestion, current product specification, exact orderable stock form, quantity, location, stock, price, and rights basis.\nDemo: DEMO-01; exact commercial offer remains unverified.","title":"S-H6061: Hydro 6061 extrusion"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-sources/#S-H6082:-Hydro-6082-extrusion","page":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory","text":"Candidate: Hydro 6082-T6, extruded stock; do not merge with 6061 or T6511.\nSource: Alloy 6082 PDF.\nDiscovery/supply route: the North America alloy index above.\nDocument revision: 2019/02 (3/2019), page 2; checked 2026-10-13.\nInspection: both pages' extracted text; page 2 table contains thickness bands, tensile minima, typical conductivity, and a separate reference-standard block. Rounded unit pairs and thickness boundaries must not become independent data.\nApplicability: retain the selected temper, stock thickness and table block; do not combine reference EN limits with a different listed specification.\nStorage/display/redistribution: not approved; R-H. No local source copy.\nGaps: visual verification, specification confirmation, orderable product and region, stock, price, quantity, and rights basis. Older revision does not establish current availability. Demo: DEMO-01.","title":"S-H6082: Hydro 6082 extrusion"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-sources/#S-EPC:-Ensinger-TECANAT-natural","page":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory","text":"Candidate: TECANAT natural, unfilled PC stock shapes, not an arbitrary PC resin.\nSource: TECANAT natural datasheet.\nDiscovery/product route: US product page.\nDocument date/version: 2023/07/19, AF; checked 2026-10-13.\nInspection: full one-page extracted text, including reference specimen and disclaimer. Mechanical, thermal, and electrical properties have differing context. Values are guidelines, not guaranteed specification limits.\nAcquisition issue: product-page direct retrieval timed out; the manufacturer PDF was readable. Text extraction displaced superscripts, so electrical exponents and compound units require visual checks before numeric ingestion.\nStorage/display/redistribution: not approved; R-E. No local source copy.\nGaps: confirmed regional variant, long-duration performance under actual load, chemical/impact conditions, exact supply form, and rights basis. Demo: DEMO-02.","title":"S-EPC: Ensinger TECANAT natural"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-sources/#S-EPEEK:-Ensinger-TECAPEEK-natural","page":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory","text":"Candidate: TECAPEEK natural, unfilled PEEK stock shapes; not TECAPEEK IM or a raw-resin grade. Keep regional source identity explicit.\nSource: manufacturer-linked datasheet.\nDiscovery/product route: India product page, Downloads section.\nDocument date/version: 2023/07/19, AG; checked 2026-10-13.\nInspection: complete one-page extracted text and product technical details. Service temperature, heat distortion, and melting point are separate fields; electrical observations include their own temperature/humidity/specimen context. The disclaimer labels values as guidelines rather than guaranteed limits.\nAcquisition: public manufacturer page to its linked PDF; no login or payment.\nStorage/display/redistribution: not approved; R-E. No local source copy.\nGaps: visual exponent checks, regional equivalence, loaded lifetime/creep, actual stock/price/form, and permission basis. Demo: DEMO-02.","title":"S-EPEEK: Ensinger TECAPEEK natural"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-sources/#S-CALN:-CoorsTek-ceramic-lead,-excluded","page":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory","text":"Candidate labels found: AlN 180, AlN-HP, AlN-HTC; these are distinct, not aliases.\nLead: Aluminum Nitride page.\nRevision: not established. Search-indexed manufacturer content inspected on 2026-10-13; direct retrieval failed, so this is not a verified catalogue source.\nThe indexed table lists grade-specific density, thermal, and electrical rows. Its conductivity unit appears as W/m rather than a complete conductivity unit. Do not silently supply the missing temperature dimension or transfer a measurement at one temperature to a different requested temperature.\nStorage/display/redistribution: not approved; R-C. No local copy or numeric import.\nNext action: retrieve a revisioned primary datasheet and check its rendered headers, units, conditions, exact product, and permissions. DEMO-03 uses this only to demonstrate a visible coverage gap, not a numeric recommendation.","title":"S-CALN: CoorsTek ceramic lead, excluded"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-sources/#Rights-and-access-register","page":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory","text":"These are conservative project admission decisions based on inspected terms, not a legal determination about the copyright status of individual facts. Resolve the intended use before importing or publishing source-derived records. No request for permission or acceptance of a new agreement has been sent.\n\nID Terms inspected Observed limitation and project decision\nR-H Hydro legal notice, updated 2024-05-15 Authorization described as personal/non-commercial; commercial reuse and public distribution not established. No ingestion approval.\nR-E Ensinger US terms, India terms, international notice Reproduction restrictions vary by regional page. International notice also restricts hyperlinks without consent. Resolve regional applicability and intended linking/display use; do not assume the PDF endpoint grants a reuse licence.\nR-C CoorsTek conditions, conditions dated 2016-07-12 Inspected indexed terms authorize personal/non-commercial use, with copying and distribution restrictions. Direct retrieval failed. No ingestion approval.","title":"Rights and access register"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-sources/#Coverage-and-immediate-implementation","page":"Materials Selection: Source Inventory","text":"Demo Evidence route inspected Current implementable scope Remaining real-data gap\nDEMO-01 bracket S-H6061, S-H6082 Synthetic observations for minima, thickness, units, provenance Permissions and exact applicability/product verification\nDEMO-02 cover S-EPC, S-EPEEK Synthetic guidelines and missing requirements Permissions and application-relevant conditions\nDEMO-03 heat spreader Metal/polymer references; S-CALN excluded lead Authored no-supported-match fixture and explicit coverage gap Admitted ceramic evidence and full electrical/thermal context\n\nMVP-02 establishes the inventory and revises the immediate demo scope; it does not complete real catalogue ingestion. MVP-04 must resolve at least one usable real-data route (a permissioned source or appropriately licensed user-supplied evidence), or record that the build remains synthetic-only. An inaccessible or restricted source is excluded rather than used as a reason to halt schema work.\n\nBefore admitting a source, record exact version/date and content hash of any permitted retained copy, grade/variant identity, source location, original units, measurement conditions, evidence basis, and separate storage/display/distribution decisions with their basis. Missing permission is unknown, not allowed. A source reference is not a stock guarantee or an orderable offer. Keep real record-level data out of the public repository and public tests synthetic.\n\n","title":"Coverage and immediate implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/performance/#Local-performance-check","page":"Local performance check","text":"Measured on 2026-08-30, using Julia 1.12.6 on the local macOS machine with SQLite.jl 1.8.2, DBInterface.jl 2.7.0, and ArgParse.jl 1.2.0. The input is the included 12-row test fixture (eight upstream example pairs and four project-authored rows; see notices), with elements Al Si O, arity 4, and threshold 0.01 (five results).\n\nThese are exploratory measurements, not a statistically controlled benchmark. The first table records the initial fixture-based implementation. Production measurements after the schema-adapter work appear below.\n\nMeasurement Before representative precompilation After representative precompilation\nFirst query timed inside a new process 142.387 ms 6.169 ms\nWarm query minimum, 20 samples 0.245 ms 0.238 ms\nWarm query middle sample, 20 samples 0.265 ms 0.274 ms\nFresh CLI process, wall time 1.67 s 0.23 s, 0.23 s\nFirst timed query allocations 13,394,512 bytes 86,336 bytes\nWarm query allocations 51,616 bytes 51,616 bytes\n\nThe warm differences are too small to interpret as improvements or regressions. The material improvement here is startup/first-use latency, not the query algorithm. The package's own precompilation step took approximately two seconds after the workload was added; dependency installation and initial dependency precompilation are additional costs.","title":"Local performance check"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/performance/#Method","page":"Local performance check","text":"julia --startup-file=no --project=. scripts/benchmark_query.jl\n/usr/bin/time -p julia --startup-file=no --project=. bin/eka -e Al Si O -n 4 -d test/fixtures/tiny_test.db\n\nThe internal @timed measurements include connection opening, header/schema checks, parameterized SQL execution, formula parsing, element/arity filtering, sorting, and connection cleanup. They exclude Julia process startup, loading EkaCompositions, and compilation that occurs before the timed expression. The separate shell measurement includes the whole CLI process but was taken after package precompilation completed.\n\nWarm measurements reuse a Julia process, not a database connection; every call opens and closes its own connection. They may benefit from the OS filesystem cache. The first process measurement is not a cold-disk measurement.\n\nsrc/precompile.jl runs a small representative workload during package precompilation using a private temporary database. It does not read user datasets or write into the repository. Cached code reduces first-use latency across Julia processes; it does not eliminate startup or OS-specific variation.\n\nThe parser returns concrete Composition values, scored rows become Tuple{Composition,Float64}, and filtering operates after the dynamically typed SQLite boundary. Tests include inference checks for construction, canonical-string access, row parsing, and filter matching. This is not a claim that every instruction in the SQLite/CLI path is free from dynamic dispatch.","title":"Method"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/performance/#Production-measurement-after-schema-adaptation","page":"Local performance check","text":"Using the upstream database identified in production-validation.md, on the same local Julia 1.12.6 setup, with elements Al/Si/O, nary 4, and inclusive threshold 0.01:\n\n1,108 returned records.\nFirst internally timed query: 123.712 ms, 12,733,760 allocated bytes.\nMinimum of 20 warm queries: 103.637 ms.\nMiddle warm sample: 106.367 ms, 11,096,672 allocated bytes.\nFull streaming audit of 4,736,551 rows: approximately 39 seconds in one run; unsupported records are reported separately, not repaired.\n\nThese observations came from an exploratory session that also ran validation work, not a controlled isolated benchmark. No statistical speedup claim is made from these samples. Unlike the standard fixture, the legacy adapter can push exact element filters into SQL and avoid parsing unrelated records. The source database has no indexes; scans remain necessary and the source was not modified to add any.","title":"Production measurement after schema adaptation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/performance/#Next-measurement","page":"Local performance check","text":"Compare representative high and low thresholds and different arities in an isolated benchmark session, recording checksum, machine details, Julia/package versions, and repeated full-process timings. Standard-table queries still apply element/arity filters in Julia, while legacy queries use explicit element columns. Profile before adding a separately built indexed store or caching. Isotope-aware queries also require an explicit representation decision, not a performance workaround that silently discards unsupported records.\n\n","title":"Next measurement"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/design/#Scope-and-engineering-decisions","page":"Scope and engineering decisions","text":"This project explores the software engineering around composition recommendation: reliable querying, explicit ranking methods, and reproducible data handling. It does not reproduce the original research model, validate its scientific conclusions, or claim superiority over newer recommendation methods. The research and database are credited in the README.","title":"Scope and engineering decisions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/design/#Keep-three-operations-distinct","page":"Scope and engineering decisions","text":"Querying: retrieve existing composition scores with explicit filters and deterministic output.\nReordering: compare compositions to a chosen reference using a clearly defined, untrained cosine metric. Stored model scores remain intact and separately labelled.\nIngestion: rebuild the software's query store from records that already carry scores. Normalization and validation cannot manufacture scientific predictions from an unscored materials dataset.\n\nThe original SQLite scope excludes Tucker/CP/NMF/SVD training. A separately loaded element-pair research comparator now permits the specified weighted symmetric nonnegative factor model and its frozen evaluation; this does not add model training to the SQLite CLI. The engineering focus is schema adaptation, multiple dispatch, scientific data validation, safe imports, and measured performance.","title":"Keep three operations distinct"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/design/#Real-data-lessons","page":"Scope and engineering decisions","text":"The source is partitioned by arity, not a single composition table. A named schema adapter makes that difference explicit and keeps SQL filtering close to the data.\nFour/five-species source tables cover ionic compositions only. The CLI reports that limitation rather than implying complete chemical-space coverage.\nScores can exceed 1. No probability interpretation is assigned to them.\nThe original script uses a strict threshold. The MVP's inclusive default is retained, with an explicit strict option and boundary tests.\nThe source contains isotope labels such as D/T and other nonstandard tokens such as Bx/Cz. Those are outside the element-only type's contract. Full audits report unsupported records; queries and imports remain strict. Source conventions must be established before adding an adapter. Converting labels to standard elements, silently skipping them, or adding isotope semantics would be a separate design decision.","title":"Real-data lessons"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/design/#Import-integrity","page":"Scope and engineering decisions","text":"The default duplicate policy is an error because proportional formulas may collapse to one composition with different stored scores. An explicit keep policy preserves separate records. The importer retains original formulas and row order instead of losing the trail back to source data.\n\nPublication occurs only after transaction commit and a full audit. A same-filesystem hard link is used as a no-clobber publish operation: it cannot replace an existing destination, including one created concurrently. The temporary link is cleaned up afterwards. Filesystems without hard-link support fail safely; no overwrite fallback is attempted. This provides atomic visibility, not a claim of power-loss durability across all operating systems/filesystems.\n\nOnly SHA from Julia's standard library was added for checksums. The existing PrecompileTools dependency remains justified by the recorded startup-latency improvement; Printf formats the stable CLI output. Cosine similarity is implemented directly and needs neither an ML framework nor a linear-algebra dependency.","title":"Import integrity"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/design/#Extension-point","page":"Scope and engineering decisions","text":"New AbstractRankingMethod subtypes implement ranking_value(method, composition, stored_score). Ranking evaluates each candidate once and handles deterministic tie-breaking centrally. Database adapters and source-score filters do not need to know which strategy is selected. A future learned method must document its own training data and evaluation rather than inheriting meaning from the existing scores.\n\n","title":"Extension point"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-learned-feasibility/#Learned-comparator:-specification-and-feasibility-gate","page":"Learned comparator: specification and feasibility gate","text":"Prepared 31 August 2026 after system holdout v2. This is a development and feasibility specification, not a frozen scientific evaluation protocol. No real outer-test rankings or labels may be used to select this model's configuration. The completed pilot, sensitivity and system-holdout results remain unchanged.","title":"Learned comparator: specification and feasibility gate"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-learned-feasibility/#Question-and-representation","page":"Learned comparator: specification and feasibility gate","text":"Can a low-rank model of training element co-occurrence add useful association information beyond element popularity and nearest-composition cosine when ranking unseen oxygen-containing ternary systems?\n\nPrototype ID: eka-element-pair-symnmf-v1. Use a symmetric nonnegative factor model of the two non-oxygen elements. Oxygen is constant in the experiment's scope; removing it from these axes prevents a universal O node from defining the association matrix. Axes are the 117 non-O elements in Eka's fixed periodic-table vocabulary, sorted alphabetically, independent of candidates and test labels.\n\nFor each unique canonical training-positive composition, increment the count of its unordered non-O pair once. Multiple stoichiometries of a pair contribute multiple observations; repeated canonical formulas are rejected. Let T be the number of training compositions and C_ab this count. For distinct elements:\n\nY_ab = log(1 + C_ab) / log(1 + T).\n\nFit nonnegative F with 117 rows and rank r. Prediction for pair (a,b) is the dot product of their factor rows. The objective, over unordered pairs a<b, is:\n\n0.5 * sum_ab w_ab * (dot(F_a,F_b) - Y_ab)^2 + 0.5 * lambda * sum_ak F_ak^2.\n\nObserved pairs have weight 1; missing pairs have weight alpha. Diagonals are excluded. Initial fixed feasibility settings: rank 4, alpha=0.01, lambda=0.01, initialization seed 20260902, at most 2,000 projected-gradient iterations, relative projected-gradient tolerance 1e-4. These are engineering defaults, not settings chosen from real recovery performance.\n\nNonnegative projected gradient descent uses Armijo backtracking (coefficient 1e-4, halving step, at most 60 trials). Initial step is 1; subsequent trial steps are min(1, twice the last accepted step). The stationarity residual is the Frobenius norm of F - max(0, F - gradient), with stopping threshold tolerance times max(1, its initial norm). Log objective and residual. Hitting the iteration limit is reported explicitly; finite results are not proof of convergence or a global optimum. Initialization is deterministic from seed/element/factor SHA-256; unseen training-element rows start at zero and remain zero.","title":"Question and representation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-learned-feasibility/#Missing-entries-and-scoring-limits","page":"Learned comparator: specification and feasibility gate","text":"Alpha supplies a weak zero-target penalty for unobserved pair associations. This is an implicit-feedback modeling assumption, not a verified-negative compound label or an unbiased PU-risk estimator. It can suppress real unobserved associations and must be disclosed. Lambda controls factor magnitude. The model is a custom weighted SymNMF comparator; it does not reproduce either the Seko model or the optimizers/results of the references below.\n\nEvery in-scope candidate is scored using its pair dot product. A pair of known training elements is scoreable even when the pair itself was never observed. If either element was absent from training, return zero with an explicit unseen_element_zero coverage flag. Do not drop the candidate. Zero is a conservative fallback, not a chemical-impossibility claim. Scores are association scores, not probabilities; do not clamp them or interpret them as synthesis odds.\n\nAll stoichiometries in a system receive identical learned scores. Use the existing v1 tie hash and formula fallback, and report tied-score/system counts in any real evaluation. This prototype tests association transfer, not within-system stoichiometry discrimination. Rank and factor fitting may encode popularity; a future evaluation must include the original popularity comparator.","title":"Missing entries and scoring limits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-learned-feasibility/#Isolation-and-future-model-selection-rules","page":"Learned comparator: specification and feasibility gate","text":"Fit accepts training formulas and explicit configuration only. It cannot receive candidate membership, source IDs, outer evaluation labels or a full-data cache. Vocabulary is predefined; pair counts, scaling, initialization's active rows, factors and convergence all come from the training set. Scoring never refits.\n\nFor this feasibility gate, perform no hyperparameter search and inspect no new real-data rankings. If proceeding to scientific evaluation, first freeze a separate protocol carrying the existing two holdout designs and all three label policies. Use the fixed settings above initially unless a prospectively documented training-only development check identifies a numerical problem.\n\nAny later tuning must occur inside each outer training-positive population: construct deterministic inner holdouts of positives, with system grouping for system-transfer selection; exclude every outer candidate and label from fitting, normalization, inner candidate construction and selection. Explicitly define the inner unlabelled/reference pool and objective before tuning; these are not invented here. No tuning is authorized until that inner protocol exists. Refit from scratch on the complete outer training set after inner selection. Equal outer candidate populations, unchanged reference methods, exact split verification and all label policies remain required.","title":"Isolation and future model-selection rules"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-learned-feasibility/#Feasibility-tests-and-decision-criteria","page":"Learned comparator: specification and feasibility gate","text":"Use artificial ternary formulas only. Test:\n\nobjective and gradient against hand-calculated and finite-difference cases;\nnonnegative finite factors, nonincreasing accepted objective and explicit stops;\ndeterministic fitting after input permutation and exact same-environment reruns;\ncomplete finite candidate coverage, unseen known-element pairs, cold elements, canonical duplicate rejection and within-system ties;\ninvariance of fitted state to candidate order, added candidates and changed evaluator labels; scoring must not mutate factors;\nfull fit → ranking → PU metrics on a small synthetic holdout, with no scientific performance claim or requirement to beat a baseline on invented labels;\nan artificial size check with 4,288 training and 9,293 candidate compositions, reporting cold fitting time, a warm fit, ranking time, allocation and termination.\n\nA go decision means the implementation is numerically checked, covers all candidates, reproduces and has a practical measured cost. It authorizes preparing a bounded scientific evaluation, not a claim of useful recovery. If numerical checks fail, fix and repeat them before inspecting real rankings. If runtime exceeds 30 seconds per warm synthetic fit on this machine or memory exceeds 512 MiB in that fit, defer full evaluation pending engineering review. Synthetic size checks do not predict real convergence or end-to-end evaluation runtime.\n\nRecord measured costs and a reasoned go/no-go decision below in a dated follow-up. Do not adopt the original roadmap's 30-hour estimate without supporting evidence.","title":"Feasibility tests and decision criteria"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-learned-feasibility/#References-and-implementation-provenance","page":"Learned comparator: specification and feasibility gate","text":"Kuang, Ding and Park, Symmetric Nonnegative Matrix Factorization for Graph Clustering (2012) provides the symmetric nonnegative factorization family. Our weighted pair-count objective, chemistry representation, initialization and projected-gradient implementation are specified above, rather than imported from that paper's code.\n\nHu, Koren and Volinsky, Collaborative Filtering for Implicit Feedback Datasets (2008) motivates making missing-observation confidence explicit. The analogy does not establish that materials missing from a database are negative examples. No upstream implementation or dataset is copied.\n\n","title":"References and implementation provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-terms-evidence/#Materials-Project-terms:-evidence-and-handling","page":"Materials Project terms: evidence and handling","text":"Reviewed 31 August 2026. The project owner supplied a copy of the Materials Project Terms of Use in response to the outstanding terms request. Intended source: MP Terms of Use. The exact supplied text is preserved locally; SHA-256: e614dcdf306ef7e324136933fc99361a8b2c702c93736340c5ddf4df8dad6c3e.\n\nOn 31 August 2026 the official page was also retrieved interactively at the intended URL, which redirected to https://next-gen.materialsproject.org/about/terms. The visible page identified website revision 9f1b7077 and database version v2026.04.13. It confirmed the CC BY 4.0 Content grant, the credit/licence/change-notice requirements, the separate CC BY-NC 4.0 terms for GNoME, and the request to contact MP for guidance before repackaging and publicly redistributing MP data. No form was submitted, message sent or new API query made during this read-only check.","title":"Materials Project terms: evidence and handling"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-terms-evidence/#What-the-text-establishes","page":"Materials Project terms: evidence and handling","text":"The supplied terms expressly permit collecting database content through the API for analysis and presenting processed results with proper attribution. They state that downloaded Content is covered by Creative Commons Attribution 4.0, including copying, distribution, transmission and adaptation without specific permission from MP when proper attribution is given.\n\nCC BY 4.0 requires appropriate credit, the licence link, retention of supplied notices and an indication of modifications; do not imply endorsement or impose incompatible restrictions. It permits commercial use. This data licence is separate from Eka's MIT licence. The terms also prohibit direct website scraping and request advance contact for large downloads. This project uses the API; this review makes no new download and sends no message to MP.","title":"What the text establishes"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-terms-evidence/#Application-to-this-project","page":"Materials Project terms: evidence and handling","text":"Use the supplied terms as the working basis for preparing attributed research results and covered MP data. A separate provider permission request is not a blanket prerequisite for uses already permitted by this grant. Do not label the grant absent simply because our web reader could not retrieve the page.\n\nThe snapshot's retained MP fields are material_id, composition, formula_pretty, theoretical, database_IDs, and deprecated. Derived artifacts include canonical composition groups, provenance labels, splits, rankings, aggregate metrics and plots. The general Content grant supports their preparation with appropriate attribution insofar as they are covered content; this is not independent field-by-field provider confirmation.\n\nPreserve MP citation, database version, retrieval date, source route and changes (normalization, grouping, labels, splitting and scoring). Keep GNoME excluded. MP identifiers do not grant rights to separately obtain or redistribute the underlying ICSD/Pauling/other source records. No such original records or crystal structures were requested by the exporter. Retain any specific exception or source notice encountered during final artifact review.","title":"Application to this project"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-terms-evidence/#Evidence-limits-and-release-status","page":"Materials Project terms: evidence and handling","text":"The owner-supplied copy does not include its browser URL or a licence-version date, but the independent read-only page check above confirms that its material licensing provisions match the official page visible on the review date. The page does not display an effective date for the Content grant. Its 10 October 2011 effective date belongs to the privacy-policy section and must not be represented as the date of the CC BY 4.0 grant. This remains a project evidence review, not an institution's legal opinion.\n\nThe source-only Git archive has a separate release-readiness review. Review any proposed data bundle for exceptions, credentials, provenance and attribution before publication; do not automatically add datasets to Git. Seek clarification only for a concrete unresolved exception or repackaging question. No data release or external message is made by this review.\n\n","title":"Evidence limits and release status"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-external-score-provenance/#External-score-provenance-and-eligibility-decision","page":"External score provenance and eligibility decision","text":"Reviewed 31 August 2026 for the recovery protocol's Day 4 external-score gate. Joshua Corbett is the author of this review.\n\nDecision: the external Seko recommender scores are excluded from the primary comparison and are not evaluated in this pilot at all. The primary experiment remains random, training-element popularity, and maximum training-composition similarity. This is an eligibility decision about evidence, not a judgement of the upstream model's scientific quality.\n\nThe investigation was time-boxed as planned and closed inside the box. No ranking, hit count, recovery metric, or performance number was computed for the external scores, and none was inspected before this decision.","title":"External score provenance and eligibility decision"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-external-score-provenance/#What-was-inspected","page":"External score provenance and eligibility decision","text":"Item Finding\nModel Matrix/tensor-factorization recommender of Seko and collaborators, sekocha/recommender; method described in Seko et al., Phys. Rev. Materials 2, 013805 (2018)\nReleased artifact recommender-2024-07-01.sqlite, 211,681,280 bytes, SHA-256 9ee6f6a81f80cab74d967b70d4b0ad5d61c55ecf70e6b689c8a2c59e489a58fb\nArtifact identity Unchanged from the production validation record of 2026-08-30; opened read-only and not modified\nStated training sources Database entries from ICSD, ICDD, and Springer Materials, per the upstream repository\nStated purpose Scoring currently unknown chemically relevant compositions against those known entries\nDocumented training membership None published: no per-composition training list, inclusion rule, or exclusion rule\nDocumented cutoff None distinct from the 2024-07-01 release date; no source-database version or snapshot date is given\nNormalization Raw score column, 0.01–1.16248 over the in-scope ternaries; uncalibrated, not a probability, with an apparent 0.01 reporting floor rather than a natural minimum\nDuplicate scores 0 duplicate canonical compositions among the in-scope ternary rows; no aggregation rule is needed","title":"What was inspected"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-external-score-provenance/#Coverage,-inspected-before-any-performance-number","page":"External score provenance and eligibility decision","text":"data3 holds 1,474,012 ternary rows, of which 247,764 are distinct-element oxygen-containing ternaries. Against the audited pool of 12,506 canonical composition groups:\n\nAudited label Groups With an external score Coverage\nPositive 5,359 331 6.18%\nUnlabelled 7,147 2,258 31.59%\nAll 12,506 2,589 20.70%\n\nOnly 1.04% of the in-scope scored compositions appear in the audited MP pool at all, so the score set and the recovery pool overlap weakly in both directions.\n\nReproduce the table with the read-only inspection script; both inputs stay local:\n\njulia --project=. scripts/inspect_external_scores.jl --scores /path/to/recommender-2024-07-01.sqlite --audit reports/local/mp-ternary-audit/compositions.tsv","title":"Coverage, inspected before any performance number"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-external-score-provenance/#Why-this-fails-the-eligibility-gate","page":"External score provenance and eligibility decision","text":"The protocol requires training-data independence to be established, not merely unrefuted, and a newer MP snapshot does not establish it. Three findings block eligibility, and the third is decisive on its own.\n\nThe stated training sources overlap the label definition. A positive here is an MP group with at least one theoretical=false record, an experimental- provenance proxy drawn largely from the same experimental databases the model reports training on. Held-out positives are therefore likely to be training data for the model, which is exactly the leakage the split design exists to prevent.\nMembership and cutoff are undocumented. Without a training list or a verifiable cutoff there is no way to partition the pool into scored compositions the model could and could not have seen, so no defensible restricted pool can be declared.\nCoverage is associated with the outcome label. Unlabelled compositions are scored about five times as often as positives, 31.59% against 6.18%. This is the pattern the model's stated purpose implies: a released list of currently unknown compositions is close to the complement of its known-compound training set. Selection into the scored pool is therefore censoring tied to the label being predicted, and the 331 scored positives are a small, atypical residue rather than a random sample of positives.\n\nConsequence: a restricted-pool evaluation on the 2,589 covered compositions would have a positive share near 2.8%, against 13.03% in the frozen candidate pool, and its ranking result would substantially reflect the censoring rule rather than recovery skill. Reporting it even as an exploratory figure would invite exactly the misreading the protocol forbids, so it is omitted rather than labelled. The protocol permits either omission or an exploratory label; omission is the honest option when the confounder is this directly tied to the outcome.\n\nThis inspection establishes the differential coverage as fact. It does not by itself prove the exclusion rule that produced it, and no claim is made about the upstream model's accuracy on its own task. The gate turns on absent evidence of independence, which is settled either way.","title":"Why this fails the eligibility gate"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-external-score-provenance/#What-would-reopen-the-question","page":"External score provenance and eligibility decision","text":"All of the following, in a separately versioned protocol written before any external-score performance is read:\n\nA published or supplied training-membership list, or a verifiable cutoff with the source-database versions and dates behind it.\nA restricted pool declared in advance, with its positive/unlabelled coverage, and the random, popularity, and similarity baselines recomputed on that same pool so no method is compared across different pools.\nAn account of why coverage differs by label, sufficient to show the remaining scored positives are not a biased residue.\nTerms permitting the intended use and any derived publication; the data and provenance review restrictions on the MP snapshot apply unchanged to any joined output.\n\nRetraining or reimplementing the recommender is out of scope for this pilot and is not required to close this decision.","title":"What would reopen the question"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-external-score-provenance/#Handling","page":"External score provenance and eligibility decision","text":"The score database remains outside Git, ignored, and read-only; this pilot did not copy, modify, or redistribute it. Only the aggregate counts above are recorded in tracked documentation. Per-composition coverage output stays in the ignored local review folder. The recommender research and database are separate work by Atsuto Seko and collaborators; see the README's citation.\n\n","title":"Handling"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair-protocol/#MP-element-pair-factor-comparator-protocol","page":"MP element-pair factor comparator protocol","text":"Protocol ID: eka-mp-element-pair-v1\n\nFrozen: 31 August 2026, after synthetic feasibility and before any real-data factor-model fitting, ranking or metric inspection. This prospective extension leaves the earlier pilot, label-sensitivity and system-holdout protocols intact.","title":"MP element-pair factor comparator protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair-protocol/#Question-and-fixed-model","page":"MP element-pair factor comparator protocol","text":"Does a low-rank model of non-oxygen element co-occurrence improve held-out experimental-provenance recovery over training-element popularity, and how does that comparison differ across composition and system holdout and label policies? This is an additional model-family comparator, not a Seko reproduction.\n\nModel ID eka-element-pair-symnmf-v1 uses the exact representation, objective, initialization and projected-gradient algorithm in the separately captured feasibility specification. Fix rank=4, missingweight=0.01, regularization=0.01, initialization seed=20260902, maxiterations=2000, tolerance=1e-4 and ranking tie seed=20260901. No tuning, restart selection or post-result increase in iterations. The following restates the scientific contract.\n\nAxes are the 117 non-O elements sorted alphabetically. Each unique training composition increments its unordered pair of non-O elements once. With T training compositions and pair count Cab, target Yab=log1p(C_ab)/log1p(T). For a<b minimize:\n\n0.5 sum_ab w_ab (dot(F_a,F_b)-Y_ab)^2 + 0.5 lambda sum_ak F_ak^2, F>=0.\n\nObserved-pair weight is 1; unobserved-pair weight is 0.01. Diagonals are omitted. Unobserved entries are low-confidence zero-target associations, not verified negative compounds. This is a modeling assumption, not an unbiased PU objective.\n\nObserved-element initial factor entry is (0.5+u)/sqrt(10*rank), where u is the integer represented by the first 13 hexadecimal digits of SHA256 of model_id + \"\\n\" + seed + \"\\n\" + element + \"\\n\" + factor_index, divided by 2^52. Unseen rows start and remain zero. Use Float64 and the explicit deterministic loop order in the captured implementation; no BLAS or global RNG dependence. Projected gradient has Armijo coefficient 1e-4, initial step 1, halving up to 60 trials, and next trial step min(1,twice last accepted step). The residual is norm(F-max(0,F-gradient)); threshold is tolerance*max(1,initial residual).\n\nSynthetic feasibility found that the size case hit the iteration cap. Therefore this experiment explicitly evaluates a fixed-compute optimization state: use a valid finite state at the cap, label it iteration_limit, and retain the entire objective/residual trace. Report convergence/cap counts by branch. Do not claim a global optimum or converged model when the tolerance was not met. Nonfinite state or failed line search fails the whole experiment; never skip it.\n\nScore each candidate by its two factor rows' dot product. Unknown training elements receive zero and a coverage flag, without dropping the candidate. Scores are associations, not synthesis probabilities. All stoichiometries in a system share a score. Resolve ties with the unchanged v1 tie hash/formula rule. Report distinct score counts, tied-candidate count and cold-element counts.","title":"Question and fixed model"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair-protocol/#Inputs,-membership-and-isolation","page":"MP element-pair factor comparator protocol","text":"Use the same verified snapshot/audit as v2, with its frozen input hashes and oxygen-containing ternary scope. GNoME stays excluded. Use both composition and system designs, all three full-pipeline policies (original, exclude_mixed, unlabel_mixed), split seeds 0–19 and budgets 20,50,100,200. Reconstruct every membership under the frozen v2 rules; require exact equality with the saved v2 training, candidate, held-out and evaluation-label files.\n\nVerify the v2 output inventory against a pre-evaluation pinned config hash and recheck each source hash. Treat saved reference scores/metrics as fixed controls; prior v2 verification and full reproduction are prerequisites. Any changed v2 artifact, even with a rewritten internal checksum, must fail the config pin. Synthetic runs allow smaller budgets/seeds and do not use the real baseline pin.\n\nFit each branch/split independently from its training formulas and fixed settings. No candidate membership, labels, IDs or whole-data fitted state enters fitting. All preprocessing is training-only; the element vocabulary is predefined. No hyperparameter search or inner selection is performed. A future tuned model needs a new protocol specifying training-only inner pools and selection.\n\nBoth alternative policies have identical training membership within each design and seed. Refit separately anyway, and require exact factor/trace equality and shared-candidate score/order invariance. Candidate additions and evaluation-label changes cannot affect fitted state. Never change previous baseline algorithms, rankings, eligible candidates or budgets.","title":"Inputs, membership and isolation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair-protocol/#Evaluation-and-diagnostics","page":"MP element-pair factor comparator protocol","text":"Attach held-out labels only after scoring. Report the unchanged PU metrics for every learned ranking: H@k, H@k/k, H@k/h, enrichment and kh/N random expectation. The new model contributes 23204=480 metric rows and 120 full rankings/fits. Keep the 1,440 reference metric rows available in captured baseline evidence.\n\nPrimary effect is learned-model Hits@100 minus popularity Hits@100 within each identical branch/split. Report all 20 differences per branch, their mean, median, range and positive/zero/negative counts. Model-minus-similarity is secondary; also report random realization and expectation and all secondary budgets. No favorable branch, policy, initialization or iteration checkpoint may be selected post hoc. Report training/candidate/positive counts and prevalence beside effects.\n\nFor each fit preserve training hash, parameters, active elements, pair counts, factors, objective/residual trace and termination. For rankings report coverage flags and observed-training-pair flags. Report every branch's convergence/cap counts, iteration range, cold candidates, distinct scores and tied candidates. No confidence intervals or significance claims: outer splits overlap.\n\nCompare both designs under each label policy without a causal chemical-separation claim; populations differ. Low-rank associations ignore stoichiometry and may encode historical research frequency. Unlabelled does not mean failed synthesis. A favorable result only supports this fixed-compute representation on this benchmark. An unfavorable result is retained and does not trigger tuning.","title":"Evaluation and diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair-protocol/#Reproduction-and-reporting-decision","page":"MP element-pair factor comparator protocol","text":"Before real fitting, record this protocol hash, exact implementation/environment and the baseline config hash. Run meaningful synthetic objective/gradient, coverage, label-isolation and complete workflow checks. Save all outputs only in new directories. Independently recalculate scores from saved factors, reconstruct memberships and metric denominators, check ranking and common-candidate invariance, and compare a complete rerun from the captured implementation/environment. Separate runtime from deterministic outputs. Preserve all earlier sealed evidence.\n\nAfter results, package favorable, null and unfavorable comparisons alike. Do not extend model development merely to obtain a win. Any further model or convergence study requires its own question and prospective protocol. Retain detailed data locally while preparing an attributed summary under the separate terms review.\n\n","title":"Reproduction and reporting decision"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/release-notes-0.1.1/#Eka-0.1.1","page":"Eka 0.1.1","text":"Eka 0.1.1 completes the first reproducible ranking-benchmark phase. It adds a deterministic, independently validated path for the reported system-disjoint subgroup, system-concentration and 20,000-iteration stability diagnostics, with synthetic corruption tests and locally hashed evidence.\n\nThe release also includes the source-release checklist, deployed Documenter manual, expanded CI matrix, package-quality checks and explicit 0.1.x API stability contract added since 0.1.0.\n\nThis is a reproducible ranking benchmark, not a validated discovery model. It reconstructs withheld provenance labels from one contemporaneous Materials Project snapshot; it does not establish chronological discovery performance, synthesis success or generalization to unseen chemical systems. The frozen 2,000-iteration result remains primary and the 20,000-iteration run is post-hoc stability characterization.\n\nThis tag is source-only. Record-level MP data, rankings, factors and environments remain excluded; the optional evidence bundle was not approved or published.\n\n","title":"Eka 0.1.1"},{"category":"section","location":"THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES/#Third-party-notices","page":"Third-party notices","text":"Eka's original implementation and documentation are covered by the root MIT LICENSE. Third-party material retains its own terms.","title":"Third-party notices"},{"category":"section","location":"THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES/#Seko-recommender-examples","page":"Third-party notices","text":"Eight composition/score pairs in test/fixtures/build_fixture.jl and test/fixtures/tiny_test.db match examples in the sekocha/recommender README:\n\nComposition Score\nAl2Ba2O7Si1 0.74232\nAl2O12Si3Zn3 0.48140\nAl1Li1O12Si5 0.41613\nAl2Ba3O14Si4 0.39355\nAl2O14Si4Sr3 0.34611\nZn1Mg3 0.39168\nZn1Mg2 0.37147\nMg1Zn4 0.26386\n\nSome of these pairs also occur in README output, examples/scored_compositions.tsv, src/precompile.jl, and the CLI, database and legacy-schema tests. These pairs are upstream examples reused for software checks, not invented Eka predictions. The other four rows in the tiny fixture are project-authored test cases. docs/production-validation.md separately records observations from querying the upstream database; it is not evidence of predictions generated by Eka.\n\nThe upstream repository supplies the BSD 3-Clause notice reproduced below (source, reviewed 31 August 2026). Preserve it with the reused examples. This notice does not assert clearance to redistribute the full database or underlying ICSD, ICDD or Springer Materials records. The full database and upstream Python query script are not bundled with Eka.\n\nBSD 3-Clause License\n\nCopyright (c) 2024, Atsuto Seko\nAll rights reserved.\n\nRedistribution and use in source and binary forms, with or without\nmodification, are permitted provided that the following conditions are met:\n\n1. Redistributions of source code must retain the above copyright notice, this\n   list of conditions and the following disclaimer.\n\n2. Redistributions in binary form must reproduce the above copyright notice,\n   this list of conditions and the following disclaimer in the documentation\n   and/or other materials provided with the distribution.\n\n3. Neither the name of the copyright holder nor the names of its\n   contributors may be used to endorse or promote products derived from\n   this software without specific prior written permission.\n\nTHIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED BY THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS AND CONTRIBUTORS \"AS IS\"\nAND ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES, INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, THE\nIMPLIED WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY AND FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE ARE\nDISCLAIMED. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE COPYRIGHT HOLDER OR CONTRIBUTORS BE LIABLE\nFOR ANY DIRECT, INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL, SPECIAL, EXEMPLARY, OR CONSEQUENTIAL\nDAMAGES (INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, PROCUREMENT OF SUBSTITUTE GOODS OR\nSERVICES; LOSS OF USE, DATA, OR PROFITS; OR BUSINESS INTERRUPTION) HOWEVER\nCAUSED AND ON ANY THEORY OF LIABILITY, WHETHER IN CONTRACT, STRICT LIABILITY,\nOR TORT (INCLUDING NEGLIGENCE OR OTHERWISE) ARISING IN ANY WAY OUT OF THE USE\nOF THIS SOFTWARE, EVEN IF ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGE.","title":"Seko recommender examples"},{"category":"section","location":"THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES/#Materials-Project-and-other-datasets","page":"Third-party notices","text":"The recovery pilot uses a separately obtained Materials Project API snapshot. Eka's MIT licence does not grant rights over that snapshot, other third-party datasets or data-derived artifacts. See the publication-permissions register for their separate status. Scientific citations are not additional conditions of MIT.","title":"Materials Project and other datasets"},{"category":"section","location":"THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES/#Dependencies","page":"Third-party notices","text":"Dependencies retain their respective copyright and licence notices. Eka's MIT licence does not replace them or license a complete bundled runtime. Preserve applicable upstream notices when redistributing dependency code or binaries.\n\n","title":"Dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api-stability/#API-stability-and-compatibility","page":"API stability and compatibility","text":"What callers may rely on in the 0.1.x series, and what may change without a breaking release. Enforced by test/test_api.jl, which fails if an exported name is added without being classified here.\n\nJulia's public keyword requires 1.11 and this package supports 1.10, so this document plus that test are the contract. Every name below is exported; anything not exported is internal regardless of what this page says.","title":"API stability and compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api-stability/#Stable","page":"API stability and compatibility","text":"Available for the life of 0.1.x. Signatures and observable behaviour will not change incompatibly; new keyword arguments with defaults may be added.\n\nName Purpose\nComposition Canonical reduced element ratio\nformula Canonical formula string\nspecies Element symbols in a composition\nsimilarity Cosine overlap of element-count vectors\nquery_compositions Read-only query over a score database\ndatabase_info Schema inspection\nvalidate_database Record audit\nimport_compositions, import_tsv Ingest already-scored records\nrank_compositions Rank rows under a ranking method\nranking_value Extension point for a new ranking method\nAbstractRankingMethod, ScoreRanking, SimilarityRanking Ranking types\nrank_by_score, rank_by_similarity Shorthand rankers\n\nComposition equality and hashing ignore element order and overall scale. That is load-bearing for callers using compositions as dictionary keys, and will not change in 0.1.x.","title":"Stable"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api-stability/#Stable-command-line-behaviour,-experimental-Julia-signature","page":"API stability and compatibility","text":"These back the frozen command-line interface. The CLI behaviour is stable for 0.1.x — the same arguments produce the same results and the same output format. The Julia function signatures are not: they may gain, lose or reorder arguments in a 0.1.x release, because they exist to serve the CLI and the research protocols rather than as a general-purpose library surface.\n\nName Backs\nmain The eka entry point\nbenchmark_rankings, benchmark_tsv eka benchmark\naudit_mp_snapshot eka audit-mp\nsplit_mp_recovery eka split-mp\nbenchmark_pu eka benchmark-pu\n\nThe CLI offers six subcommands — import, validate, benchmark, audit-mp, split-mp, benchmark-pu — plus the default mode, where eka with no subcommand runs a stored-score query.\n\nmain is exported today, but the name is generic enough to collide with other packages. The intent is to unexport it at 0.2.0 while keeping the function. Do not rely on using EkaCompositions bringing main into scope.","title":"Stable command-line behaviour, experimental Julia signature"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api-stability/#Experimental","page":"API stability and compatibility","text":"Part of the research surface. These may change in any 0.1.x release; a change is recorded in the changelog rather than in the version number.\n\nmp_recovery_splits, load_mp_recovery, pu_rank, pu_metrics, recovery_metrics, write_synthetic_mp_snapshot.\n\npu_rank and pu_metrics are the documented interfaces for positive–unlabelled ranking, and are usable, but the positive–unlabelled work is research code whose shape follows the protocols. Treat them as experimental.\n\nrecovery_metrics is the protocol-independent experimental metric kernel for a complete composition ranking and an explicit held-out relevant subset. It adds system-concentration and cutoff-tie diagnostics without changing pu_metrics or any frozen report schema.","title":"Experimental"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api-stability/#Internal","page":"API stability and compatibility","text":"Everything under EkaCompositions.Research — MPLabelSensitivity, MPSystemHoldout, ElementPairModel, PairFrequencyModel, EvaluationSuite, MPElementPair — is internal. Nothing there is exported, no research subcommand is wired into the CLI, and interfaces may change between any two releases with no deprecation path. They live in the package so they are precompiled, tested and loaded once, not because they are a public API.","title":"Internal"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api-stability/#Database-schema-compatibility","page":"API stability and compatibility","text":"Two layouts are read, both read-only and validated strictly:\n\nA standard table with composition (text) and score (real) columns.\nThe legacy data2 / data3 / data4ionic / data5ionic layout, with ele1…eleN and int1…intN columns alongside composition and score.\n\nThe legacy reader is frozen for 0.1.x: no table names will be added or removed, and the strict validation behaviour will not be relaxed. Formulas that cannot be parsed — isotope notation such as D, for example — cause a strict query to fail rather than silently drop or alter rows. eka validate --report scans without failing on the first problem.\n\nQueries never create, modify or migrate a database.","title":"Database schema compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api-stability/#Generated-report-formats","page":"API stability and compatibility","text":"Generated metadata carries a schema_version field. Its current state is not uniform across producers, and this is documented as observed rather than as a guarantee:\n\nProducer Field value\nbenchmark_rankings (src/benchmark.jl) integer 1\nimport_compositions (src/import.jl) string \"1\"\nwrite_synthetic_mp_snapshot (src/mp_snapshot.jl) integer 2\nMP audit output (src/mp_audit.jl) integer 1; reads accept 1 or 2\n\nReaders should accept both an integer and a string for this field. The string/integer inconsistency in the import path is a known wart; correcting it would break existing readers, so it is deferred to 0.2.0.\n\nWithin a schema_version, keys may be added without a version change. Removing a key, or changing the meaning or type of an existing one, requires a schema_version bump.\n\nGenerated research bundles additionally record protocol_id and protocol_sha256. Those identify a frozen protocol document and are not a software compatibility signal: a changed protocol is a new experiment, not a new release. See the recovery protocol.","title":"Generated report formats"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api-stability/#Julia-compatibility","page":"API stability and compatibility","text":"The floor is Julia 1.10. Raising it is a breaking change for 0.1.x purposes and will not happen in this series.\n\n","title":"Julia compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#Materials-Selection:-Decision-Log","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Started 11 October 2026. Owner: Joshua Corbett. Basis: Joshua's clarification of P0.1-P0.6 in the project conversation. Accepted directions, implementation proposals, and hypotheses are distinguished. Update entries with new evidence without silently replacing their history.","title":"Materials Selection: Decision Log"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D01.-Build-an-MVP-before-a-pilot","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Accepted, 11 October 2026. Joshua is the sole builder, assisted by Codex. Build something usable to approach one engineer; do not require a funded pilot, recruitment programme, or hired specialist first. Replace the earlier dependency chain and withdraw its schedule. Revisit when a working demo or a concrete data-access cost creates a need for external participation or funding.","title":"D01. Build an MVP before a pilot"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D02.-Support-existing-material-selection","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Accepted, 11 October 2026. Serve engineers in smaller firms/consultancies and potentially larger firms with materials specialists. Provide a quick reference with multiple options and tradeoffs; the engineer makes the final design decision.\n\nHypotheses: many target tasks can use existing materials, and engineering agents will benefit from reliable selection tools. These are not measured market facts. Revisit after the first engineer tries a real task. No claim about generative materials research being restricted to particular industries is needed.","title":"D02. Support existing-material selection"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D03.-Keep-a-broad-ambition-and-declare-actual-coverage","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Accepted direction, 11 October 2026. Broad known-material search is the long-term ambition. A ceramics-only application and UK/EU market were earlier suggestions, not user decisions; both defaults are withdrawn.\n\nImplementation proposal: three illustrative briefs and roughly 15-30 documented grades, across more than one family where feasible. The counts are provisional. No results means no supported match in the searched sources, not no possible material. Revisit scope after source inspection and the first complete comparison.","title":"D03. Keep a broad ambition and declare actual coverage"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D04.-Evidence-and-tradeoffs-are-the-core-output","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Accepted, 11 October 2026. Show pros, cons, relevant conditions, citations, sourcing links, and unanswered questions. Distinguish documented grade properties, inference, and missing information. Preferences cannot cancel mandatory failures. Verify initial source values without making external sign-off a build dependency. Revisit comparison rules when source context or engineer feedback reveals a flaw.","title":"D04. Evidence and tradeoffs are the core output"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D05.-Leave-the-product-form-open","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Accepted direction; delivery details proposed, 11 October 2026. Keep reusable selection functions for both an interface and other agents. MCP remains intended. A compact local comparison view is a proposed demonstration surface. A separate typed service with a small database and official SDK is a stack proposal to confirm during implementation. Neither multiple agents nor a specific model provider is a requirement. Revisit with implementation evidence and first-user use.","title":"D05. Leave the product form open"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D06.-Preserve-research-and-branch-product-work","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Completed, 11 October 2026. Saved the 30 pending source, test, paper, and documentation files in checkpoint commit 2ea4459 on main; created codex/materials-selection-mvp from it. The original 8 October plan remains in that commit. No remote push or publication was performed.\n\nVerification completed: 4,874 Julia checks and 41 Python tests passed. The repository release audit and whitespace checks passed. This verifies the existing software checkpoint, not the scientific claims in the paper or a selection MVP.\n\nExisting protocols and score meanings remain as documented. Selection requires its own evidence; research scores are not measures of application suitability.","title":"D06. Preserve research and branch product work"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D07.-Maintain-a-small-task-board","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Accepted, 11 October 2026. Codex prepares the board and decision log. Use 14 MVP tasks with owner, dependencies, artifact, and completion criteria instead of transferring all 118 old tasks. Joshua initially approved Linear in the Dialext workspace/team. Access attempts failed; no Linear project or issues were created.\n\nSuperseding decision, 12 October 2026: Joshua explicitly requested an AGENTS.md file instead of Linear. AGENTS.md now holds the authoritative task-status and dependency board; the backlog holds detailed specifications and completion evidence. The charter and this log remain linked local documents. External publication is no longer planned, and browser access or security-setting changes are not prerequisites. P0.5 is complete; the 14 implementation/learning tasks remain open.\n\nEvidence: Joshua's instruction to keep the work in an agents file. Revisit only if Joshua requests an external tracker or local tracking causes a demonstrated coordination problem. Keep statuses in one place and require dated artifact and verification evidence before marking a task complete.","title":"D07. Maintain a small task board"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D08.-Spend-and-validate-in-proportion-to-the-stage","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Accepted direction, 11 October 2026. Defer the formal pilot budget and named reviewer. Default to local development and permitted accessible sources; measure actual model cost. No paid contract, hosting subscription, or reviewer engagement has been approved.\n\nStill part of the MVP: correct source context, conversions, grade identity, missing-data behavior, and evidence-backed explanations. Independent application validation, commercial claims, and commitments require later evidence. Revisit when the working product reaches an engineer or a specific expense is necessary.","title":"D08. Spend and validate in proportion to the stage"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D09.-Begin-with-three-cases-and-a-synthetic-software-fixture","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Implementation decision, 13 October 2026. The demonstration briefs cover a bolted bracket, an underspecified hot cover, and a no-supported-match heat-spreader screen. The inventory records four inspected manufacturer references across metals and polymers, plus an excluded ceramic lead. Replace the provisional 15-30-grade starting estimate with these four reference candidates; expansion needs an actual comparison need.\n\nEvidence: the inspected documents require temper, thickness, evidence-basis, and condition distinctions. Published reuse terms do not establish permission to populate a commercial catalogue. Build and test with fictional grades and values; do not silently relabel manufacturer values as synthetic or claim the resulting software selects real materials yet. MVP-02 completes the inventory, not licence clearance or data ingestion. Real-data admission remains work for MVP-04.\n\nRevisit when one source has an adequate use basis and verified observations, or when the first comparison exposes a missing material class. No commercial data contract, source outreach, or legal determination is authorized by this decision.","title":"D09. Begin with three cases and a synthetic software fixture"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D10.-Use-a-small-independent-Python-evidence-service","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Implemented and tested, 13 October 2026. Use Python 3.14.6 for this initial environment, Pydantic 2.14.1 for strict typed records/JSON Schema, Pint 0.26.1 for dimensional checks, and standard-library SQLite for immutable catalogue snapshots. All runtime packages are pinned in the service. No Julia bridge, web framework, LLM provider, or MCP SDK has been selected or added.\n\nEvidence: 29 focused tests pass, including serialization/storage round trips, identity/reference checks, original units and provenance, unknowns, conflicts, permission denials, corruption detection, and temperature/unit conversions. The 41 existing Python tests and repository release audit also pass. CLI validation and persistence work with the authored synthetic fixture. Julia was unchanged and its suite was not rerun.\n\nUse separate IDs for families, grades, variants, observations, sources, tasks, and offers. Keep original bounds and units, with normalized values derived only when requested. Missing conditions and permissions do not acquire defaults that claim evidence or authorization. Retention/display decisions are enforced by storage/CLI, but the stated permission basis remains an importer responsibility.\n\nStore canonical JSON snapshots before adding query indexes: this is sufficient to verify the evidence model without designing the eventual search backend. Revisit with the first admitted catalogue's size/query needs or an actual host integration. Strict/open numeric bounds and categorical requirements need explicit schema work before use; no silent approximations. Schema validation does not evaluate constraints or validate an engineer's design.","title":"D10. Use a small independent Python evidence service"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-decisions/#D11.-Compare-manually-screened-records-on-one-derived-index","page":"Materials Selection: Decision Log","text":"Implemented 24 November 2026 for a coursework use case. A local worksheet can identify the material at the level used in the source. It records the user's screening decision, derivation, and property evidence. Educational material families can then be compared without inventing commercial grades. Worksheets are read on demand and do not add records to the evidence catalogue.\n\nThe worked example shows two overlapping index ranges and a third that is strictly separated. Report these pairwise relationships. Revisit the supported mathematical form when an actual derivation requires additional terms. Revisit integration when the catalogue can supply checked screening outcomes and applicable observations.\n\n","title":"D11. Compare manually screened records on one derived index"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-splits/#Composition-safe-MP-recovery-splits","page":"Composition-safe MP recovery splits","text":"Day 2 implements membership only, through eka split-mp and the Julia library. No scoring, recovery metrics, or model evaluation occurs. The existing binary eka benchmark stays unchanged; the Day 3 PU evaluator now consumes these verified bundles. Joshua Corbett is the author of this implementation and documentation.\n\nThe frozen protocol specifies the scientific choices. This implementation adds no new chemistry filter or ranking-informed decision. Random composition holdout does not ensure unseen chemical systems. Mixed polymorphs stay grouped; unlabelled compounds remain distinct from negatives.","title":"Composition-safe MP recovery splits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-splits/#Reproduce-the-preserved-real-splits","page":"Composition-safe MP recovery splits","text":"From the repository root, with the original local snapshot and audit:\n\njulia --project=. bin/eka split-mp \\\n  --snapshot data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot \\\n  --audit reports/local/mp-ternary-audit \\\n  --output reports/local/mp-recovery-splits-v1\n\nThe output directory must be new and its parent must already exist. All 20 seeds (0–19) and planned budgets (20, 50, 100, 200) are mandatory for real data. The budgets are validated against pool size; no metrics or rankings are calculated. Do not re-export MP data to reproduce this experiment. Keep outputs under ignored reports/local/; the distribution review remains unresolved.","title":"Reproduce the preserved real splits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-splits/#Offline-synthetic-example","page":"Composition-safe MP recovery splits","text":"The fixture generator is Julia-native; no Python, mp-api, API key, network query, or private data is needed. Julia dependencies must already be installed. Run from the repository root:\n\nmkdir -p reports/local\njulia --project=. examples/mp_recovery/make_snapshot.jl reports/local/recovery-example-snapshot\njulia --project=. bin/eka audit-mp \\\n  --snapshot reports/local/recovery-example-snapshot \\\n  --output reports/local/recovery-example-audit\njulia --project=. bin/eka split-mp \\\n  --snapshot reports/local/recovery-example-snapshot \\\n  --audit reports/local/recovery-example-audit \\\n  --output reports/local/recovery-example-splits \\\n  --synthetic --budget 1 4\n\nThis arbitrary software fixture has ten positive, two unlabelled and one unresolved composition groups, including a mixed-flag polymorph group. Each split has eight training positives, two held-out positives and four candidates. These numbers are synthetic, not an experimental finding. The unresolved group's two structures appear in neither training nor candidate membership.\n\nSynthetic mode must be explicit and the snapshot must declare is_synthetic=true. A real snapshot cannot be passed unchanged to --synthetic. Synthetic bundles use eka-mp-recovery-synthetic-v1, not the real protocol ID. Only synthetic mode permits alternative --seeds and --budget values; duplicates, negatives, non-integers and oversized budgets are errors. Rerun commands with new directory names rather than overwriting a previous artifact.","title":"Offline synthetic example"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-splits/#Julia-interfaces","page":"Composition-safe MP recovery splits","text":"using EkaCompositions\n\n# File API: verifies provenance before saving all frozen real splits.\nreport = split_mp_recovery(\n    \"data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot\", \"reports/local/mp-ternary-audit\",\n    \"reports/local/mp-recovery-splits-library-v1\")\n\n# Pure synthetic membership helper: no file/provenance verification is possible here.\ngroups = vcat([(\"Li$(i)Na1O1\", \"positive\") for i in 1:10],\n              [(\"MgAl2O4\", \"unlabelled\"), (\"BaTiO3\", \"unresolved\")])\nresult = mp_recovery_splits(groups; seeds=[0, 1], budgets=[1, 3])\nsplit = first(result.splits)\ntraining, candidates = split.inputs.training, split.inputs.candidates\n# Only the evaluator may receive these; never pass them into rankers.\nheldout, labels = split.evaluation.heldout, split.evaluation.labels\n\nThe pure helper accepts (composition, label) tuples or two-field named tuples; compositions may be strings or Composition values. It canonicalizes first and rejects duplicates across all labels, including proportional formulas. The caller supplies already-grouped records; it never relabels or combines duplicates silently. Labels are exactly positive, unlabelled, or unresolved.\n\nFor P positives and U unlabelled groups, h = floor(P/5); training is P − h and candidates are h + U. Require P ≥ 5, U ≥ 1, and every planned budget ≤ h + U. Unresolved groups are tracked but excluded. Each output list uses canonical formula order. evaluation.labels aligns with the candidate list and uses no binary negative encoding.","title":"Julia interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-splits/#Determinism-and-provenance-checks","page":"Composition-safe MP recovery splits","text":"Positive selection uses ascending (SHA256(\"eka-pu-split-v1\\n\" + decimal_seed + \"\\n\" + canonical_formula), formula). The first h become holdouts. UTF-8, LF delimiters, no trailing newline in the hash payload, and explicit ones in formulas match the frozen protocol. Global RNG state, row order and structure multiplicity cannot affect membership.\n\nThe file API checks all of the following before reserving an output directory:\n\nExact frozen protocol document and real snapshot/audit hashes. Merely updating a tampered file's accompanying hash cannot substitute a different real dataset.\nSnapshot query/schema, original TSV/JSONL hashes, exporter implementation hash, and equality of original and audit-copied snapshot metadata.\nRebuilt audit labels, composition groups, material IDs, source-ID serialization, exclusions, counts, and audit/composition implementation hashes. Reconstruction uses captured input bytes in a temporary directory; original files are untouched.\nCanonical duplicate rejection, valid scope/labels, sufficient pool size, and the fixed real seed/budget configuration.\n\nSynthetic grouped-file row reordering and equivalent formula spelling are accepted only when the canonical rows still match the rebuilt audit. Their input byte hashes change but membership does not. Real files remain pinned to their exact frozen bytes. The audit's recorded Julia/package version strings may differ from the current runtime; all other audit metadata must match reconstruction. The new runtime versions are recorded separately in the bundle manifest.\n\nIntegrity is not upstream authentication. For real v1, the pinned JSONL/TSV were also semantically checked during Day 1. The Julia audit itself does not implement an arbitrary JSON parser or establish authenticity of synthetic JSON contents. A caller declaring new inputs synthetic cannot obtain a real protocol result. Changes to real source data or producer code require a reviewed compatibility check/new audited input and protocol version; there is no force/skip-provenance flag. Keep the shipped protocol document intact, including its bytes. Git attributes preserve LF endings for the protocol and frozen producer files even on Windows checkouts; automatic line-ending conversion must not invalidate their identities.","title":"Determinism and provenance checks"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-splits/#Artifact-layout-and-hashes","page":"Composition-safe MP recovery splits","text":"output/\n  manifest.toml\n  README.md\n  provenance/\n    protocol.md\n    snapshot/snapshot.toml\n    audit/{snapshot.toml,audit.toml,compositions.tsv,excluded.tsv}\n    implementation/{src/...,scripts/export_mp_pilot.py,Project.toml}\n    unresolved.tsv\n  split-00/                     # also split-01 through split-19\n    manifest.toml\n    inputs/training.tsv\n    inputs/candidates.tsv\n    evaluation/heldout.tsv\n    evaluation/labels.tsv\n\nRankers consume only inputs/training.tsv and inputs/candidates.tsv, each a single composition column. Source IDs, provenance flags, source record counts, and held-out labels are absent. Folder separation is an interface boundary, not an operating-system access-control sandbox: callers must not give rankers other files. Day 3's evaluator should enforce that boundary when invoking methods.\n\nEach split manifest records protocol ID/hash, scope, algorithm version, split seed, separate reserved ranking seed (10000 + split seed), tie seed 20260901, budgets, counts, original input hashes, implementation hashes, and four membership file hashes. These ranking/tie settings are recorded for later use, not executed. The root manifest binds all split manifests by hash and records unresolved and excluded counts. Membership files use UTF-8 with LF, a header, and a final LF; hashes cover the exact file bytes, including that header.\n\nThe bundle preserves exact relevant implementation bytes even when the working tree is dirty, without requiring Git at runtime. Keep the original snapshot TSV and JSONL alongside it; they are hashed but not duplicated in every bundle. No input/output absolute paths or timestamps are embedded, so reruns under the same code and Julia/package versions are byte-identical. Different runtime versions can change manifest metadata without changing deterministic membership. Day 5 must additionally freeze the experiment commit and resolved dependencies.\n\nThe writer exclusively creates a new directory and refuses existing paths, including dangling symlinks. If writing fails after reservation, it removes only its own new directory. Never reuse an output directory to append or replace splits.","title":"Artifact layout and hashes"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-splits/#Verification","page":"Composition-safe MP recovery splits","text":"julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_mp_export.py' -v\n\nSynthetic tests cover fixed independently computed holdouts, equivalent formulas, row reordering, global RNG independence, duplicate rejection, mixed polymorphs, train/pool disjointness, unresolved/excluded records, tiny/invalid pools, provenance corruption, output non-overwrite, manifest hashes, and exact CLI/library reruns. Existing binary benchmark tests remain part of the same suite.\n\n","title":"Verification"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-protocol/#MP-composition-recovery-protocol","page":"MP composition recovery protocol","text":"Protocol ID: eka-mp-recovery-v1\n\nFrozen: 31 August 2026, before recovery ranking evaluation.\n\nStatus: scientific contract for implementation; not an implemented PU runner, not measured performance, and not formal external preregistration.","title":"MP composition recovery protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-protocol/#Question-and-scope","page":"MP composition recovery protocol","text":"How well do simple composition rankings recover withheld compositions with MP experimental provenance, within MP-covered chemistry?\n\nRetain the audited oxygen-containing ternary pool: O plus exactly two other elements, non-deprecated records, and the export query's include_gnome=false. Keep H-containing and halogen-containing systems. Do not call this an oxide validator. Element membership does not establish oxygen oxidation state or exclude hydroxides, oxyhalides, salts, or unusual stoichiometries. The Day 1 coverage review supports retaining the existing pool without an additional unvalidated filter. There is no stability, energy, source-ID-presence, or stored-score cutoff.\n\nThis is a composition recovery experiment, not a synthesis recommendation. It measures neither first discovery nor transfer to wholly unseen chemical systems. The snapshot is fixed; do not re-query MP to reproduce it.","title":"Question and scope"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-protocol/#Data-identity-and-verification","page":"MP composition recovery protocol","text":"Source: data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot/, MP database 2026.04.13, retrieved 2026-08-31T12:16:39.322001+00:00, mp-api 0.46.5. Grouped input: reports/local/mp-ternary-audit/compositions.tsv.\n\nFile SHA-256\nSnapshot snapshot.toml 5c82fb38f90105a03779daab0cb32f2ba134cb1ce50cf94e845b38e92719ad58\nSnapshot records.tsv 98a6ce78d592e5e7e4cef98d675badcf991cc2caecef9a6b0ec10c1e08731a08\nSnapshot records.jsonl 5ba17f307d61c9635a93f75fe86afc9e81af430036f3ef1fc0fe199a75f3829b\nAudit audit.toml c63f465ab9104508b5a08f4a3263d267842b06d94954d0479e3662da7268eefa\nAudit compositions.tsv 722ecf2e40a99c59b0f024219782a1fe8791f000353cd5e6cf7faa1ef6e5213d\n\nBefore splitting, verify these identities, snapshot schema/query metadata, copied metadata equality, audit counts, and the exporter/audit/composition implementation hashes recorded in the source metadata. Fail if provenance cannot be established. Changed implementation requires a documented compatibility check or new audited input and protocol version; never silently accept stale provenance.\n\nHashes establish identity and integrity, not upstream authenticity or permission to distribute data. Day 1 additionally checked JSONL-to-TSV and grouped-record consistency offline. Full evidence, sample IDs, and coverage tables remain in reports/local/mp-recovery-day1-2026-08-31/.","title":"Data identity and verification"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-protocol/#Unit,-labels,-and-exclusions","page":"MP composition recovery protocol","text":"The unit is a canonical reduced composition. Sum repeated element symbols, reduce integer amounts by their greatest common divisor, sort symbols alphabetically, and include explicit ones, as in Eka's Composition. Group all polymorphs and equivalent formulas before any split. Structure count must not weight sampling, popularity, or metrics.\n\nEvidence within a composition group Label\nAt least one explicit theoretical=false record Positive\nEvery record explicitly theoretical=true Unlabelled\nNo positive record and at least one missing flag Unresolved\n\nMixed true/false groups are positive. An external source ID does not override a flag. Missing IDs do not change labels, and missing flags do not become true. A positive is an experimental-provenance proxy, not a literature-verified synthesis claim. This interpretation follows the MP provenance implementation; Day 1 compared installed emmet-core 0.87.2 with the retrieved upstream file. Neither establishes the exact server-side builder version for this snapshot.\n\nExclude unresolved groups from both training and evaluation. Preserve explicit record exclusions for invalid symbols, unsupported formulas, nonpositive, nonintegral or out-of-range counts, and records outside the declared scope. Do not round occupancies. Reject duplicate canonical input groups; do not silently choose one. If exclusions or source identity change, version the protocol before ranking. Unlabelled never means confirmed negative.","title":"Unit, labels, and exclusions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-protocol/#Frozen-experiment-choices","page":"MP composition recovery protocol","text":"Decision Frozen value\nHoldout h = floor(P / 5), where P is the eligible positive group count\nTraining All other positive groups, T = P - h\nCandidate pool All h held-out positives plus every eligible unlabelled group; N = h + U\nSplit seeds Exactly the 20 integers 0–19\nMain budget k = 100\nSecondary budgets k = 20, 50, 200; always report all four\nPrimary comparison Maximum training-composition cosine similarity minus training-element popularity, paired within each split\nReference One pseudorandom ranking per split plus exact uniform-random expectations\nRandom-ranking seed 10000 + split_seed (10000–10019), recorded separately\nShared tie seed 20260901, fixed for every method and split\nTuning None on evaluation splits; no hyperparameter search in this pilot\nExternal scores Not primary; exploratory only unless independence is established in a separately versioned protocol\nRepetitions Repeated holdouts of one snapshot, not independent experimental datasets\n\nReject empty/too-small inputs if h < 1, T < 1, U < 1, or any requested k exceeds N. Budgets must be distinct positive integers; do not truncate an oversized budget.","title":"Frozen experiment choices"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-protocol/#Exact-split,-tie,-and-random-procedures","page":"MP composition recovery protocol","text":"All strings below use UTF-8, literal LF newlines, no trailing newline, canonical formula f, and decimal nonnegative integer seeds without leading zeros. SHA-256 keys are lowercase hexadecimal strings ordered lexicographically ascending. Never use the language's process-dependent hash() or global RNG state.\n\nSort unique eligible positive formulas lexicographically. For split seed s, compute SHA256(\"eka-pu-split-v1\\n\" + s + \"\\n\" + f). Sort by (hash, f) ascending and hold out the first h. Remaining positives are training inputs.\nCandidate membership is the union of holdouts and all eligible unlabelled formulas, stored in canonical formula order. Exclude training positives and unresolved formulas, including equivalent representations.\nShared tie key is SHA256(\"eka-pu-tie-v1\\n20260901\\n\" + f). Popularity and similarity sort by descending finite score, then ascending tie key, then formula as a final hash-collision fallback. Use exact numeric equality for ties; no score rounding or tolerance grouping. Never use stored model scores.\nRandom ranking uses ascending SHA256(\"eka-pu-random-v1\\n\" + ranking_seed + \"\\n\" + f), then shared tie key, then formula. This is a versioned hash-based pseudorandom ordering. Its analytical reference is ideal uniform sampling without replacement; a fixed hash run need not equal the expected number of hits.\n\nSeeds serve different purposes and have different hash domains. Do not select seeds after seeing outcomes. Store evaluation labels separately from what rankers receive: training formulas and candidate formulas only.","title":"Exact split, tie, and random procedures"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-protocol/#Methods-and-permitted-inputs","page":"MP composition recovery protocol","text":"Training-element popularity. For each element e let n(e) be the number of training-positive compositions containing it, counting each composition once. For candidate c with element set E(c), score sum(n(e) for e in E(c)) / (length(E(c)) * T). This preserves the existing binary benchmark's popularity definition but fits it anew on each split. An unseen element contributes zero. All candidates here have three elements and oxygen; oxygen's contribution is the constant 1/3. Report tie sizes and whether the other element frequencies dominate. Do not reuse a cache fitted to all positives.\n\nMaximum composition similarity. Represent each composition as its vector of reduced element counts, zero outside its elements. Score c by max(dot(c,t) / (norm(c)*norm(t)) for t in training). Reuse Eka's pairwise cosine calculation (Float64, clamped to [0,1]) without inheriting its stored-score tie breaker. Choose no reference using held-out labels. No atom weighting, learned embeddings, oxidation features, fitting, or search over similarity variants. Batch or stream the maximum; a full candidate-by-training matrix is unnecessary.\n\nRandom. Uses only candidate formulas and its declared ranking seed. No source IDs, MP flags, stored scores, evaluation labels, structure counts, chemical-system label frequencies, or source dates are scoring features for any primary method. Changing evaluation labels with fixed training/candidate formulas must not alter scores or ranks. Source identifiers may be used for audit provenance only.\n\nExternal score gate. Day 4 may inspect model/version, training sources and membership/exclusions, cutoff evidence, normalization, coverage, and duplicate score handling for Seko or another proposed score. A newer snapshot does not prove training independence. Without adequate evidence omit the score or label it exploratory outside the primary comparison. Inspect coverage before performance; never impute missing scores or silently drop rows. A restricted-pool analysis must first declare its pool, label coverage, and matching baselines for that same pool. Any primary promotion needs a new protocol before its real evaluation.","title":"Methods and permitted inputs"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-protocol/#Metrics-and-analysis","page":"MP composition recovery protocol","text":"For each method, split, and budget, let H@k be the number of held-out positive compositions among the first k ranked candidates. With h holdouts and N candidates:\n\nMetric Definition\nObserved-positive hits H@k\nObserved-label fraction H@k / k\nHeld-out-positive recall H@k / h\nObserved-label enrichment (H@k / k) / (h / N)\nUniform-random expected hits k * h / N\nUniform-random expected observed-label fraction h / N\nUniform-random expected recall k / N\nUniform-random expected enrichment 1\n\nThese formulas require h > 0, N >= k > 0. Check them against hand-calculated synthetic fixtures, including tied scores and invalid denominators. A top-ranked unlabelled entry is not a confirmed failed synthesis. Do not report ordinary negative-class accuracy, specificity, or false-positive rate as a discovery result.\n\nLead with D_s = H_similarity@100 - H_popularity@100 for every one of the 20 identical splits. Show all D_s, mean, median, and positive/zero/negative counts. Report all other budgets as secondary and keep weak/zero effects. Show the random realizations alongside their exact expectations; extra random seeds are not extra datasets. Report complete pool/holdout sizes and complete rankings locally.\n\nDescribe variation across overlapping holdouts as split sensitivity. Do not claim a population confidence interval from their standard error, bootstrap just the selected top rows, or infer equality from overlapping separate intervals. No formal significance claim is planned. A negative or inconclusive outcome is valid; beating a baseline is not a completion requirement.\n\nPredetermined descriptive breakdowns: chemical system; element membership (with O identified as universal); H-containing and any-halogen-containing groups (F, Cl, Br, I, At, Ts); mixed-flag positive groups; score-tie sizes. Report subgroup denominators, treat overlapping element groups as overlapping, and do not select a subgroup winner. New subgroup analyses are exploratory. MP coverage, historical research effort, provenance quality, normalization restrictions, and shared train/test chemical systems constrain interpretation.","title":"Metrics and analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-recovery-protocol/#Run-records,-amendments,-and-release-boundary","page":"MP composition recovery protocol","text":"Before real evaluation, freeze the tested implementation commit and dependencies. If dirty, retain the exact changed/untracked file bytes, patch, and their hashes. Each new, non-overwritten run directory must bind protocol ID and file hash, snapshot/audit identities, algorithm versions, split/ranking/tie seeds, membership hashes and counts, code/dependency versions, full rankings, and per-split metrics. Keep timestamps and runtime separate from deterministic outputs. Reproduce one split in a fresh directory and compare membership, ranks, and metrics exactly.\n\nOnly synthetic evaluation is allowed during implementation validation. Do not inspect real ranking performance before the implementation freeze. If a defect requires a change, record it, bump the affected version, and rerun every affected method/split; do not retain favorable earlier results.\n\nRaw records, samples, membership, rankings, detailed coverage tables, and unreviewed data derivatives stay under ignored data/local/ or reports/local/. The data/provenance review records unresolved terms; this protocol grants no redistribution permission. No data publication, push, new export, or background schedule is part of this Day 1 milestone. The existing binary eka benchmark contract remains unchanged; benchmark-pu is still a proposed future interface, not an available command.\n\nVersion history: v1 (31 August 2026) retains the roadmap defaults and specifies hash domains, seed values, tie equality, method equations, and descriptive breakdowns before recovery evaluation.\n\n","title":"Run records, amendments, and release boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarking/#Fixed-budget-ranking-experiments","page":"Fixed-budget ranking experiments","text":"This first milestone evaluates supplied scores against simple baselines on one explicit, labelled candidate pool. It does not fit Seko's model, generate outcomes, split a dataset, or claim to establish real discovery performance. The synthetic example exercises the software only. Its outcomes are arbitrary test labels, not claims about the named compounds.\n\nFrom the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. bin/eka benchmark \\\n  --input examples/benchmark/candidates.tsv \\\n  --training examples/benchmark/training.tsv \\\n  --methods score,random,popularity \\\n  --budget 2 5 10 --seeds 0 1 2 \\\n  --source \"Synthetic software example v1; arbitrary scores and outcomes, disjoint hand-authored pools\" \\\n  --output /tmp/eka-benchmark-demo\n\nThe output directory must not exist; its parent must exist. No database is changed and no network access is needed. An input validation failure creates no report. Writing errors remove the newly created report; abrupt process termination can leave an incomplete directory. Existing outputs are never overwritten.","title":"Fixed-budget ranking experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarking/#Inputs-and-experimental-responsibility","page":"Fixed-budget ranking experiments","text":"Candidates are UTF-8 TSV with the exact header composition<TAB>score<TAB>outcome. Each row has a supported simple formula, a finite score, and literal 0 or 1. Training is a separate known-positive reference set with the exact single-column header composition. LF and CRLF are accepted; blank lines, extra columns, quoting, and unknown outcomes are not supported. Both sets must be nonempty. Canonical duplicates and cross-set overlap are errors, including scaled formulas.\n\nFor a real experiment, define the outcome first: for example, a specific measured criterion or recovery of held-out documented compounds. A missing database entry is not evidence of an unsuccessful candidate. If only positives and unlabelled records exist, this binary evaluation protocol is unsuitable without additional labels; do not turn unlabelled records into negatives.\n\nConstruct the training/evaluation split before fitting the upstream score model. Generate scores for the entire declared evaluation pool using training information only. An old precomputed database may already encode held-out compounds; disjoint TSVs alone cannot prevent that leakage. Record dataset version, label definition, split construction, score-model version/training provenance, and candidate-generation rules in --source. The tool records your description but cannot verify those claims. Do not interpret the score method as a validated Seko reproduction unless you have independently established that provenance.\n\nAll methods evaluate the same pool without score thresholds. Choose a chemical family upstream and record its definition; there is no implicit --family oxides filter or family classification. Results apply to the supplied pool and sampling scheme, not to all possible chemistry. Verify redistribution rights before sharing reports: they include full input snapshots and labels.","title":"Inputs and experimental responsibility"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarking/#Methods","page":"Fixed-budget ranking experiments","text":"Method Ranking Ties\nscore Descending supplied score Ascending canonical formula\npopularity Mean training occurrence fraction of the candidate's distinct elements Ascending canonical formula\nrandom Ascending SHA-256 of eka-random-v1\\nSEED\\nCANONICAL_FORMULA Ascending canonical formula\n\nTraining occurrence counts each element once per unique training composition. Unseen elements have frequency zero. This is an intentionally simple element popularity baseline, not a learned substitution or stoichiometry model. Baselines never consult evaluation outcomes or use supplied scores as tie breakers. Hash ordering provides a deterministic pseudorandom baseline independent of input order and global RNG state. Seeds are nonnegative machine-sized integers. Each random seed is reported separately; deterministic methods run once. Seeds measure random-baseline variability on this pool, not dataset uncertainty or confidence intervals. Stronger conclusions require independent splits and datasets.","title":"Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarking/#Metrics","page":"Fixed-budget ranking experiments","text":"For budget k, select exactly the first k unique candidates. Budgets must be positive and no larger than the pool; the tool never silently clips them.\n\nField Definition\nhits Number of selected candidates with outcome 1\nprecision Hits / k\nrecall Hits / number of positive candidates in the entire evaluation pool\nenrichment Precision / positive fraction of the entire evaluation pool\nnovel_system_fraction Fraction selected whose element set is absent from training\nunique_system_fraction Number of distinct selected element sets / k\nelement_coverage Number of distinct selected elements / number of distinct pool elements\n\nRecall and enrichment are JSON null if the pool contains no positives. Chemical systems ignore stoichiometry: FeO and Fe2O3 have the same system. Novelty and unique-system fraction are simple composition-level proxies, not measures of physical usefulness, chemical distance, or new crystal structures. Every candidate is composition-disjoint from training, so exact-formula novelty would trivially equal one; element-set novelty is more informative here.","title":"Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/benchmarking/#Reports-and-reruns","page":"Fixed-budget ranking experiments","text":"report/\n  config.toml       # parameters, source, input hashes, runtime/package/code versions\n  input.tsv        # exact original candidate bytes\n  training.tsv     # exact original training bytes\n  candidates.csv   # complete rankings, stored scores, method values, labels\n  metrics.json     # schema_version=1; per-method/seed/budget results\n  benchmark.md     # readable table, definitions, and limitations\n\nCSV's seed cell is empty for deterministic methods; JSON uses null. For random rankings the ranking_value column contains a hex digest (ascending); other methods have numeric descending ranking values. Preserve its type when importing. Reports have no timestamps or absolute paths, so identical inputs/options and software produce identical files. The benchmark source hash identifies changes within a package version; preserve the repository revision and Julia environment as well for archival reproduction. No plots or aggregate significance claims are generated in this milestone.\n\nRerun through the library using the saved configuration (with a fresh output path):\n\nusing EkaCompositions, TOML\nreport = \"/tmp/eka-benchmark-demo\"\nconfig = TOML.parsefile(joinpath(report, \"config.toml\"))\nbenchmark_tsv(joinpath(report, config[\"input\"]),\n    joinpath(report, config[\"training\"]), \"/tmp/eka-benchmark-rerun\";\n    source=config[\"source\"], methods=config[\"methods\"],\n    budgets=config[\"budgets\"], seeds=config[\"seeds\"])\n\nFor in-memory experiments, benchmark_rankings(records, training; budgets, seeds, methods) accepts (formula_or_Composition, score, integer_outcome) tuples and training formulas or Composition values. It returns canonical inputs, complete rankings, metrics, and resolved settings without writing files or mutating inputs.\n\nThe next milestones can add split generation and model fitting (including a matrix-factorization baseline), then uncertainty/diversity selection and sequential reveal/retrain simulations. Those require explicit leakage-safe training adapters; they are not implemented by this evaluation command.\n\n","title":"Reports and reruns"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/publication-permissions/#Publication-permissions-register","page":"Publication permissions register","text":"Reviewed 31 August 2026. This is an evidence and release-handling register, not a legal opinion. The reviewed source-only archive is ready as a release candidate; this does not approve a separate MP record-level data bundle.","title":"Publication permissions register"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/publication-permissions/#Code-decision-and-ownership-limits","page":"Publication permissions register","text":"Joshua Corbett selected MIT and confirmed that no applicable institutional IP exceptions or assignment obligations apply to this project on 31 August\n\nOn that owner declaration, the root MIT licence is now\n\napplied to Eka's original code, documentation and original test material. This records the owner's confirmation, not an institutional clearance letter or an independent legal determination. No repository visibility change is requested. Source licensing does not clear third-party data rights.\n\nThe optional Seko SQLite database and its research are separately attributed in README; the upstream Python script and full database are not bundled. Eight composition/score pairs in the tiny SQLite fixture match the upstream README, and some are repeated in examples, tests and precompilation. Earlier descriptions of all these rows as invented were incorrect. The upstream BSD 3-Clause notice is retained in third-party notices, with exact scope and source revision. Original Eka code remains MIT. This is not a blanket grant over the full database or the sources underlying it.\n\nThe recovery pilot and label-sensitivity experiments use Materials Project API data, not Seko scores; the latter remain excluded from the primary experiment. Frozen local evidence retains its historical text and hashes; this register records the subsequent software-licensing decision.","title":"Code decision and ownership limits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/publication-permissions/#Materials-Project-terms-evidence","page":"Publication permissions register","text":"The owner supplied the MP Terms of Use on 31 August 2026. The text explicitly permits API analysis and presenting processed results with attribution, and licenses downloaded Content under CC BY 4.0. The supplied text, checksum, source caveat and application to all six retained fields are recorded in the terms evidence review.\n\nThis supersedes the earlier missing-text blocker. A read-only check of the live official page on 31 August 2026 confirmed the same material grant and requirements. A separate provider permission request is not required by that text for uses within its grant. Specific exceptions and the contents of any proposed data bundle remain to be checked; do not apply MIT to MP data. GNoME remains excluded, and external database identifiers do not license the underlying third-party records. Original source records/structures are not bundled.","title":"Materials Project terms evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/publication-permissions/#Artifact-by-artifact-status","page":"Publication permissions register","text":"Category Basis established so far Attribution / notices to preserve Restrictions and unresolved questions Current release status\nEka original code and methodological documentation Owner confirms authority; MIT applied in root LICENSE Joshua Corbett; MIT copyright and permission notice Third-party material retains its own terms; this is not data clearance Original software licensed under MIT\nDependencies Installed source licence files inspected for the recorded versions; core Julia packages below have MIT notices Preserve each package's copyright and licence notices when distributing covered code Wrapper licences do not cover wrapped binaries; transitive/native/Python components require artifact-specific review if bundled Conditional component evidence only; no blanket bundle clearance\nTest fixtures and examples Original synthetic MP/benchmark fixtures; tiny SQLite fixture includes eight Seko README pairs and four original rows MIT for original material; retained upstream BSD notice for reused pairs Do not label reused pairs invented or infer full-database rights; see third-party notices Software-example notices supplied; live source datasets remain separate\nAggregate results and plots Computed locally; current official MP terms permit covered adaptations with attribution MP citation, version/date, source, protocol, transformations and limitations Apply attribution and transformation notices; review any specific exception and final contents Attributed aggregate prose in the source archive reviewed; standalone result bundle remains separate\nComposition records MP API composition/formula fields; source and transformation hashes retained MP source/version/date, composition normalization and grouping changes Supplied CC BY Content grant is the working basis; current-version applicability and specific exceptions remain a final review item Preparation supported for covered content; records remain local\nProvenance fields MP flags, IDs and source references retained and grouped MP attribution; third-party attribution where established General Content grant is evidence, not field-specific provider confirmation; do not infer underlying database rights Review source-specific notices; no original ICSD/Pauling records distributed\nOther derived artifacts Splits, candidate-level rankings, policy labels, audit tables and detailed diagnostics transform MP records Source, protocol, hashes, representation and processing history Retain CC BY attribution for covered content and identify transformations; review source-specific exceptions Preparation supported for covered content; no automatic publication\nEnvironment and run records Project/Manifest, source hashes and local reproduction instructions are captured Package notices if actual sources/binaries are bundled Review paths, source archives, logs, licences and data-bearing files separately; a lockfile is not a dependency licence Source archive excludes local runs, environment lockfile and logs; full environments remain local","title":"Artifact-by-artifact status"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/publication-permissions/#Dependency-evidence-captured","page":"Publication permissions register","text":"Installed licence notices were read for ArgParse 1.2.0, DBInterface 2.7.0, SQLite.jl 1.8.2, PrecompileTools 1.3.4, SHA 0.7.0 and TOML 1.0.3. These provide MIT-style source-code grants with notice requirements. SQLite_jll 3.53.2+0 explicitly distinguishes its MIT wrapper code from the bundled binary's terms. Do not extend any of those grants to Eka, MP records or a complete runtime bundle.\n\nA local inventory of 30 Julia package source locations and discovered licence files, plus copies/hashes of the reviewed notices, is preserved under reports/local/mp-label-sensitivity-v1-2026-08-31/. It is not a completed transitive/native/Python redistribution audit. The proposed source package should not vendor those environments by accident.","title":"Dependency evidence captured"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/publication-permissions/#Open-actions","page":"Publication permissions register","text":"Use the confirmed current MP terms and CC BY 4.0 as the working basis for covered content. Before a record-level data release, check specific exceptions, required notices and whether MP guidance is appropriate for the concrete repackaging plan. The older blanket clarification request remains unsent.\nPreserve the applied MIT licence and scoped third-party notices in source distributions. The owner-confirmation and code-licence decision are complete; further incorporated material requires its own provenance review.\nRe-run python scripts/verify_release.py --archive <treeish> on the exact source release commit. Review any later proposed data or runtime bundle as a distinct artifact; keep unresolved material excluded.\n\nFor the 0.1.1 release, the evidence-bundle decision is do not publish. The source-only release does not need it, and no final file-by-file contents, source-specific exception, attribution, environment or credential/path review has been completed for the record-level MP data, rankings, factors and runtime artifacts. This is a deferral, not a finding that such a bundle cannot be published after its separate review.\n\nThe environment restore, original-code licensing, current MP terms check and source-candidate content review are complete. Any record-level data/runtime bundle and specific source exceptions remain separate from MIT software licensing.\n\n","title":"Open actions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot-reproduction/#Frozen-pilot-run-and-reproduction","page":"Frozen pilot run and reproduction","text":"The first complete v1 pilot was run locally on 31 August 2026. It used clean implementation commit 568ed605c131edfa40695afc53781015c9739f6c, Julia 1.12.6, and the unchanged frozen protocol. All 20 splits, three methods and four budgets completed. Scientific results and unreviewed derivatives remain outside Git; this document records the workflow, not a public release of measured results.\n\nLocal evidence directory: reports/local/mp-recovery-pilot-v1-2026-08-31/. Start with its REPORT.md, freeze.json, reproduction-checks.json, and analysis/report.md. results/ contains the original evaluator outputs; clean-real-results/ contains the separate clean-checkout rerun. The original snapshot and audit remain in their original local directories and are required.","title":"Frozen pilot run and reproduction"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot-reproduction/#Environment-decision","page":"Frozen pilot run and reproduction","text":"Preserve the complete resolved Manifest.toml and Project.toml with each run, together with the exact Julia version, platform and source archive. Do not commit a moving library-wide manifest merely to describe an old experiment. Do not restore an experiment by resolving Project.toml alone or running Pkg.update(). The run's environment directory and freeze record bind the actual lockfile bytes.\n\nThe local manifest retains an unused historical Recommender package entry at path .. Preserve it unchanged for this run: the clean-checkout restore and tests established that EkaCompositions resolves and runs correctly with those exact bytes. This is an environment record, not an instruction to reintroduce the old package name.","title":"Environment decision"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot-reproduction/#Restore-a-fresh-checkout","page":"Frozen pilot run and reproduction","text":"Install Julia 1.12.6 on the recorded platform for exact Float64 comparisons. Python 3.11+ suffices for standard-library verification; the run records 3.14.6. No MP API key, exporter dependency environment or new API query is required. From the repository root, choose a new destination for each execution:\n\ngit clone --no-hardlinks . /tmp/eka-pilot-reproduce\ngit -C /tmp/eka-pilot-reproduce checkout --detach 568ed605c131edfa40695afc53781015c9739f6c\ncp reports/local/mp-recovery-pilot-v1-2026-08-31/environment/Manifest.toml /tmp/eka-pilot-reproduce/Manifest.toml\ncd /tmp/eka-pilot-reproduce\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test(; allow_reresolve=false)'\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_mp_export.py' -v\n\nCheck the restored manifest's SHA-256 against freeze.json before and after instantiation. Instantiation may download pinned packages and binary artifacts; it must not change the lockfile. The observed restore reused the installed Julia depot; this verifies a clean source checkout, not a network-free or empty-depot installation. Source and lockfile hashes do not guarantee that upstream packages will remain available forever. Cross-platform bitwise reproduction was not tested.\n\nRun the synthetic end-to-end example in that checkout first. Then regenerate all real splits from the preserved original snapshot and audit:\n\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. bin/eka split-mp \\\n  --snapshot /absolute/path/to/mp-ternary-snapshot \\\n  --audit /absolute/path/to/mp-ternary-audit \\\n  --output /absolute/path/to/new-splits\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. bin/eka benchmark-pu \\\n  --snapshot /absolute/path/to/mp-ternary-snapshot \\\n  --audit /absolute/path/to/mp-ternary-audit \\\n  --splits /absolute/path/to/new-splits \\\n  --output /absolute/path/to/new-results\n\nThe exact commands actually exercised are preserved as argument arrays in reproduction-commands.json; reproduction.log records their completion.","title":"Restore a fresh checkout"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot-reproduction/#Checks-completed","page":"Frozen pilot run and reproduction","text":"Julia: 1,729 tests passed both before the freeze/run and in the clean checkout.\nOffline exporter: 10 tests passed in both environments.\nClean-checkout synthetic workflow: 20 splits, three methods, two budgets.\nClean-checkout real workflow: all 80 membership files, 60 full ranking files, 240 metric rows, and the raw report matched the primary run byte for byte.\nRuntime is intentionally excluded. The sole evaluator configuration difference is the split-bundle manifest hash: regenerated bundles capture the newer producer implementation. Scientific membership and evaluation implementation hashes are identical, as are all deterministic scientific outputs.\n\nscripts/analyze_pu_pilot.py was added after the experiment freeze and reads saved results only. It independently reconstructs seeded membership and labels, checks ranking permutations, score/tie ordering, random keys, popularity scores, and all metric denominators and values. Similarity is fully recomputed by the clean Julia rerun; the Python check only checks its bounds and ordering. The analysis never changes inputs, scores or labels, and refuses an existing output directory. Run it from the current source tree, not the earlier frozen commit:\n\npython3 scripts/analyze_pu_pilot.py /absolute/path/to/results /absolute/path/to/new-analysis\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_pu_analysis.py' -v\n\nFive additional synthetic checks cover complete recovery, overwrite refusal, incorrect metrics/scores with rewritten checksums, duplicate ranking entries and altered membership. Original input provenance remains the frozen Julia loader's responsibility; this analysis is not a standalone authenticity verifier.\n\nThe local analysis records every paired difference, all budgets, all predefined subgroups and denominators, and exact tie sizes. No confidence intervals, significance tests, alternative-label experiments, or system holdouts are added. Plotting is optional and separate from validation: the local plot_pilot.py and captured Python plotting versions produced standalone PNG/SVG figures.\n\nThe completed local evidence is protected against ordinary writes and inventoried by SHA-256. This is an integrity check and operational freeze, not a WORM storage guarantee: the owner can change file permissions. Preserve the original artifacts and use new directories for subsequent analyses. Sharing permissions remain unresolved, including for aggregate results; nothing was published or pushed.\n\n","title":"Checks completed"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/audit-hardening-compatibility/#Audit-hardening-compatibility-check","page":"Audit hardening compatibility check","text":"Reviewed 31 August 2026 after commit b1d3ef5 strengthened the MP snapshot and source-release audits. The change requires complete exporter metadata, parses the preserved JSONL records, independently normalizes them, and reconciles them with the saved TSV. It also rejects non-regular members in source archives.","title":"Audit hardening compatibility check"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/audit-hardening-compatibility/#Frozen-snapshot-result","page":"Audit hardening compatibility check","text":"The hardened audit completed against the frozen 24,020-record snapshot without an API query. It reported 5,359 positive, 7,147 unlabelled and zero unresolved composition groups. Its scientific artifacts matched the frozen v1 audit byte for byte:\n\nArtifact SHA-256 Frozen match\ncompositions.tsv 722ecf2e40a99c59b0f024219782a1fe8791f000353cd5e6cf7faa1ef6e5213d yes\nexcluded.tsv 99bd23253455f17375178383f501e8ee46af77776f675e17cff11c9fe7ab46cb yes\n\nEvery one of the 81 scientific membership files in the frozen v1 split bundle also matched a newly generated bundle: training compositions, candidates, held-out compositions, evaluation labels and the unresolved-group file across all 20 splits. The only audit.toml difference was audit_code_sha256, changing from the historical implementation hash 40e758732869135283d7ef450c8125d193b8696101a459d08c3d179297a2d345 to the hardened implementation hash e7d3c274e0fa0cf12ca6301688e9a5b3f8d526f602793e14d6370bcb2f244c6e.\n\nThe compatibility run then executed all 20 splits, four budgets and the random, popularity and similarity methods through the hardened loader. All 60 complete ranking files and metrics.tsv matched the frozen v1 results byte for byte. The metrics hash remained 2c0607f62f9dcf073c57b1ff754bdb77dbff7876bed1667e3b63fce4a26c4907.\n\nThe frozen real v1 loader continues to require the exact historical audit file hash and all frozen input hashes. It permits that pinned report to retain its historical implementation hash while current code must reconstruct every other schema, provenance, count, grouping and exclusion field. Synthetic and new audit artifacts continue to require their current implementation identity.","title":"Frozen snapshot result"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/audit-hardening-compatibility/#Decision","page":"Audit hardening compatibility check","text":"No normalization mismatch was found in the frozen snapshot. The hardened audit does not change composition groups or experimental membership, and no ranking, metric or model-fitting source changed. The completed random, popularity, similarity and element-pair results therefore remain the frozen evidence. The v1 compatibility rerun confirms identical simple-method results; it is verification, not a replacement experiment or an additional replicate.\n\nThe full local Julia and Python suites passed after the change, and the exact source archive passed its content audit. GitHub Actions run 33446865634 passed on Linux, macOS and Windows configurations for b1d3ef5.\n\nThis check establishes internal compatibility and file consistency. It does not authenticate the upstream service, re-query Materials Project, expand data-sharing permission, or turn the provenance proxy into verified synthesis evidence.\n\n","title":"Decision"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/engineering-roadmap/#Engineering-roadmap:-documentation,-API-contract-and-package-quality","page":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality","text":"Prepared 4 September 2026. This covers software packaging and documentation quality. It does not propose new science; scientific sequencing lives in the recovery roadmap, and no item here amends a frozen protocol.\n\nTasks 1–4 and 6 are complete. Task 5 is deferred to a version boundary.","title":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/engineering-roadmap/#Constraints","page":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality","text":"These bound every task below. Violating any breaks the package or the comparability of existing evidence.\n\nC1. Four protocol documents are content-hash-pinned. recovery_protocol() (src/mp_recovery.jl) reads the document from docs/ and throws unless its SHA-256 matches a frozen literal: mp-recovery-protocol.md, mp-label-sensitivity-protocol.md, mp-system-holdout-protocol.md and mp-element-pair-protocol.md. They are never moved, renamed, reformatted or edited. A change means a new protocol identifier and a new pin.\n\nC2. Five docs/ paths are literal provenance-manifest keys. src/mp_element_pair.jl records docs/mp-element-pair-protocol.md and docs/mp-learned-feasibility.md; src/mp_system_holdout.jl records docs/mp-recovery-protocol.md, docs/mp-label-sensitivity-protocol.md and docs/mp-system-holdout-protocol.md. Moving one changes manifest keys and breaks key-for-key comparison with the baselines under reports/local/. mp-external-score-provenance.md and mp-pu-evaluation.md appear only in prose and are not constrained.\n\nC3. Provenance hashing is broad, and is the reason not to reformat src/. src/mp_element_pair.jl and src/mp_system_holdout.jl read and hash roughly ten src/*.jl files, four scripts, two documents and Project.toml into implementation_hashes. Any byte change to any of them changes recorded hashes. The .gitattributes text eol=lf entries are checkout normalisation, not a formatting guard. src/ranking.jl is in no list, which is why Task 1 was cheap.\n\nC4. Source paths under src/ are stable by contract, enforced by the provenance-path test in test/test_mp_element_pair.jl.\n\nC5. The v1 command-line surface is frozen — six subcommands plus the default bare-query mode.\n\nC6. No real snapshots, record-level results or credentials enter the repository, enforced by scripts/verify_release.py.\n\nC7. Further release-gate rules. verify_release.py rejects Manifest.toml and .env by name at any depth, rejects any tracked file containing an absolute home-directory path, and caps files at 2 MB. Documentation examples therefore use relative or placeholder paths only.","title":"Constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/engineering-roadmap/#Task-1-—-Public-API-contract-(complete)","page":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality","text":"Delivered in docs/api-stability.md and test/test_api.jl: three tiers (stable, stable command-line behaviour, experimental), database-schema and report-format compatibility, and docstrings for the last three undocumented exports. The provenance-path guard was extended to the mp_system_holdout and mp_label_sensitivity lists.","title":"Task 1 — Public API contract (complete)"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/engineering-roadmap/#Task-2-—-Package-quality-automation,-excluding-formatting","page":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality","text":"Aqua.jl. Method ambiguities and unbound type parameters are already clean, and there is no type piracy. Two prerequisites: Random and Test in [extras] have no [compat] entries and Aqua's dependency-compat check inspects extras, so those and Aqua itself need entries. Aqua's undocumented names check relies on a Julia 1.11+ facility, so it is inert on the 1.10 job; Task 1 having documented every export removes that asymmetry as a concern. Adding Aqua to Project.toml changes recorded implementation hashes under C3.\n\nCoverage. julia-actions/julia-runtest already collects coverage; only processing and upload need adding, on the Ubuntu current-Julia job. A badge follows the first real measurement, not the configuration.\n\nPython 3.12 and 3.13. The four analysis test files run in the Julia test job, not the exporter job, so both jobs need the version matrix or neither gains coverage. Analysis scripts are standard-library only; the pinned mp-api client is optional and not installed in continuous integration.\n\nDependency automation. Dependabot for GitHub Actions, weekly. CompatHelper for Julia bounds. Manifest.toml is ignored, so there is no lockfile churn. No pip ecosystem entry: scripts/requirements-mp.txt is a deliberately pinned optional client.","title":"Task 2 — Package-quality automation, excluding formatting"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/engineering-roadmap/#Task-3-—-CONTRIBUTING.md","page":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality","text":"Setup, the three test commands, the release-gate order, and the repository's distinctive rules: protocol documents are frozen (C1); provenance paths are stable (C2–C4); a software fix keeps the protocol identifier while a new scientific question needs a prospective protocol written before any run; the API tiers from Task 1; no real data (C6); do not reformat src/ (C3); no absolute home paths in examples (C7).","title":"Task 3 — CONTRIBUTING.md"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/engineering-roadmap/#Task-4-—-Documenter-site-(complete)","page":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality","text":"Delivered in docs/make.jl, docs/Project.toml and docs/src/, with a Documentation job in ci.yml running julia-actions/julia-docdeploy.\n\nThe canonical documents are never written back to. make.jl assembles a staging tree under docs/staging/ — gitignored, rebuilt from scratch each run — and Documenter reads that, not docs/. All 27 canonical documents are copied byte for byte, satisfying C1 and leaving C2's manifest keys untouched.\n\nLayout. The staging tree mirrors the repository, with docs/ renamed to reference/: authored pages sit at the root, canonical documents under reference/, and LICENSE and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md are mirrored from the repository root. That placement is what makes the ../LICENSE and ../THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md links in mp-data-provenance-review.md, performance.md and publication-permissions.md resolve without editing them. Documenter rewrites the depth correctly under prettyurls, which was verified. The one build-time transform is on the mirrored THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, whose docs/... links become reference/...; canonical documents are not rewritten.\n\ncheckdocs = :exports. The manual is required to cover the entire exported surface, which docs/src/api.md does, grouped by the three tiers from Task 1 — so the API contract and the reference cannot drift apart. The Research modules are listed in checkdocs_ignored_modules: they are internal by contract and are deliberately absent from the site.\n\nNavigation is grouped explicitly in make.jl. A document that is added to docs/ without being grouped still ships, under \"Additional documents\", and a group entry naming a document that does not exist fails the build.\n\nExecutable examples. Five @example blocks in docs/src/getting-started.md execute at build time against the shipped fixture, reached through pkgdir(EkaCompositions) rather than a relative path. As anticipated, this guarantees the examples still run, not that command-line output still matches; the quick-start shell output remains an unchecked text block.\n\nDeployment. deploydocs publishes to gh-pages using GITHUB_TOKEN, with push_preview on. GitHub Pages must be pointed at the gh-pages branch once, in repository settings, before the site is reachable. /dev/ follows main; /stable/ appears at the next tag, since v0.1.0 predates this task. The README carries a dev-documentation badge.\n\ndocs/build/ and docs/staging/ are ignored, and docs/Manifest.toml is already covered by the repository-wide Manifest.toml rule (C7), verified against the release gate.","title":"Task 4 — Documenter site (complete)"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/engineering-roadmap/#Task-5-—-Formatting-(deferred-to-a-version-boundary)","page":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality","text":"No blanket reformat. Either check formatting on changed files only, or take a single whole-repository pass at 0.2.0 with the hash churn under C3 recorded in the changelog.","title":"Task 5 — Formatting (deferred to a version boundary)"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/engineering-roadmap/#Task-6-—-Release-checklist-(complete)","page":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality","text":"Delivered in docs/release-readiness.md: an ordered pre-tag checklist freezes and records the candidate commit, runs the Julia, Python and exact-commit archive gates, requires the matching continuous-integration run and documentation site, and verifies the commit again immediately before tagging. The 6 September 2026 review records the actual local and CI findings. No tag or release was created.","title":"Task 6 — Release checklist (complete)"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/engineering-roadmap/#Order","page":"Engineering roadmap: documentation, API contract and package quality","text":"1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 6, with 5 deferred. Each task is a separate commit.\n\n","title":"Order"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-metrics/#Ranking-evaluation-metric-contract","page":"Ranking evaluation metric contract","text":"recovery_metrics is the reusable experimental metric kernel proposed in the evaluation roadmap. It evaluates an already ordered candidate pool; it does not construct a split, score candidates or attach a scientific meaning to labels.","title":"Ranking evaluation metric contract"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-metrics/#Inputs-and-unit-of-evaluation","page":"Ranking evaluation metric contract","text":"using EkaCompositions\n\nranking = [\"LiNaO\", \"Li2NaO\", \"CaTiO3\", \"MgAl2O4\"]\nrelevant = [\"LiNaO\", \"Li2NaO\", \"MgAl2O4\"]\nrows = recovery_metrics(ranking, relevant;\n    budgets=[1, 2, 4], scores=[0.9, 0.9, 0.8, 0.1])\n\nranking is a complete, ordered collection of unique formula strings or Composition values. relevant is a nonempty unique subset. Canonically equivalent spellings are duplicates. budgets are unique positive integers no larger than the candidate count; output rows are sorted by budget.\n\nThe natural unit is one ranking/candidate pool, such as one outer recovery split. Metrics can be macro-averaged across splits later. The function deliberately does not pool splits, because overlapping holdouts are not independent datasets and pooling would weight larger relevant sets more heavily.\n\nAn optional scores collection enables cutoff-tie diagnostics. Scores must be finite real values aligned with the complete ranking and sorted descending. Ties use exact Float64 equality: evaluation never rounds scores or changes order.","title":"Inputs and unit of evaluation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-metrics/#Composition-level-fields","page":"Ranking evaluation metric contract","text":"For a cutoff K, candidate count N, relevant set R and hits H_K:\n\nField Definition\nhits H_K, relevant compositions in the first K\nobserved_label_fraction H_K / K; not a synthesis-success rate\nrecall `H_K /\nenrichment `(H_K / K) / (\nrandom_expected_hits `K\nrandom_expected_hits_numerator, random_expected_hits_denominator Exact reduced fraction for the same expectation\ndcg Sum of 1/log2(rank+1) for relevant items at ranks at most K\nndcg Binary DCG divided by the ideal DCG for `min(K,\n\nBinary NDCG is a position-sensitive diagnostic, not a replacement for Hits@K. Every field treats the supplied relevance set as evaluator ground truth. In a PU experiment, unlabelled candidates can still be scientifically viable, so none of these fields is precision over confirmed negatives.","title":"Composition-level fields"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-metrics/#System-concentration-fields","page":"Ranking evaluation metric contract","text":"A chemical system is the unordered set of elements in a composition, ignoring stoichiometry. LiNaO and Li2NaO are distinct compositions in one system.\n\nField Definition\nselected_system_count Distinct systems represented in the first K\nrelevant_system_count Distinct systems represented by the complete relevant set\nhit_system_count Relevant systems with at least one composition hit by K\nsystem_recall hit_system_count / relevant_system_count\neffective_hit_systems H_K² / sum_s h_s², where h_s is the hit count in system s\nlargest_hit_system_fraction Largest h_s / H_K, or zero when there are no hits\n\nThe effective count is one when every hit comes from one system and reaches the ordinary hit-system count when hits are distributed evenly. These are clustering diagnostics. They do not claim that element-set diversity equals chemical, structural or functional diversity.","title":"System-concentration fields"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-metrics/#Cutoff-ties","page":"Ranking evaluation metric contract","text":"When scores are supplied, each row also reports:\n\ncutoff_score;\ncutoff_tie_size across the complete ranking;\ncutoff_tie_selected in the first K; and\ncutoff_tie_crosses, which is true when only part of the exact-score tie block is selected.\n\nThe metric function preserves the supplied order. The ranking producer remains responsible for a deterministic, score-independent tie rule. PU experiments use the versioned SHA-256 tie key and canonical formula fallback defined by their protocol.","title":"Cutoff ties"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-metrics/#Failure-behavior-and-compatibility","page":"Ranking evaluation metric contract","text":"Empty rankings/relevance, duplicate canonical formulas, relevance outside the ranking, invalid budgets, nonfinite or misaligned scores and scores that are not descending are errors. Inputs are not mutated.\n\nThis API is additive and experimental. It does not change pu_metrics, the v1 PU CLI or any frozen output schema. Existing frozen protocol documents and result bundles remain unchanged.\n\n","title":"Failure behavior and compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"#EkaCompositions","page":"Home","text":"Reading the gaps in the inorganic record.\n\nEkaCompositions ranks canonical chemical compositions and evaluates reproducible recovery experiments. It is for researchers testing stored scores and composition-level ranking methods, and it supports SQLite score queries and positive–unlabelled recovery benchmarks.\n\nThis site collects three kinds of material:\n\nGetting started — installing the package, running a query from Julia, and the shape of the command-line interface.\nAPI reference — every exported name, grouped by the stability tier it belongs to under the API contract.\nReference documents — the project's methodological corpus, reproduced from the repository's docs/ directory. These are the canonical records of what was run, under what protocol, and what the evidence showed.","title":"EkaCompositions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Orientation","page":"Home","text":"If you want to use the package, read Getting started and then the API reference.\n\nIf you want to reproduce or extend the science, start with the recovery findings for what has been established, then the recovery roadmap for what is sequenced next. Every experiment is governed by a frozen protocol; the protocol documents are content-hash-pinned and are never edited once a run has cited them.\n\nIf you want to contribute code, the repository's CONTRIBUTING.md covers setup, the test commands and the release gate, and API stability says what may change without a breaking release.","title":"Orientation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Data-and-licensing","page":"Home","text":"The package ships no real snapshot data. The bundled fixture is a twelve-row software-testing artefact, not a set of predictions; see third-party notices for its provenance. Materials Project terms, attribution and redistribution limits are recorded in the data provenance review and publication permissions.","title":"Data and licensing"},{"category":"section","location":"#Pipeline","page":"Home","text":"flowchart LR\n    A[make_system_snapshot.jl<br/>synthetic fixture] --> B[eka audit-mp<br/>verified groups]\n    B --> C[eka split-mp<br/>composition-safe splits]\n    C --> D[eka benchmark-pu<br/>rankings and metrics]\n    D --> E[analyze_pu_pilot.py<br/>independent analysis]\n\n","title":"Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-findings/#Recovery-findings:-completed-benchmark-series","page":"Recovery findings: completed benchmark series","text":"The completed series compares three simple references and one fixed-compute learned comparator on the same MP snapshot. All planned label policies and holdout designs are retained; model settings were frozen before its real fits.","title":"Recovery findings: completed benchmark series"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-findings/#All-methods-at-top-100","page":"Recovery findings: completed benchmark series","text":"Mean held-out experimental-provenance positives recovered across 20 splits. Random expectation uses each split's actual candidate prevalence. Within each row, all methods use exactly the same candidates and evaluation labels.\n\nHoldout Label policy Random observed Random expected Popularity Similarity Element-pair model\ncomposition original 11.55 13.03 12.90 13.40 12.70\ncomposition exclude_mixed 9.05 10.10 14.70 17.75 12.75\ncomposition unlabel_mixed 7.85 8.64 11.30 14.10 10.15\nsystem original 43.35 42.65 44.30 29.85 52.25\nsystem exclude_mixed 35.95 35.81 46.45 26.75 47.80\nsystem unlabel_mixed 32.70 32.00 40.15 24.95 42.30\n\nThe similarity baseline is also a leakage diagnostic. It is the strongest method under composition holdout, where candidates may share a chemical system with training, but the weakest method under system holdout: its 29.85, 26.75 and 24.95 mean hits are all below the corresponding random expectations of 42.65, 35.81 and 32.00. Maximum cosine similarity to training is therefore useful chiefly when system proximity is permitted; forbidding that proximity makes it anti-predictive in these system-holdout pools.\n\nRaw hit counts are not comparable between the two designs. Composition-holdout pools are 8.64–13.03% positive, whereas system-holdout pools are 32.00–42.65% positive. The favorable system-holdout hit totals therefore come from a much denser evaluation task and exceed random expectation by 22–33%, not by the raw difference between the designs' hit counts.\n\nUnder composition holdout the element-pair model trails popularity by 0.20, 1.95 and 1.15 mean hits across the three policies, and trails similarity by 0.70, 5.00 and 3.95. Under system holdout it leads popularity by 7.95, 1.35 and 2.15, and similarity by 22.40, 21.05 and 17.35. The smaller margins under the alternative label policies constrain any general claim about its benefit.\n\nThe original-label system advantage over popularity is positive in 13 splits, zero in two and negative in five. Excluding mixed labels gives 12 positive, two zero and six negative splits; unlabelling mixed groups gives 12 positive, one zero and seven negative splits. These overlapping splits describe sensitivity, not independent replications or statistical significance.\n\nThe composition-holdout subgroup diagnostic is negative for transfer to unseen systems. Only 16–22% of held-out positives are system-disjoint. Across all 20 overlapping splits, the fixed-compute model recovers 17, 24 and 22 such positives at top 100 under the original, exclude-mixed and unlabel-mixed policies, versus uniform-random expectations of 42.13, 45.04 and 38.53. These small event counts do not support precise ratio estimates or confidence intervals. They show no demonstrated ability to prioritize system-novel positives; the composition-holdout signal is concentrated among positives whose chemical system occurs in training.\n\nThe system-holdout top 100 also has a smaller effective decision count than its 100 rows imply because all stoichiometries in a system share a score. Under the original policy it contains a mean 13.5 distinct systems, only 9.0 systems supply any positive hit, and concentration of hits corresponds to 5.68 equally weighted systems by inverse Herfindahl index. The largest system supplies 27.9% of hits on average, and one system can occupy 52 of 100 rows. Exclude-mixed and unlabel-mixed top-100 lists average 14.55 and 13.20 systems and 7.34 and 6.58 effective hit systems. The 52.25 mean therefore describes clustered composition hits from a small number of system-level ranking decisions.","title":"All methods at top 100"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-findings/#What-the-series-establishes","page":"Recovery findings: completed benchmark series","text":"The original similarity comparator has a small composition-holdout advantage under the original label rule. Its magnitude depends on mixed-flag treatment, and its reversal to below-random performance under system holdout identifies shared-system proximity as the mechanism behind that advantage. The pair-factor model improves mean system-holdout results on denser pools, but those hits are clustered in a small number of system decisions. It does not improve the sparse composition comparison and shows no demonstrated recovery ability within that design's system-disjoint subgroup.\n\nThis task recovers withheld provenance labels from a single contemporaneous MP snapshot: it is partition reconstruction, not chronological discovery prediction. This is evidence about the specified recovery tasks and fixed configurations, not synthesis success, a universal ranking method, or a causal effect of chemical separation. System pools have different sizes/prevalence and can still contain close chemical analogues to training. Unlabelled does not mean failed synthesis. The MP experimental-provenance flag is a proxy; none of the alternative policies has been independently established as ground truth.","title":"What the series establishes"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-findings/#Model-and-numerical-limitations","page":"Recovery findings: completed benchmark series","text":"The model uses only non-O element-pair counts. All stoichiometries in a system tie; the predefined hash resolves their order. Missing pairs are weak zero-target associations, not verified-negative compounds. Cold elements receive explicit zero scores and are never silently dropped. Factors can reflect research frequency.\n\n101 of 120 real fits hit the 2,000-iteration cap; 19 met the declared stationarity criterion. This was anticipated in synthetic feasibility and frozen as a fixed-compute evaluation. The results do not establish converged or global optima. No hyperparameter, initialization, iteration checkpoint or favorable policy was selected after inspecting the learned results. These are not Seko model predictions.\n\nA post-hoc stability run changed only the cap from 2,000 to 20,000 iterations. All 120 fits then met the same stationarity criterion by iteration 11,309. None of the 19 already-converged top-100 lists changed, but 55 of the 101 previously capped lists changed, with as many as 48 of 100 compositions replaced. Top-100 hit deltas averaged +0.16 and ranged from -5 to +4, so aggregate means were comparatively stable while individual recommendations were compute-budget-sensitive. The converged sensitivity recovered 18, 26 and 23 system-disjoint composition-holdout positives across the three policies; this does not change the negative subgroup interpretation above. The prospectively frozen 2,000-iteration result remains the primary result; the later run characterizes its stability rather than replacing it. The exact runner, independent analyzer, synthetic corruption tests and local hashed-evidence boundary are documented in the element-pair execution record.","title":"Model and numerical limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-findings/#Reproduction-and-artifact-boundaries","page":"Recovery findings: completed benchmark series","text":"The original pilot, both sensitivity modes and system-holdout evidence remain unchanged. Composition reference controls match the saved full-pipeline sensitivity. The learned runner pins v2's config, verifies its inventory and source, and reconstructs all memberships before fitting. Alternative policies with identical training sets reproduce identical factor matrices and optimizer traces.\n\nLocal validation passed 4,583 Julia checks and 36 Python tests after the audit hardening compatibility check. The independent learned analyzer reconstructs membership, pair counts, metrics and coverage, recomputes every score and the final objective/gradient from factors, and rejects rehashed corruption. Complete refitting reproduction and inventories are recorded with the local evidence; do not substitute saved-score checks for that refit.\n\nSee pilot reproduction, label sensitivity, system holdout, and element-pair execution/evidence. Those guides identify the frozen protocols and local restore instructions. The tracked package includes source, synthetic workflows, methods and this attributed aggregate summary. Record-level data, factors, rankings and environments remain local; public exact real reproduction requires separately available input artifacts. No data bundle, new API query or provider message was sent.","title":"Reproduction and artifact boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-findings/#Attribution-and-handling","page":"Recovery findings: completed benchmark series","text":"Data source: Materials Project, database version 2026.04.13, API snapshot retrieved 31 August 2026 at 12:16:39 UTC. Eka normalized and grouped compositions, applied the declared label policies, constructed the holdouts and computed scores and metrics; GNoME was excluded. These analyses do not imply provider endorsement. The supplied terms review records the CC BY 4.0 basis for covered content and its provenance limits. Original Eka code is MIT; third-party notices remain separate.\n\nReference: A. Jain et al., “Commentary: The Materials Project: A materials genome approach to accelerating materials innovation,” APL Materials 1, 011002 (2013), doi:10.1063/1.4812323. The learned-method references and exact assumptions are in its specification.","title":"Attribution and handling"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/recovery-findings/#Reassessment","page":"Recovery findings: completed benchmark series","text":"The planned benchmark series is complete locally. Do not start a larger or tuned model merely to improve the table. The useful next commitment is review of these findings and the permitted sharing package. Any convergence study, stoichiometric extension, tuned model or literature-label audit should begin with a separate question and prospective protocol. Full Seko reproduction and production deployment remain outside this work.\n\nAny future rank or initialization study must be prospectively framed as stability characterization, predeclare its ranks, restarts and consensus metrics, report every run, and retain the frozen rank-4 fixed-compute result regardless of the sweep.\n\nThe separate-checkout refit reproduced all 992 deterministic files, the complete configuration and all six analysis files exactly. Julia tests also passed in that checkout. The captured Manifest was unchanged. This is same-platform reproduction using an existing depot, not an empty-depot or cross-platform claim.\n\nMeasured across the real run, fitting totaled 18.71 seconds and ranking 0.83 seconds (excluding input verification, file output, analysis, process startup and reproduction). These are measured computation costs, not a recorded total of implementation effort or a commitment to a larger model-development schedule.\n\n","title":"Reassessment"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-bundle-schema/#Evaluation-bundle-schema","page":"Evaluation bundle schema","text":"Status: internal research schema, version 1. It is additive and does not change any frozen schema-v1 recovery, label-sensitivity, system-holdout or element-pair bundle.\n\nThe EkaCompositions.Research.EvaluationSuite module writes and validates a portable directory with this shape:\n\nrun.toml\ninventory.toml\nrankings.tsv\nmetrics.tsv\nruntime.tsv                 optional; excluded from deterministic identity\nprovenance/...              exact captured inputs, code, locks and protocol\nmemberships/...             exact split memberships","title":"Evaluation bundle schema"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-bundle-schema/#Identities","page":"Evaluation bundle schema","text":"run_id is SHA-256 over a sorted canonical TOML representation of the complete scientific table. It deliberately excludes output paths, wall-clock timestamps, host/runtime measurements and the descriptive execution environment. The table binds:\n\ndataset identity, version, retrieval metadata and input hashes;\naudit algorithm and implementation hashes;\ncode commit, dirty flag, source-tree digest, relevant source hashes and dependency-lock hashes;\nprotocol ID, protocol bytes and hash;\nsplit algorithm, label policy and membership hashes;\ncandidate definition and exclusion counts;\nmethod configurations and the role of every seed;\nmetric algorithm/version, ordered budgets, grouping rule and denominators.\n\ninventory_sha256 hashes inventory.toml. That inventory binds every deterministic output and every declared provenance artifact. runtime.tsv, when present, is named under excluded_files and is never part of either identity. This means two same-environment reruns may have different timings while retaining the same run and deterministic-inventory identities.\n\nInternal hashes detect ordinary corruption. They cannot establish historical identity if an editor rewrites a file and every hash that points to it. A report or release record must therefore preserve at least the externally published run_id; preserving inventory_sha256 additionally fixes non-scientific output diagnostics such as coverage strings. validate_bundle accepts both expected values for this purpose.","title":"Identities"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-bundle-schema/#Output-tables","page":"Evaluation bundle schema","text":"rankings.tsv contains one complete, canonical composition ranking for every (split_id, method) pair. Its fixed columns are:\n\nsplit_id method rank composition score tie_key coverage observed_relevant\n\nThe file is UTF-8 tab-separated text with LF endings and a trailing LF. Ranks are one-based and contiguous. Formula strings must already be canonical. A ranking has either finite numeric scores for every candidate or an empty score for every candidate. Numeric scores are nonincreasing. The tie key is a lowercase SHA-256 digest. All methods in one split must receive the same candidate and relevant sets.\n\nmetrics.tsv prepends split_id and method to every field documented in Evaluation metric semantics. The writer ignores caller-supplied metric values and computes them from the saved ranking and evaluator-owned relevance membership. The validator independently rebuilds the entire metrics file byte for byte from rankings.tsv; missing, extra, reordered, wrong-typed or edited rows fail validation.","title":"Output tables"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-bundle-schema/#Failure-behavior","page":"Evaluation bundle schema","text":"Schema tables use exact key sets. Missing and extra keys, wrong types, unsafe relative paths, undeclared artifacts, duplicate artifact declarations, hash mismatches, incomplete rankings and unknown methods are errors. The writer:\n\nvalidates every in-memory input before reserving the destination;\nrefuses an existing file, directory or dangling symlink;\nrequires the parent directory to exist;\nreserves the output with mkdir; and\nremoves only that newly reserved directory if a later write fails.\n\nThe module has no CLI entry point and is internal pending a prospectively frozen real-data protocol.\n\n","title":"Failure behavior"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout-protocol/#MP-chemical-system-holdout-protocol","page":"MP chemical-system holdout protocol","text":"Protocol ID: eka-mp-system-holdout-v2\n\nFrozen: 31 August 2026, after the original pilot and label-sensitivity results, before inspecting system-holdout rankings. Prospective follow-on design; not a preregistration preceding the original pilot. The v1 and sensitivity protocols, inputs and preserved outputs remain unchanged.","title":"MP chemical-system holdout protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout-protocol/#Question-and-scope","page":"MP chemical-system holdout protocol","text":"How does similarity-minus-popularity recovery change when candidate element combinations have no training positives? The comparison is between two complete sampling protocols, not a causal estimate of chemical separation. Chemical systems can still be close analogues: BaTiO3 and SrTiO3 have different systems. Similarity's advantage may shrink, persist, disappear or reverse; no outcome is preferred. Unlabelled compositions are not known failed syntheses.\n\nUse the same verified MP snapshot/audit and oxygen-containing ternary scope as v1, including its source hashes, canonical formulas, unresolved exclusions and GNoME exclusion. No new API query, external scores, energy filters, learned model, temporal claim or manual relabelling. Carry original, exclude_mixed and unlabel_mixed from the frozen label-sensitivity protocol into both designs. Use full-pipeline labels, never evaluation-only relabelling with old training.","title":"Question and scope"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout-protocol/#Exact-system-and-membership-rules","page":"MP chemical-system holdout protocol","text":"A system is the ASCII-lexicographically sorted set of distinct element symbols in an Eka canonical composition, joined with -. Stoichiometry does not affect system identity. Reordered and proportionally equivalent formulas have the same system; canonical duplicate composition groups are errors. Only three-element systems containing O are eligible.\n\nConstruct a single common system universe S from the original positive and unlabelled groups, before policy filtering. Original unresolved groups never contribute to S or to any training/candidate population. This shared universe and all system assignments are identical across policies, including systems that become empty after excluding mixed groups.\n\nFor each split seed s=0,...,19, sort S by the tuple:\n\n(hex(SHA256(\"eka-pu-system-split-v2\\n\" + decimal(s) + \"\\n\" + system)), system).\n\nSelect the first floor(|S|/5) systems for candidates; the rest are training-side systems. Selection is uniform by system under the hash ordering, not balanced by composition count, positive count or outcome. Large systems are neither split nor downweighted. No rejection sampling, seed replacement, class balancing or post-result subset selection is allowed.\n\nApply each label policy before constructing memberships:\n\noriginal: original positives and unlabelled groups.\nexclude_mixed: remove groups with both explicit experimental and theoretical records; retain remaining positives and original unlabelled groups.\nunlabel_mixed: those mixed groups become unlabelled, never training positives.\n\nFor system holdout, training contains only policy positives outside selected systems. Held-out positives are all policy positives in selected systems. Candidates contain those holdouts plus all policy unlabelled groups in selected systems. Unlabelled groups outside selected systems are omitted entirely, not used for training or candidate evaluation. Excluded/unresolved groups are absent. A selected system can have only unlabelled candidates, only positive candidates, or no remaining rows under a policy; report these cases rather than reselection. Training and candidate system sets must be disjoint. Both alternative policies share exactly their training and held-out positives; their candidate difference is the mixed groups in selected systems.\n\nComposition-holdout controls use the frozen v1 formula-hash selection separately under each policy: floor(P_policy/5) held-out positives, remaining positives in training, all policy unlabelled groups in candidates. These controls must match the saved full-pipeline sensitivity memberships, rankings and metrics exactly. They do not share a candidate population with system holdout.\n\nBefore any real v2 scoring, check every branch/seed has at least one training positive, one held-out positive, one unlabelled candidate, at least one selected and one nonselected system, and candidate count >=200. All configured budgets must be feasible. If any branch fails, stop the entire experiment and report the preflight failure; amend/version a new design before scoring, never silently omit branches or change selection. Synthetic tests may specify smaller budgets and seed sets; all other rules are identical and explicitly marked synthetic.","title":"Exact system and membership rules"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout-protocol/#Scoring-and-evaluation","page":"MP chemical-system holdout protocol","text":"Use unchanged v1 random, training-only popularity, and maximum training composition similarity. Ranking seed is 10000+s; tie seed is 20260901; preserve v1 hash domains, Float64 operations, formula tie fallbacks and metric definitions. Rankers receive training/candidate compositions only, with labels attached after ranking. No cache from a different training set. Exclusion/unlabelling branches must have equal scores and relative orders on their shared candidates.\n\nBudgets: 20,50,100,200. For actual candidate count N and held-out-positive count h, report H@k, H@k/k, H@k/h, enrichment=(H@k/k)/(h/N), uniform-random expected hits k*h/N, and the declared random realization. Primary within-branch effect is Ds=Hsimilarity@100-Hpopularity@100. Report every Ds, mean, median, range and positive/zero/negative counts, plus all secondary budgets and per-method means.\n\nFor each policy report the system-minus-composition change in mean D and the seed-aligned D differences, without treating aligned seed numbers as matched samples. Do not interpret raw hit changes alone as the effect of separation. There are two designs × three policies × twenty seeds × three methods × four budgets = 1,440 metric rows. Every branch is required.","title":"Scoring and evaluation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout-protocol/#Population-and-similarity-diagnostics","page":"MP chemical-system holdout protocol","text":"For every design/policy/seed report training-positive count, candidate count, held-out-positive count, unlabelled candidate count, prevalence, excluded groups, and unused unlabelled groups. Report common-universe system count, selected system count (system design only), observed training/candidate/positive system counts, their overlap, selected empty systems and mixed training/candidate counts.\n\nReport positive concentration separately in training and holdouts: the fraction in the largest system and in the five largest systems (or all if fewer than five). Report these counts/fractions for both designs; unequal system sizes and candidate prevalence are part of the protocol difference.\n\nUse maximum cosine similarity to training positives in the existing elemental count-vector representation (equivalently normalized atomic fractions). This is exactly the unchanged similarity scorer, not a learned chemical distance. For all candidates, held-out positives and unlabelled candidates separately, save every maximum and report n, mean, min, p10, median, p90 and max. Quantiles use the sorted nearest-rank rule at index max(1,ceil(q*n)); mean uses formula-order Float64 accumulation. Report the fraction >=0.9 and >=0.99; these thresholds are descriptive choices, not proof of near-duplicate chemistry. Label-specific summaries are evaluator diagnostics only and cannot change scores/membership.\n\nNo matched-size control is included. Therefore no causal claim separating system disjointness from training size, pool size, prevalence or composition changes is permitted. Overlapping splits describe split sensitivity, not independent replications. No confidence intervals, significance tests, tuning or post-result threshold changes. No inference of synthesizability or universal out-of-distribution generalization from this elemental representation.","title":"Population and similarity diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout-protocol/#Validation,-freeze-and-reproduction","page":"MP chemical-system holdout protocol","text":"Register this protocol by ID and exact SHA-256 without changing earlier pins. Preserve its bytes/hash before real scoring and record preflight populations separately. Capture complete implementation, Project/Manifest, Julia version, input hashes, baseline identity and the exact source archive before evaluation. Validate source by the existing reaudit/hash checks. Validate the saved baseline file inventory and independently reconstruct controls; compare every control membership, ranking field and metric with saved full-pipeline sensitivity.\n\nTests must cover grouping invariance, unequal system sizes, policy alignment, excluded/mixed/unused groups, unlabelled-only systems, infeasibility, deterministic order, composition/system separation, ranking label isolation, common-candidate score invariance, protocol tampering and no-overwrite behavior. Independently check saved membership/metrics and population/diagnostic calculations; corruption must fail even if a file's manifest checksum is rewritten.\n\nWrite only to new directories. Record every ranking and membership, config, source/implementation hashes and deterministic output inventory. Keep wall-clock timings separate. Recompute all branches from the captured environment in a separate checkout and compare deterministic outputs exactly. Do not edit prior sealed evidence. Data remain local during this experiment; the separate terms review governs any later release. Report any implementation correction before rerunning; changes to this design require a new protocol identity.\n\n","title":"Validation, freeze and reproduction"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#Eka-Materials-Selection:-MVP-to-First-User","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Revised 13 October 2026 following Joshua's clarification. This replaces the 8 October pilot-first sequence, retained in Git at 2ea4459. Its recruitment, reviewer, budget, and benchmark targets are not current prerequisites. The first three tasks are complete: demonstration briefs, source inventory, and the isolated evidence/storage service. The code currently uses synthetic fixtures; recommendations and the interface are not built.","title":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#Direction","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Build a quick, inspectable materials reference for engineering firms, consultants, and materials specialists. An engineer describes a design problem; Eka proposes existing materials, explains their pros and cons, and links to the evidence and sourcing options. Other engineering agents can use the same service.\n\nJoshua is the sole builder. The immediate objective is a working MVP to show a potential first user. Engineer feedback will help determine the final product form. A funded pilot, hired reviewer, interviews, and a fixed industry niche are not entry requirements.\n\nThe long-term ambition is broad search across known materials. The MVP will disclose the sources and material classes actually searched. A limited catalogue cannot support a claim of searching every known material, and an empty result cannot establish that no suitable material exists.\n\nOne-page charter\nDecision log\nLocal task board and agent instructions\nDetailed task specifications","title":"Direction"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#Phase-0-decisions","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Original item Current disposition Evidence or next action\nP0.1 Charter Done Charter records the user's direction and open product decisions\nP0.2 Pilot budget Deferred Track MVP costs; consider paid data/hosting when a specific need appears\nP0.3 Named technical reviewer Deferred Joshua builds the prototype; first engineer feedback precedes a formal review programme\nP0.4 Commit and branch Done Existing work saved in 2ea4459; branch is codex/materials-selection-mvp\nP0.5 Task board Done AGENTS.md tracks all 14 tasks locally; linked backlog defines artifacts and completion criteria. Linear is not required\nP0.6 Decision log Done Accepted decisions, proposals, and hypotheses are recorded separately","title":"Phase 0 decisions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#Build-sequence","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Stage Result Issue IDs\n1. Define a small demonstration Three example briefs and a usable source set MVP-01, MVP-02\n2. Establish the evidence model Distinct grades, comparable properties, exact citations MVP-03, MVP-04\n3. Make comparisons work Constraint checks, tradeoffs, and sourcing links MVP-05, MVP-06\n4. Add the agent workflow Brief interpretation and evidence-backed reports MVP-07, MVP-08\n5. Make it usable Small comparison interface and MCP access MVP-09, MVP-10\n6. Prepare a first-user demo Reliability checks and reproducible setup MVP-11, MVP-12\n7. Learn from an engineer One observed real task and prioritized corrections MVP-13, MVP-14\n\nThese are repository-local task identifiers. Joshua owns all work; Codex can assist with implementation and board maintenance. Completion criteria and artifact targets are in the backlog. The task board records actual progress; this roadmap does not imply the full product already exists.","title":"Build sequence"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#1.-Define-a-small-demonstration","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Write three illustrative engineering briefs. They can be self-authored and must be labelled as demonstrations, not customer research or an engineering benchmark. Choose them against available evidence so they are feasible to implement.\n\nPossible examples are a lightweight bracket, a temperature-exposed enclosure, and an electrically insulating component that must transfer heat. These are proposals, not commitments to particular applications or materials. Include incomplete requirements and competing priorities.\n\nKeep the input model general. If practical, cover more than one material family; do not force breadth when reliable comparisons are missing. The initial inventory now identifies four reference candidates; the former 15-30-grade estimate is not a starting quota. No manufacturer records are approved for reuse yet.\n\nBuild a source register: manufacturer, document URL, grade coverage, revision and access date, acquisition method, storage/display permissions, and gaps. Use accessible, permitted sources initially. Public visibility does not establish republication rights. Synthetic records can unblock implementation while real data access is unresolved.\n\nDone when: three briefs and a small source set support an end-to-end demo. No recruited engineer is needed to start.","title":"1. Define a small demonstration"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#2.-Establish-the-evidence-model","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Use separate identifiers for material families, manufacturer grades, product forms, observations, sources, and supplier listings. Composition is optional metadata; it does not uniquely identify a commercial grade.\n\nEach observation needs its value/range, units, applicable conditions, test method where stated, typical versus specified-limit status, evidence type, source revision, and exact page/table or other location. Preserve missing facts and conflicting observations. Do not merge different grades or conditions.\n\nManually check the initial decisive observations. Automated extraction can propose entries, but ambiguous headers, units, and footnotes need resolution before the values qualify a candidate. Publish a versioned catalogue.\n\nKeep the Julia research package and frozen protocols intact. The new service lives under services/materials-selection/. MVP-03 established Python, Pydantic, Pint, and SQLite; the MCP adapter is later work. Do not introduce a Julia/Python runtime bridge unless a specific reusable capability justifies it.\n\nDone when: each decisive demo value resolves to a grade, condition, and source; unchanged inputs reproduce the catalogue identity.","title":"2. Establish the evidence model"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#3.-Make-comparisons-work","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Implement grade/alias lookup and structured property filters first. Evaluate hard requirements before preferences. Each constraint returns pass, fail, unknown, or conflicting evidence with the observations and rule used.\n\nCheck units, temperature, processing state, direction, and evidence requirements before comparing values. A typical value cannot establish a guaranteed minimum. Unsupported extrapolation and missing conditions remain unknown.\n\nShow alternatives and the reasons to choose or reject them. Expose where improving one preference sacrifices another. Use visible weights only when useful; never let a preference score cancel a failed hard constraint. Separate provisional candidates from supported matches.\n\nLink exact grades/products to manufacturers or suppliers. Show quantity, region, price basis, availability, and date only when documented. A product page supports a sourcing route; it does not prove stock or volume capacity. Accept geographic preferences without imposing UK/EU as the product's market. Supplier outreach, quotations, and purchasing remain outside this MVP.\n\nDone when: structured requests produce reproducible comparisons and sourcing links or explicit sourcing gaps without an LLM.","title":"3. Make comparisons work"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#4.-Add-the-agent-workflow","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Translate a brief into function, environment, geometry, manufacturing, supplied limits, preferences, and unknowns. Separate user statements from model suggestions and derived requirements. Ask questions likely to change the comparison.\n\nInitially collect and clarify engineering limits rather than inferring detailed physics from vague descriptions. Add a numerical derivation only when its formula, inputs, units, and validity assumptions are explicit and tested. Let the engineer correct the proposed specification.\n\nHave the model call the comparison engine and explain its outputs. The evidence store supplies values; deterministic rules perform numerical checks. Retrieved content is data, not instructions. A multi-agent scientist or autonomous research loop is not necessary for the first version.\n\nReturn roughly three to five options when supported, with advantages, disadvantages, decisive evidence, unknowns, and sourcing links. Return fewer or an insufficient-evidence result when appropriate. Include coverage and assumptions that could change the answer.\n\nDone when: a demo brief can be clarified, searched, and explained with every decisive numerical claim grounded in a returned observation.","title":"4. Add the agent workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#5.-Make-it-usable","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Provide a small local interface: brief, editable requirements, comparison table, and source detail. Open directly into selection. Export the task, tradeoffs, sources, and catalogue version. This is a demonstration surface; the eventual product could become agent-first, browser-first, or both.\n\nExpose the same functions through MCP: validate requirements, search, inspect a grade, compare, and retrieve evidence/sourcing. Use an official SDK, pin a tested release, and start with local stdio. A host agent can prepare the specification without duplicating an internal model call. Handle malformed inputs, absent data, cancellation, and errors. Hosted access and accounts follow actual demand.\n\nDone when: the interface and an MCP client can produce the same comparison without manually repairing the data.","title":"5. Make it usable"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#6.-Prepare-the-first-user-demo","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Check identity, units, boundaries, wrong conditions, conflicting sources, hard failures masked by preferences, unknowns treated as passes, broken citations, and no-match cases. Include out-of-coverage questions and misleading instructions embedded in a source. These are software checks, not application approval.\n\nRun the three demonstration briefs end to end. Record latency, model cost, failures, and manual interventions. Check source links and exports. Test setup from a clean environment with synthetic fixtures. Keep restricted data, private briefs, and credentials out of Git.\n\nPackage a runnable build, short setup instructions, and example tasks. A stable local demo that an engineer can inspect and challenge is enough; public launch, a formal benchmark, and multiple recruited firms are not requirements.\n\nDone when: all three demos work, decisive claims are traceable, coverage is visible, and a first engineer can try the workflow.","title":"6. Prepare the first-user demo"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#7.-Learn-from-one-engineer","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Approach one engineer with the working MVP. Observe a real task and record useful suggestions, missing facts, disputed comparisons, and usability problems. Get permission before retaining or transmitting confidential project information.\n\nPrioritize corrections and another real use. Let experience determine initial applications, coverage, interface, and specialist-review needs. A demonstration does not establish demand, design qualification, or broad accuracy.\n\nA structured pilot, independent review, held-out evaluation, paid data access, hosting, and payment tests become relevant afterward when they answer observed questions. Keep final design decisions with the responsible engineer.","title":"7. Learn from one engineer"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#Deferred-work","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"Recruiting several firms or hiring a specialist before building.\nA formal pilot budget, payment targets, and a 20-task held-out benchmark.\nComplete database aggregation, exhaustive coverage claims, and broad crawling.\nGenerative material design and learned property prediction.\nAutomated qualification, supplier contact, and purchasing.\nEnterprise accounts, CAD/FEA integrations, and multi-tenant hosting.\n\nBasic source checks, correct comparisons, and honest unknowns belong in the MVP. The deferred work is a later product programme, not a condition of beginning.","title":"Deferred work"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials-selection-plan/#Next-step","page":"Eka Materials Selection: MVP to First User","text":"MVP-01 through MVP-03 are complete. Continue with MVP-05's deterministic comparison logic against synthetic fixtures, while MVP-04 establishes a permitted real-data route. The source inventory records the current rights and verification gaps; the service README documents the runnable foundation. Build one complete comparison before expanding. Re-estimate after that first path works. The earlier 12-16 week beta schedule is withdrawn because it assumed a different team and validation sequence.\n\n","title":"Next step"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout/#Chemical-system-holdout-workflow","page":"Chemical-system holdout workflow","text":"The prospective v2 protocol compares random composition holdout with whole-system holdout on the same MP snapshot, under all three full-pipeline label policies. It is a separately loaded research workflow; the original benchmark-pu CLI and ranking algorithms are unchanged.\n\nA system is a sorted set of element symbols, independent of stoichiometry. System holdout assigns a hash-selected fifth of the common system universe to candidates. All training positives lie outside those systems. Unlabelled groups outside the selected systems are omitted. This changes pool sizes and prevalence as well as system overlap; no causal claim isolates chemical separation.","title":"Chemical-system holdout workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout/#Running-against-the-frozen-inputs","page":"Chemical-system holdout workflow","text":"Use the saved full-pipeline sensitivity results, not evaluation-only metrics. All output directories must be new. No API request or credential is needed. From the repository root:\n\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. scripts/run_system_holdout.jl \\\n  data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot reports/local/mp-ternary-audit \\\n  reports/local/mp-label-sensitivity-v1-2026-08-31/results \\\n  /absolute/path/to/new-preflight --preflight\n\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. scripts/run_system_holdout.jl \\\n  data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot reports/local/mp-ternary-audit \\\n  reports/local/mp-label-sensitivity-v1-2026-08-31/results \\\n  /absolute/path/to/new-results\n\npython3 scripts/analyze_system_holdout.py \\\n  /absolute/path/to/new-results /absolute/path/to/new-analysis\n\nThe preflight checks all memberships and budget feasibility before any new ranking. The full run verifies the source by reaudit and frozen hashes, captures baseline files, reconstructs every composition control, and compares its membership, scores, order and metrics with the saved full-pipeline sensitivity. It also checks shared-candidate score/order invariance under the alternative label policies. Rankers receive only training/candidate compositions.\n\nOutputs contain 1,440 metric rows, 360 rankings, 120 population rows and 360 similarity summaries, with per-candidate maximum similarities. All seeds, methods, budgets and labels are retained; runtime is separate from deterministic artifacts. config.toml inventories captured source, implementation, baseline and result bytes. The independent Python analyzer reconstructs membership, random/popularity scores, ordering, metrics, positive-system concentration and similarity summary statistics. A separate complete Julia rerun checks full similarity-score reproduction.","title":"Running against the frozen inputs"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout/#Offline-verification","page":"Chemical-system holdout workflow","text":"julia --startup-file=no --project=. test/runtests.jl\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_system_holdout_analysis.py' -v\n\nThe Python tests create a synthetic snapshot with unequal system sizes, mixed labels and an unlabelled-only system, then execute the entire workflow. They reject corruption even when a result-file checksum is rewritten. Synthetic results are software tests, not scientific evidence. The generator is examples/mp_recovery/make_system_snapshot.jl; custom seeds/budgets are allowed only in explicitly synthetic runs.\n\nThe tracked protocol is immutable after its freeze; fixes to code must preserve its scientific contract and require revalidation/rerunning affected outputs. A design change needs a new protocol identity. Keep the original pilot and sensitivity evidence untouched. The separate terms review governs preparation of attributed results and any later data release.","title":"Offline verification"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-system-holdout/#Completed-evidence","page":"Chemical-system holdout workflow","text":"The frozen real run and complete separate-checkout reproduction are preserved under reports/local/mp-system-holdout-v2-2026-08-31/. Its REPORT.md links every primary split, all secondary budgets, method means and population/similarity diagnostics. verify_evidence.py verifies the local inventory and all 1,472 deterministic outputs, the complete config and seven analysis files against the rerun. Reproduction used the same platform and existing package depot; no cross-platform or empty-depot claim is made.\n\nAll 4,530 Julia checks and 26 Python checks passed before real scoring. The composition controls reproduce the earlier full-pipeline sensitivity results; the original sealed pilot and sensitivity inventories remain unchanged. At the completion of this system-holdout study, the learned comparator remained a specification/feasibility decision. The subsequent element-pair evaluation completed that gate and a separate frozen comparison; it does not alter this study or its original results.\n\n","title":"Completed evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting started","text":"EkaCompositions supports Julia 1.10 and newer. From a repository checkout:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate(); Pkg.precompile()'\n\nThe package requires no network access after installation. Queries, tests and the synthetic examples all run offline.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Compositions","page":"Getting started","text":"A Composition is a canonical reduced element ratio. Parsing normalises element order and divides out any common factor, so equality and hashing ignore both:\n\nusing EkaCompositions\n\ncomposition = Composition(\"Zn1Mg2\")\nformula(composition)\n\"Mg2Zn1\"\n\ncomposition == Composition(\"Mg2Zn1\")\ntrue\n\nComposition(\"Mg2Zn2\") == Composition(\"MgZn\")\ntrue\n\nThat normalisation is load-bearing for callers using compositions as dictionary keys, and it will not change during 0.1.x:\n\nlength(Set([Composition(\"MgZn\"), Composition(\"Zn2Mg2\")]))\n1\n\nFormulas use valid element symbols and positive integer amounts. Repeated symbols are combined and counts reduce to the simplest ratio. Parentheses, isotopes, charges, whitespace and fractional amounts are unsupported.","title":"Compositions"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Querying-a-score-database","page":"Getting started","text":"query_compositions is a read-only query over an existing SQLite score database. The examples below use the twelve-row fixture that ships with the repository for software testing — it is not a set of predictions.\n\nfixture = joinpath(pkgdir(EkaCompositions), \"test\", \"fixtures\", \"tiny_test.db\")\n\nresults = query_compositions(fixture; elements=[\"Al\", \"Si\", \"O\"], nary=[4], threshold=0.3)\n\nfor (composition, score) in results\n    println(formula(composition), \" => \", score)\nend\nAl2Ba2O7Si1 => 0.74232\nAl2O12Si3Zn3 => 0.4814\nAl1Li1O12Si5 => 0.41613\nAl2Ba3O14Si4 => 0.39355\nAl2O14Si4Sr3 => 0.34611\n\nInspect a database's schema with database_info, and audit every record in it with validate_database.","title":"Querying a score database"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Command-line","page":"Getting started","text":"The eka executable in bin/ exposes the same query path plus six subcommands. Running it with no subcommand performs a stored-score query:\n\njulia --project=. bin/eka -e Al Si O -n 4 -d test/fixtures/tiny_test.db\n\n# Composition, Score\nAl2Ba2O7Si1   0.74232\nAl2O12Si3Zn3   0.48140\nAl1Li1O12Si5   0.41613\nAl2Ba3O14Si4   0.39355\nAl2O14Si4Sr3   0.34611\n\nCommand Purpose Guide\neka -d PATH [options] Query and rank stored SQLite scores. Production validation\neka import Build a new query database from scored TSV records. Design notes\neka validate Audit every record in a SQLite database. Production validation\neka benchmark Evaluate binary-labelled rankings at fixed budgets. Benchmark contract\neka audit-mp Verify and group an MP-shaped snapshot. MP pilot\neka split-mp Generate deterministic PU splits. Split guide\neka benchmark-pu Verify splits and evaluate PU methods. PU evaluation\n\nAppend --help to any subcommand. A bare --help describes the query options.\n\nThe command-line surface is frozen for 0.1.x: the same arguments produce the same results and the same output format. The Julia functions behind it are not equally stable — see API stability.\n\n","title":"Command line"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair/#Element-pair-comparator:-execution-and-evidence","page":"Element-pair comparator: execution and evidence","text":"The feasibility specification, go decision and frozen evaluation protocol define this separate research workflow. The SQLite CLI and the original three-method evaluators are unchanged. No new Julia dependency was added.\n\nThe model fits a rank-four nonnegative factor matrix to training non-O element pair counts. Missing pairs receive the declared weak zero-target penalty; they are not verified-negative compounds. Every candidate receives an association score, with zero and an explicit flag for an unseen element. Scores cannot separate stoichiometries within a system. Optimization is deliberately capped at 2,000 iterations, with convergence and cap status reported separately.","title":"Element-pair comparator: execution and evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair/#Synthetic-demonstration-and-tests","page":"Element-pair comparator: execution and evidence","text":"julia --startup-file=no --project=. scripts/run_pair_feasibility.jl /absolute/path/to/new-demo\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. test/runtests.jl\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_element_pair_analysis.py' -v\n\nThe demonstration accepts only a new output path; it cannot read real input or label files. It includes a small artificial holdout and a generated 4,288-training, 9,293-candidate size check. Results are software evidence, not research outcomes. Tests check analytical/finite-difference gradients, nonnegative factors, deterministic fitting, cold coverage, ties, candidate/evaluation isolation, corrupted outputs and complete synthetic evaluation.","title":"Synthetic demonstration and tests"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair/#Frozen-real-evaluation","page":"Element-pair comparator: execution and evidence","text":"Use the exact original snapshot/audit and preserved v2 results. No API key or new API query is needed. Output directories must be new:\n\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. scripts/run_element_pair.jl \\\n  data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot reports/local/mp-ternary-audit \\\n  reports/local/mp-system-holdout-v2-2026-08-31/results \\\n  /absolute/path/to/new-pair-results\n\npython3 scripts/analyze_element_pair.py \\\n  /absolute/path/to/new-pair-results /absolute/path/to/new-pair-analysis\n\nThe runner pins the real v2 config hash, verifies its full file inventory and the original source, and reconstructs all memberships before fitting. Each of the 120 fits receives only training compositions and the fixed settings; no tuning or test-label selection occurs. The alternatives are refitted independently and must yield identical factors/traces and shared-candidate scores.\n\nOutputs include 480 metric rows, 120 rankings, pair counts, factors, optimizer traces, fit diagnostics, captured reference metrics and provenance. The independent analyzer recomputes every score and the final objective/projected-gradient residual from factors, reconstructs memberships and metrics, and checks coverage and ties. The full rerun independently refits every model; matching saved-factor arithmetic alone is not the refitting check.","title":"Frozen real evaluation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair/#Local-evidence-and-limits","page":"Element-pair comparator: execution and evidence","text":"reports/local/element-pair-evaluation-2026-08-31/ preserves design and implementation freezes, the source archive, exact Manifest, results, analysis, reproduction commands and comparison checks. REPORT.md links the complete findings; REPRODUCE.md explains restoration. The earlier evidence remains intact.\n\nThe tracked combined findings retain all four methods and both favorable and unfavorable comparisons. Real source records, rankings, factors and environments remain local. A public reader can run the synthetic workflow; exact real reproduction requires the retained snapshot and baseline artifacts, which are not distributed in this source repository.\n\nThe separate-checkout refit reproduced all 992 deterministic files, the complete configuration and all six analysis files exactly. Julia tests also passed in that checkout. The captured Manifest was unchanged. This is same-platform reproduction using an existing depot, not an empty-depot or cross-platform claim.","title":"Local evidence and limits"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-element-pair/#Post-hoc-diagnostics-and-stability","page":"Element-pair comparator: execution and evidence","text":"The later subgroup, system-concentration and 20,000-iteration diagnostics are reproducible from the retained primary run and one new stability run. The stability runner permits only the declared cap change; it is not a general hyperparameter interface. From the repository root, with the original local snapshot, audit, v2 baseline and primary pair run present, use new output paths:\n\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. scripts/run_element_pair_stability.jl \\\n  data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot reports/local/mp-ternary-audit \\\n  reports/local/mp-system-holdout-v2-2026-08-31/results \\\n  /absolute/path/to/new-stability-results\n\npython3 scripts/analyze_element_pair_stability.py \\\n  reports/local/element-pair-evaluation-2026-08-31/results \\\n  /absolute/path/to/new-stability-results \\\n  /absolute/path/to/new-stability-analysis\n\nThe analyzer independently validates both complete runs, reconstructs every membership and ranking, writes per-split and aggregate diagnostics, and hashes its deterministic output inventory. Synthetic coverage is available with --synthetic on both Julia runners and is exercised by test/test_element_pair_stability_analysis.py. The retained real evidence is reports/local/element-pair-diagnostics-stability-2026-09-07/; it remains ignored and is not part of the source release.\n\n","title":"Post-hoc diagnostics and stability"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot/#Materials-Project-recovery-pilot:-data-audit","page":"Materials Project recovery pilot: data audit","text":"The pilot asks whether a ranking recovers held-out compositions with experimental provenance within a declared pool. It does not label unexplored compositions as failed experiments. This milestone prepares and audits a snapshot; it does not itself generate train/test splits, run PU recovery metrics, fit a model, or establish scientific performance. The separate Day 2 split generator now creates verified, composition-safe membership without ranking.","title":"Materials Project recovery pilot: data audit"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot/#Scope-and-decisions","page":"Materials Project recovery pilot: data audit","text":"The first audit queries non-deprecated MP materials containing O and exactly two other distinct elements, with include_gnome=False. This is an oxygen-containing ternary pool, not a validated oxide classification. It may include hydroxides, oxyhalides, salts, and compounds with unusual oxygen states. Review the chemical systems in the audit before freezing a narrower oxide rule. Do not choose that rule after inspecting which model wins.\n\nUse MP's existing composition pool for the first feasibility check. This avoids introducing a candidate enumeration scheme at the same time, but limits eventual conclusions to MP-covered chemistry. There is no energy/stability threshold and no filter on theoretical status: both experimentally referenced and other records must be available for grouping.\n\nThe query uses MP's official client. The exporter records the actual database version before and after querying and rejects an export spanning a release change. It cannot request arbitrary historical releases or reconstruct discovery years. A snapshot and its hashes are how we freeze this particular export.","title":"Scope and decisions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot/#Obtain-a-local-snapshot","page":"Materials Project recovery pilot: data audit","text":"Requires Julia as usual and Python 3.11+ for the optional exporter/tests. Install the MP client in an isolated environment; it is not a Julia package dependency:\n\ncd /path/to/EkaCompositions.jl\npython3 -m venv .venv-mp\n. .venv-mp/bin/activate\npython -m pip install -r scripts/requirements-mp.txt\nmkdir -p data/local reports/local\npython scripts/export_mp_pilot.py --prompt-key --output data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot\n\nGet an API key from your Materials Project account. --prompt-key reads it without echoing or saving it, and requires a real terminal. Alternatively configure MP_API_KEY locally; do not paste it into chat, scripts, commits, or shell history. The key is never included in snapshot metadata or error messages. The exporter does not silently fall back to including GNoME if a client rejects the exclusion option; use a client supporting include_gnome.\n\nIf export fails, it prints the failing stage (client initialization, version check, query, or snapshot validation). Errors raised by the exporter include a specific validation reason and record number where relevant. Other exceptions show only their type and code locations, never the external exception text, request headers, URLs, or local variable values. Share that diagnostic block to investigate; do not share the key or a raw traceback from the client. An error type such as ValueError alone does not establish that your key is invalid.\n\nThe output directory must be new and its parent must exist. Returned IDs must be unique. All result pages are requested. Selected source records are retained in records.jsonl; these are decoded API dictionaries, not original HTTP bytes. records.tsv contains normalized inputs for Julia and snapshot.toml records parameters, counts, versions, retrieval time, and SHA-256 hashes. Retain all three.\n\nOrdinary failures clean up only the newly created output directory. Abrupt process termination may leave a partial directory; hashes and required-file checks prevent silently auditing it as a complete snapshot.","title":"Obtain a local snapshot"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot/#Audit-the-snapshot","page":"Materials Project recovery pilot: data audit","text":"julia --project=. bin/eka audit-mp \\\n  --snapshot data/local/mp-ternary-snapshot \\\n  --output reports/local/mp-ternary-audit\n\nThe audit writes:\n\nFile Purpose\naudit.md Readable counts, definitions, and next decisions\naudit.toml Counts, exclusions by reason, and implementation/input hashes\ncompositions.tsv Grouped compositions, labels, chemical systems, counts, source material IDs\nexcluded.tsv Every excluded record and its reason\nsnapshot.toml Copy of original export metadata; keep the original snapshot alongside it\n\nExit 0 means the audit completed, not that the dataset is scientifically sufficient. An empty usable subset is reported, not hidden. The estimated 20% holdout size is floor(number_of_positive_compositions / 5); it is neither a split nor a power calculation. The has_minimum_pilot_data flag only means at least one positive could be held out and one unlabelled composition is available. It is deliberately not a scientific pass/fail threshold.\n\nThe library equivalent is audit_mp_snapshot(snapshot_directory, new_output_directory). No original files are modified. Existing reports are never overwritten.","title":"Audit the snapshot"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot/#Composition-and-provenance-rules","page":"Materials Project recovery pilot: data audit","text":"The exporter builds simple formulas from MP's composition element-count mapping, not formula_pretty. This avoids display parentheses and preserves actual integer counts. Only exactly integral, finite, positive counts within the supported integer range are accepted; fractional occupancies are reported rather than rounded or rationalized. Julia then validates symbols, reduces ratios, and groups equivalent compositions. These restrictions can exclude legitimate chemistry and must be reported as coverage limitations.\n\nAfter grouping all valid records for a formula:\n\nRecord evidence Composition label\nAt least one explicit theoretical=false positive\nEvery record explicitly has theoretical=true unlabelled\nNo positive record and at least one missing flag unresolved\n\nThe positive label is an experimental-provenance proxy, not a manual literature verification or proof of synthesis under a specific condition. Polymorphs can have different flags, so a mixed true/false group is reported and labelled positive. Source IDs are retained, including a count of records without them. Missing flags are not converted to true. Unresolved groups must be excluded from the initial recovery pool unless their evidence is subsequently resolved.\n\nThe audit verifies the complete exporter metadata contract, both saved file hashes, and that every preserved JSONL record independently normalizes to the corresponding TSV row. This detects edits that update one file and its hash without making the preserved records and normalized inputs agree. It does not establish upstream authenticity or permission to redistribute the data.\n\nThe grouped file is intentionally not input to eka benchmark: that existing command has a binary-outcome contract. There is no outcome=0 for unlabelled here. The separately versioned PU recovery commands consume the audited groups while preserving this distinction; see PU evaluation.","title":"Composition and provenance rules"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot/#Decisions-after-the-real-audit","page":"Materials Project recovery pilot: data audit","text":"Verify applicable source/data terms, exclusion coverage, and provenance quality. GNoME exclusion is a query condition, not a certification that all remaining records have identical redistribution rights. Snapshots start unreviewed and stay out of Git under data/local/; reports under reports/local/ are ignored too.\nFreeze the chemistry definition, source snapshot, and candidate-generation rule.\nGroup compositions first; hold out a predetermined fraction of positives across predetermined seeds. Keep unresolved groups out. Never infer first-discovery dates from API creation/update timestamps.\nFit popularity and any trainable comparator on each training subset only. No training positives or equivalent polymorph compositions may appear in its test pool. Existing precomputed Seko scores are excluded from the primary comparison; the external score review records the coverage evidence and the eligibility decision.\nReport observed-positive hits@k, held-out-positive recall@k, and observed-label enrichment with precise denominators. These do not estimate the true success rate of unlabelled chemistry. Compare methods on the same splits. Choose an uncertainty method appropriate to the split/sampling design; overlapping intervals do not establish that a method has no useful effect.\n\nNo win against a baseline is required for the pilot to be informative. Too little data, unresolved provenance, or an inconclusive difference are valid findings.","title":"Decisions after the real audit"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot/#Offline-verification","page":"Materials Project recovery pilot: data audit","text":"python3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_mp_export.py' -v\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\nExporter tests cover exact count normalization, missing provenance, query filters, release changes, hashes, duplicate IDs, authentication prerequisites, and cleanup. Julia tests cover equivalent formulas, mixed/missing flags, scope exclusions, integrity failures, reproducible audits, and CLI behaviour. No tests need MP access. Live export compatibility and real data sufficiency must still be established with an authenticated run; mocked API tests cannot prove either.","title":"Offline verification"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pilot/#Sources-for-the-protocol","page":"Materials Project recovery pilot: data audit","text":"Official MP summary client: query fields and GNoME exclusion.\nMP provenance implementation: flags, source IDs, and timestamp semantics.\nMP release notes: version identification and GNoME-specific restrictions.\nMP API access: client and account setup.\nConfidence-interval comparison: limitations of judging significance from separate interval overlap.\n\n","title":"Sources for the protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Evaluation-roadmap:-rigorous-recovery,-controls-and-scaling","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Prepared 14 September 2026 from repository state 0629ba9d600afcd0cdbf1ddfc30ef83dbe6088c4. The working tree contained the untracked owner file PROJECT_BRIEF.md; it was read for context and not changed. This document proposes the next stage. It does not amend the four frozen protocols, reinterpret their results, or authorize a new real-data run.","title":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Executive-recommendation","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Do not add PyTorch now. Eka already has the important scientific foundation: composition-safe positive–unlabelled (PU) recovery, a whole-chemical-system holdout, three defensible references, a fixed element-pair factor comparator, label-sensitivity analysis, independent Python checks and exact local reruns. The highest-value next step is to make the evaluation layer reusable and system-aware, then add one direct observed-pair control and one prospectively specified missing-pair ablation.\n\nUse chemical-system holdout as the primary generalisation protocol. Retain composition holdout as an interpolation control, not as the headline discovery test. Keep composition Hits@100 as the primary operational outcome because it answers “how many known positive-provenance formulas are recovered in a fixed screening budget?” Add macro Recall@100 to expose changing holdout sizes and a system-level recovery diagnostic so that many stoichiometries from one element system cannot masquerade as many independent discoveries.\n\nThe most important limitation is upstream of modelling: this is retrospective recovery inside a fixed Materials Project (MP) catalogue. The positive label is an experimental-provenance proxy, the unlabelled class is not a negative class, and the candidate universe consists of already catalogued formulas. No model or metric extension can turn this dataset into evidence of synthesizability or prospective discovery.","title":"Executive recommendation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#A.-Current-state","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"","title":"A. Current state"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#A.1-Two-ranking-products-that-must-remain-distinct","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"The repository has two separate tasks.\n\nThe SQLite query path in src/database.jl retrieves stored composition/score rows subject to element, arity and score-threshold filters. rank! in src/ranking.jl orders those rows by either stored score or cosine similarity to one supplied reference. It does not fit a model or evaluate held-out recovery. Its generic tie rule is descending ranking value, descending stored score, then canonical formula.\nThe MP research path audits and groups a fixed MP snapshot, creates PU holdouts, fits or constructs training-only scorers, ranks a complete candidate pool and evaluates deliberately hidden positive-provenance compositions. This path has its own score-independent tie hash and does not inherit the SQLite stored-score tie breaker.\n\nThe public benchmark_rankings path in src/benchmark.jl is a third, narrower interface for an explicitly binary-labelled pool. It already reports fixed-budget hits, precision, recall, enrichment and simple coverage proxies. Its contract correctly warns that it is inappropriate when “0” merely means unobserved.","title":"A.1 Two ranking products that must remain distinct"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#A.2-Composition,-candidate-and-positive-definitions","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Composition in src/compositions.jl accepts simple formulas with valid element symbols and positive integer amounts. It combines repeated symbols, reduces the amounts by their greatest common divisor and alphabetically orders the symbols. Equality and hashing therefore collapse element permutations and proportional spellings such as MgZn, Zn2Mg2 and Mg2Zn2.\n\nFor the MP workflow, audit_mp_snapshot in src/mp_audit.jl first verifies the snapshot metadata and checks that records.jsonl normalises to records.tsv. It then groups every polymorph/record by canonical reduced composition. Within a group:\n\npositive means at least one record has theoretical=false;\nunlabelled means every record has theoretical=true;\nunresolved means no positive record exists and at least one flag is missing.\n\nThe fixed real snapshot records 24,020 MP records, 12,506 canonical compositions, 5,359 positives, 7,147 unlabelled compositions, no unresolved compositions and 1,343 mixed true/false groups. The scope is oxygen plus exactly two other elements; it is not an oxidation-state-validated oxide set. These counts are in reports/local/mp-ternary-audit/audit.toml and summarised in docs/recovery-findings.md.\n\nIn mp_recovery_splits (src/mp_recovery.jl), a composition-holdout candidate pool is the union of a hash-selected fifth of positive compositions and every eligible unlabelled composition. Training contains the remaining positives. For the original policy, each of the 20 real splits has 4,288 training positives, 1,071 held-out positives and 8,218 candidates.\n\nIn system_splits (src/mp_system_holdout.jl), a chemical system is the sorted set of element symbols, ignoring stoichiometry. A hash-selected fifth of the eligible system universe supplies candidates: all held-out positives and unlabelled formulas in those systems. Training positives come only from other systems. Depending on label policy and seed, the real system-holdout pools have 2,096–2,710 candidates and 740–1,171 held-out positives.\n\nThus a candidate is an eligible, already catalogued canonical composition in the evaluator-owned pool. It is not a formula newly generated by Eka. A recommendation is a candidate selected from the top of a complete deterministic ranking. A relevant item is a deliberately held-out positive-provenance composition under the active label policy. There are hundreds or thousands of relevant items per split, not one relevant item per query.","title":"A.2 Composition, candidate and positive definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#A.3-Filtering-and-label-isolation","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Filtering occurs before ranking and is explicit:\n\nthe API snapshot query requires oxygen, exactly three elements, non-deprecated records and include_gnome=false;\nthe audit excludes invalid/nonintegral formulas and anything outside scope;\nunresolved groups are outside training and evaluation;\nthe selected label policy either retains, excludes or unlabels mixed groups;\nsystem holdout keeps candidate formulas only in selected systems, so eligible unlabelled formulas in other systems are deliberately unused;\nno post-score threshold removes candidates, and budgets are rejected rather than truncated when K exceeds the pool;\nthe element-pair model retains cold candidates with score zero and an explicit coverage flag instead of dropping them.\n\npu_rank accepts training formulas, candidate formulas, method and seeds, but no labels, IDs or stored scores. pu_metrics receives held-out labels only after the ranking exists. Tests in test/test_mp_pu.jl, test/test_mp_label_sensitivity.jl and test/test_mp_system_holdout.jl check label isolation, refitting, membership disjointness, deterministic order and tamper rejection.","title":"A.3 Filtering and label isolation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#A.4-Methods-already-implemented","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"The PU workflow already includes:\n\na deterministic SHA-256 pseudorandom ranking plus an exact uniform-random expected hit count;\ntraining-element popularity, counting each element once per unique training composition;\nmaximum cosine similarity between a candidate's reduced stoichiometric vector and any training composition;\na rank-four symmetric nonnegative element-pair factor model in src/element_pair_model.jl.\n\nThe learned model constructs a 117×117 matrix over non-oxygen elements. Each training composition increments its unordered non-O pair. It fits nonnegative factors to log-scaled counts with weight 1 on observed pairs, weight 0.01 on unobserved pairs, L2 regularisation 0.01 and deterministic projected-gradient descent. Candidate score is the factor dot product for its non-O pair. All stoichiometries in the same chemical system therefore receive the same model score.\n\nThe model is not a Seko reproduction. Its missing-pair penalty is a disclosed implicit-feedback assumption, not a claim that missing compounds are negative. The primary 2,000-iteration study had 101 capped fits out of 120. A later 20,000-iteration stability run converged all fits; 55 formerly capped top-100 sets changed, with up to 48 replacements, although hit deltas averaged +0.16 and ranged from -5 to +4. The frozen 2,000-iteration result remains primary.","title":"A.4 Methods already implemented"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#A.5-Results-already-established","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"The completed comparisons make several proposed “future” tasks unnecessary:\n\nUnder original composition holdout at K=100, maximum similarity exceeded popularity by only 0.50 mean hits across 20 overlapping splits.\nUnder original system holdout, similarity trailed popularity by 14.45 mean hits at K=100.\nThe element-pair model was essentially tied with popularity under original composition holdout (-0.20 mean hits) and exceeded it under original system holdout (+7.95 mean hits).\nLabel-policy branches, all four budgets and unfavorable outcomes are retained.\nPost-hoc diagnostics show that the original system-holdout model's top 100 contained about 13.5 systems, about 9 positive-hit systems and only about 5.68 effective hit systems on average. Composition-level Hits@100 therefore does not represent 100 independent chemical decisions.\n\nThese are descriptive results over overlapping repeated holdouts. The repository correctly makes no confidence-interval, significance, synthesis-success or causal claim from them.","title":"A.5 Results already established"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#A.6-Validation-and-provenance-already-present","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"The project is unusually strong here. Frozen protocol documents are content-hash pinned in src/mp_recovery.jl. Split and run bundles retain source/input hashes, membership hashes, implementation hashes, configuration, seeds, Julia/package versions, complete rankings and metric denominators. Python analyzers independently reconstruct membership, baseline scores, ordering and metrics. Separate-checkout reruns reproduced deterministic outputs; runtime files are explicitly excluded from byte equality.\n\nReal snapshots, record-level rankings, factors and complete environments remain under ignored data/local/ and reports/local/ paths. This preserves source release hygiene but means a public checkout cannot exactly reproduce the real results without the separately retained inputs.","title":"A.6 Validation and provenance already present"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#B.-Scientific-task-definition","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"","title":"B. Scientific task definition"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#B.1-Prediction-task","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"For each outer split s, let:\n\nT_s be the canonical positive-provenance training compositions;\nC_s be the complete eligible candidate pool;\nR_s ⊂ C_s be the deliberately hidden positive-provenance compositions;\nf_m(c; T_s) be method m's score using only permitted training information;\nπ_ms be the deterministic complete ordering of C_s.\n\nThe task is: rank C_s so that members of R_s appear early. This is masked-entry recovery from a positive–unlabelled catalogue. It is not binary classification, candidate generation, crystal-structure prediction, stability prediction or prospective synthesis recommendation.\n\nThe relevant set is R_s. It is complete with respect to the positives deliberately masked by the split, but it is not complete with respect to all scientifically viable materials because the unlabelled pool can contain viable compounds.","title":"B.1 Prediction task"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#B.2-Natural-evaluation-unit","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"The natural statistical/reporting unit is an outer split and its ranked candidate pool. The ranked item is a canonical composition. There is no natural per-user or per-reference “query,” so query-averaged recommender metrics should not be imported by analogy.\n\nChemical system is an important secondary unit because formulas within a system are clustered and the element-pair model assigns them identical scores. Report both composition recovery and system recovery; do not silently replace one with the other because they answer different screening questions.","title":"B.2 Natural evaluation unit"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#B.3-Meaningful-budgets","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Retain K = 20, 50, 100 and 200 for continuity with every frozen study. K=100 is the primary fixed screening budget. The smaller and larger budgets describe the recovery curve and cutoff sensitivity. They are not percentiles: K=100 is about 1.2% of an original composition-holdout pool but roughly 3.7–4.8% of a system holdout pool. Consequently, compare methods within the same split first; comparisons between split designs must show pool size and prevalence.\n\nIf a system-ranked diagnostic is added, choose its system budgets from pre-score population counts and a stated experimental decision capacity, then freeze them. Do not choose them after viewing method performance.","title":"B.3 Meaningful budgets"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#C.-Gaps","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Metrics are embedded in workflow code. pu_metrics returns only the existing composition-level fixed-budget fields. There is no reusable relevance metric type, system-level evaluator or single schema shared by the binary and PU paths.\nClustered recovery is not a first-class metric. Effective/unique hit-system summaries exist only in a post-hoc Python diagnostic. A method can receive many composition hits from a few systems, especially when its score is constant within system.\nThe learned representation lacks its most direct control. The factor model is compared with element popularity and composition similarity, but not with the raw training pair count that it factorises. That control is necessary to show whether low-rank completion adds value beyond memorising observed pairs.\nThe missing-pair assumption is unablated. Weight 0.01 is a major modelling choice. Current code rejects zero, so an observed-pairs-only ablation would be a new, prospectively frozen experiment.\nOptimisation status complicates the headline model result. Most primary fits hit the 2,000-iteration cap, and many rankings changed at 20,000 iterations. The stability evidence is good, but any next model comparison should use a convergence rule or fixed-compute claim defined before scoring.\nThe primary split should be stated more strongly. Composition grouping blocks exact duplicates and polymorph leakage, but composition holdout permits same-system stoichiometries and close analogues in training. System holdout is the more defensible headline for transfer to unseen element combinations.\nNo protocol tests a genuinely new material or time period. The snapshot lacks defensible first-discovery timestamps, and all candidates already occur in MP. Chronological or prospective claims are unavailable.\nProvenance is comprehensive but not uniform. Core result configs do not consistently store a Git commit plus dirty-tree digest, Python environment, OS/architecture/CPU, thread counts or one common result schema. Source hashes are stronger than a commit alone, but restoration currently depends on experiment-specific packaging.\nPerformance evidence is split across scripts and reports. There are useful measurements but no isolated benchmark environment, scaling grid or end-to-end phase timings. Cumulative allocations are sometimes available; peak resident memory is not.\nPublic reproducibility is deliberately incomplete. Real data and detailed  outputs are local pending artifact review. Public claims must stay within the  aggregate evidence that can be shared and attributed.","title":"C. Gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#D.-Recommended-evaluation-design","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"","title":"D. Recommended evaluation design"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#D.1-Primary-protocol:-repeated-chemical-system-holdout","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Use the existing v2 definition as the foundation: hash-select whole canonical element sets, fit on positive compositions outside those systems, and rank all eligible held-out-positive plus unlabelled formulas inside the selected systems. This supports the narrow claim:\n\nrecovery of MP positive-provenance compositions when their exact element combination is absent from positive training data.\n\nIt prevents exact composition leakage, proportional/permuted formula leakage, polymorph leakage and same-element-system stoichiometric leakage. It does not prevent transfer through chemically analogous systems, periodic-family neighbours or each element's occurrence with other partners. That remaining information is intentional for the element-pair task and must be stated.\n\nRetain the 20 deterministic split seeds for continuity, but treat them as overlapping sensitivity analyses, not independent replicates. Report every split. Use macro means/medians across splits as the main aggregate so each split receives equal weight. A pooled micro recall can be supplied only as a labelled diagnostic; it weights splits with more held-out compositions more heavily and should not replace macro summaries.","title":"D.1 Primary protocol: repeated chemical-system holdout"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#D.2-Interpolation-control:-repeated-composition-holdout","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Retain the existing composition holdout as a secondary control. It simulates recovering missing formulas when other stoichiometries or formulas in the same element system may already be known. It is scientifically useful for database completion and for quantifying how much a method depends on local analogues. It does not support a claim of transfer to unseen chemistry.","title":"D.2 Interpolation control: repeated composition holdout"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#D.3-Optional-later-stress-test:-leave-one-element-out","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"If a cold-start question becomes important, prospectively define a leave-one-nonoxygen-element-out protocol over elements with adequate positive and unlabelled support. Training must exclude every formula containing the held-out element; candidates contain that element. This tests transfer to an unseen element, not merely an unseen pair. It is expected to expose the element-pair model's documented zero-score fallback and is valuable as a limitation test, not as a route to a favorable score.\n\nDo not call this “leave-one-chemical-family-out.” The repository has no validated chemical-family taxonomy. Do not add a chronological split until a source with defensible observation/discovery dates is obtained; MP snapshot or creation timestamps are not sufficient.","title":"D.3 Optional later stress test: leave-one-element-out"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#D.4-Leakage-map-and-split-local-recomputation","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Leakage path Current protection Remaining risk / required rule\nFormula order and overall scale Composition canonicalisation before grouping Keep canonicalisation deterministic and versioned.\nMultiple MP structures/polymorphs Group all records by composition before splitting Never sample records or weight by structure count.\nSame-system stoichiometries Prevented by primary system holdout Allowed only in the interpolation control and labelled as such.\nRelated periodic/chemical families Not prevented Requires a separately defined taxonomy or leave-element-out test.\nMixed theoretical flags Three existing label policies Keep policy-specific membership, fitting and denominators separate.\nSource-dataset duplicates Formula grouping collapses same formula; source IDs are not features Cross-source correlated families remain; a source holdout needs a defensible source definition.\nGlobal fitted statistics PU rankers and factor model refit per split Every new normaliser, vocabulary learned from data, sampler and cache must be fit inside the outer training split.\nCandidate/test labels in scoring Ranker signatures exclude labels; tests enforce isolation Candidate membership is evaluator constructed. Never expose held-out labels, provenance fields or full-data counts to fitting.\nCandidate filtering after scores No post-score filters now Freeze eligibility before scoring; invalid/excluded items are not denominator members and must remain counted in audit summaries.\nHyperparameter selection Current learned model was frozen without tuning Any tuning needs an inner training-only split matching the outer grouping; refit on all outer training data after selection.\nExternal stored scores Excluded because training independence is unknown Include only after score-training data and candidate coverage are proven independent.\n\nAudit-time syntax normalisation and global formula grouping may use the complete snapshot because they define immutable item identity and prevent duplicate leakage; they must not compute predictive statistics. Label-policy construction may inspect provenance because it belongs to the evaluator, but model inputs may contain only the resulting permitted training formulas.","title":"D.4 Leakage map and split-local recomputation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#D.5-Primary-metrics","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Use the smallest set that answers the screening question and keeps changing denominators visible.\n\nComposition Hits@100. Number of held-out positive-provenance formulas in the first 100. This is the primary operational measure. At a fixed screening budget it has a direct meaning and preserves all existing comparisons.\nComposition Recall@100. Hits@100 divided by the split's held-out positive count. This is necessary when system-holdout relevant-set sizes differ. Call it “held-out recovery recall,” not sensitivity to true viable materials.\nPaired ΔHits@100. Within each identical split/candidate pool, report the method-minus-popularity difference and method-minus-raw-pair-frequency difference. This is the primary comparative statistic rather than a standalone retrieval metric. Retain every split, mean, median, range and sign counts.\n\nAlways show N, |R|, prevalence and the analytical uniform-random expected hits beside these metrics. Precision@100 is algebraically Hits@100/100 and need not be a separate headline number; the current name observed_label_fraction is scientifically safer because unlabelled is not negative.","title":"D.5 Primary metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#D.6-Secondary-diagnostic-metrics","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Hits and recall at K = 20, 50 and 200 to show budget sensitivity.\nObserved-label enrichment over split prevalence. This helps compare pools with different prevalence but does not correct selection bias in the catalogue.\nDistinct positive systems@K, system recall@K (held-out systems with at least one recovered formula / held-out systems) and the already used effective hit-system count. These expose clustered hits without changing the composition screening budget.\nA separately ranked system diagnostic, using one prospectively defined aggregate score per system and deterministic system ties, only if the scientific decision is genuinely system-level.\nBinary NDCG@K as an optional position-sensitive diagnostic. With binary held-out relevance it asks whether recovered positives occur near the very top, and its ideal denominator is well defined per split. It should not replace Hits@K.\nScore coverage/cold-candidate count, distinct-score count, tie-block sizes and whether a tie crosses K.\nCandidate-to-training similarity distributions and the share of candidates sharing systems with training; these diagnose split difficulty rather than model quality.\nUnique selected systems, effective selected systems and element coverage for list-composition diagnostics. Call them diversity/coverage proxies, not chemical diversity or novelty.\n\nDo not add MRR: the first recovered item discards almost all information when hundreds of positives are relevant. Do not add hit rate: “at least one hit” will usually saturate at these budgets. Do not make MAP primary: there is one global ranking per split rather than many user/query rankings, and average precision implicitly gives meaning to the complete rank tail. Do not report exact-formula novelty because every held-out candidate is composition-disjoint by construction. NDCG and MAP also inherit the incomplete-ground-truth limitation of every PU metric; an unlabelled formula can be scientifically valuable while counted as nonrelevant.","title":"D.6 Secondary diagnostic metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#D.7-Ties,-duplicates-and-denominators","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Preserve exact score equality. Do not round or use tolerance bins before ordering.\nUse the existing score-independent SHA-256 tie key and canonical formula fallback for PU composition rankings. Record the tie seed and algorithm ID.\nIf system rankings are added, derive a separate versioned system tie key from the canonical system string. Do not let the number of formulas in a system decide ties accidentally.\nReject duplicate canonical candidates before scoring. Do not deduplicate a ranked output after the fact.\nDefine N and R after all protocol eligibility rules but before scoring. Invalid, out-of-scope, unresolved and policy-excluded groups are audit counts, not metric denominators. Under system holdout, eligible formulas in unselected systems are also outside that split's denominator.\nNever drop an unscorable candidate. Use an explicit fallback/coverage state and keep it in N, as the element-pair implementation already does.\nWhen exclusion changes eligibility, compact ranks before applying K and record the original rank depth, following the existing label-sensitivity implementation.","title":"D.7 Ties, duplicates and denominators"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#D.8-Baselines-and-ablations","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Retain random only as calibration: one deterministic hash ranking per split plus the analytical expectation. It is not evidence that a learned method is useful; the serious comparisons are below.\n\nComparator Scientific question Exact inputs and score Fit? Expected cost Evidence the factor model adds value\nTraining-element popularity (existing) Does the model beat historical marginal element frequency? Training formulas only; mean occurrence fraction of each candidate's three distinct elements. Count frequencies per split. O(Ta + Na + N log N), with arity a=3. Positive paired ΔHits@100 and system-recovery diagnostics across predeclared policies/splits, not just one favorable mean.\nMaximum composition cosine (existing) Is retrieval explained by proximity to one known stoichiometric analogue? Training and candidate reduced count vectors; maximum cosine to any training formula. Build training vectors per split; no learned parameters. O(NTa² + N log N), streamed. Under system holdout, the model should beat this without losing coverage; composition-holdout results diagnose interpolation.\nRaw observed-pair frequency (new direct control) Does low-rank completion add information beyond direct pair memorisation? Training formulas only; score candidate pair (a,b) by the same log1p(C_ab)/log1p(T) target used by the factor model. Zero for unseen pairs. Count pairs only. O(T + N + N log N); 117×117 counts. On system holdout every held-out pair should be unseen and tied at zero; improvement over this control isolates cross-pair factor generalisation. On composition holdout it tests whether factorisation beats raw frequency.\nObserved-pairs-only factor ablation (new, conditional) Are results driven by treating missing pairs as weak zero targets? Identical training formulas, rank, regularisation, initialisation and optimizer; set missing-pair weight to zero and still score all candidates. Yes, independently per split. Same asymptotic fit cost as the main model. Similar conclusions across the frozen grid support robustness to the PU assumption; material changes show the assumption is load-bearing and must temper claims.\n\nDo not ablate the oxygen-ternary scope or system filter: those define the population, not optional features. Treat the three mixed-label policies as label sensitivity, not model ablations. Defer rank sweeps; a sweep would be model selection or stability analysis and needs its own prospective ranks, restarts and consensus rule. The direct pair-frequency control is more informative first.","title":"D.8 Baselines and ablations"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#E.-PyTorch-decision","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Decision: defer.\n\nThe current learned problem has only 117 element rows and rank four. Reimplementing the same weighted symmetric factor objective in PyTorch would mainly compare an optimizer/autodiff stack with Julia's explicit projected-gradient implementation; it would not add a scientifically distinct predictor. The measured primary run spent about 18.71 seconds fitting all 120 branches and 0.83 seconds ranking them, so accelerator support is not needed. A neural collaborative-filtering scorer or MLP would require a new representation and a defensible PU loss/negative-sampling policy. Adding one merely to claim PyTorch would be disconnected from the current evidence.\n\nReconsider PyTorch only after both of these prerequisites hold:\n\nthe system-aware evaluation and raw-pair control identify a specific remaining error mode, such as inability to order stoichiometries within a system; and\na representation with information capable of addressing that error is available without test leakage—for example training-only composition descriptors or structure/property features with complete provenance.\n\nThe smallest credible later experiment would keep the exact outer splits, candidate universe, held-out positives, filters, tie policy and metric code. It would use one shallow model over the agreed representation, a prospectively defined PU objective, training-only negative/unlabelled sampling, deterministic CPU execution first, fixed seeds, inner grouped validation for early stopping and hyperparameters, and TSV rankings plus TOML/JSON provenance consumable by Julia and the independent Python validator. GPU use would require recording device, driver, framework determinism settings and nondeterministic operators. Until the loss and representation are scientifically specified, optimizer, learning rate, regularisation and negative-sampling counts should not be invented.","title":"E. PyTorch decision"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#F.-Proposed-architecture-and-algorithmic-analysis","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Let L be formula-string length, a distinct elements per composition, R source records, G canonical groups, T training compositions, N candidates, E element vocabulary size (117 non-O elements in the pair model), r factor rank, I accepted optimizer iterations and B≤60 line-search attempts.\n\nPath and implementation Current data structures Time Working memory and allocations\nComposition parsing Regex matches, Dict{String,Int}, sorted tuple of Pair{String,Int}, canonical String O(L + a log a), treating the fixed 118-symbol validity scan as constant O(a + L) per composition; strings and pair vectors are allocated before the immutable tuple/string.\nSQLite query and rank SQLite row iterator, Vector{Tuple{Composition,Float64}}, ranking-value vector and sortperm O(Rq·parse + N log N), where Rq is rows returned/scanned by the adapter O(N) Julia result/order storage. Standard tables apply element/arity filters after parsing; legacy tables push element predicates into SQL.\nGeneric rank! score Vector{Float64}, permutation vector O(N·S + N log N), S scorer cost O(N); permute! reorders in place after the value/order arrays are built.\nPU popularity/random Dict{String,Int}, per-candidate named tuples and SHA strings, sorted vector O(Ta + Na + N log N) O(N + E); SHA strings dominate current per-candidate allocations.\nPU maximum similarity Vector{PUVector} for training, one candidate vector at a time, score vector O(NTa² + N log N); a=3 in scope O(Ta + N), not O(NT); no pairwise score matrix is materialised.\nMP audit complete TSV/JSON byte arrays, JSON row vector, TSV row vector, Dict{Composition,Vector{NamedTuple}}, output group vector O(R·parse + R log within-group + G log G) O(input bytes + R + G). Unlike validate_database, this audit is not streaming end to end.\nSQLite full validation DB row iterator, bounded issue vectors and count dictionaries O(R·parse) plus SQLite integrity check O(max_issues + tables/categories) in Julia, excluding SQLite/page cache.\nTSV/database ingestion row generator, SQLite transaction/statements and SHA context; indexed duplicate lookup Approximately O(R log R) with the formula B-tree and inserts Streaming in Julia; database and indexes are on disk. Publication uses a no-clobber hard link.\nPair feature construction sorted composition vector, dense Matrix{Int} counts, dense Matrix{Float64} target, BitVector O(T log T + Ta + E²) O(T + E²).\nPair-model fit dense factor matrix, target/weight/count matrices, gradient and temporary factor/delta arrays O(I·B̄·E²r), where B̄ is actual line-search evaluations O(E² + Er) peak working state, but repeated broadcast temporaries cause much larger cumulative allocation. Trace is O(I).\nPair-model scoring/ranking candidate named tuples, factor dot products, SHA tie strings, sort O(Nr + N log N) O(N + Er).\n\nDeterministic ordering matters because cutoffs often intersect large exact-score ties. Input order, process hash randomisation or a different sort implementation would otherwise change Hits@K without changing the model. The versioned SHA keys make split selection, random rankings and ties reproducible across input permutations and supported processes; canonical formula is a final collision fallback.","title":"F. Proposed architecture and algorithmic analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#F.1-Existing-empirical-evidence","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"The 12-row fixture measurement reduced first in-process query latency from about 142 ms to 6 ms after representative precompilation; warm-query differences were too small to interpret. This is startup evidence, not an algorithmic speedup.\nA production legacy query returned 1,108 rows in roughly 104–106 ms warm. A streaming validation scan of 4,736,551 rows took about 39 seconds in one exploratory run.\nThe synthetic real-size similarity benchmark scored about 35.2 million candidate/training pairs in 0.132 s and allocated 3.2 MiB; full pu_rank similarity took 0.141 s and 21.8 MiB. A stored Float64 pairwise matrix would be 268.9 MiB. These are cumulative allocations, not peak RSS.\nIn the retained real pilot, similarity ranking totaled about 2.90 s across 20 splits, versus 0.45 s for popularity and 0.37 s for random, including first-call compilation effects.\nThe 120 primary pair fits totaled 18.71 s; ranking totaled 0.83 s. Mean cumulative fit allocation was about 75 MiB for composition branches and 72 MiB for system branches. Input verification, output, analysis, startup and reproduction were not included in those totals.","title":"F.1 Existing empirical evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#F.2-Next-benchmark-design","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Create a separate benchmark/Project.toml with BenchmarkTools so benchmarking dependencies do not enter the package runtime. Add:\n\nmicrobenchmarks for parsing, tie-key construction, pu_metrics, raw pair score, factor objective/gradient and sorting at controlled tie rates;\nscaling benchmarks for N, T, E and r, reporting median time, allocations and fitted slopes against the theoretical terms;\nend-to-end phase timers for verify/audit, split construction, fitting, scoring, sorting, metric computation and output;\nOS-reported peak RSS in a fresh process when making memory claims;\nmachine, OS/architecture, Julia, dependency, thread-count, dataset hash and commit/dirty-state metadata.\n\nProfile before optimizing. Likely targets are SHA string allocation in ranking, MP audit's full-file duplication and broadcast temporaries in projected gradient, but none should be called a bottleneck until a profiler on the intended workload shows it.\n\nDefensible claims after the proposed measurements include “streamed maximum similarity with O(T+N) working storage,” “deterministic O(N log N) ranking,” and a specific measured reduction in time/allocations on a named workload. Do not claim generic “faster ranking,” lower peak memory from @allocated, production-scale speedup from the 12-row fixture, or asymptotic improvement from a constant-factor change.","title":"F.2 Next benchmark design"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#F.3-Repository-integration","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Preserve every frozen protocol file and existing result bundle. Prefer additive files and a new protocol ID.\n\nsrc/\n  ranking_evaluation.jl       reusable experimental relevance/system metrics\n  pair_frequency_model.jl     direct observed-pair control\n  evaluation_suite.jl         new Research orchestration, no CLI change initially\nbenchmark/\n  Project.toml                isolated BenchmarkTools environment\n  ranking_scaling.jl          controlled N/T/tie-rate scaling\n  factor_scaling.jl           E/r/iteration and allocation scaling\nscripts/\n  run_evaluation_suite.jl     one explicit frozen-suite entry point\n  analyze_evaluation_suite.py independent reconstruction and reporting\ndocs/\n  evaluation-roadmap.md       this plan\n  evaluation-metrics.md       reusable metric semantics and schemas\n  mp-evaluation-suite-v3.md   new pinned prospective real-data protocol\ntest/\n  test_ranking_evaluation.jl\n  test_pair_frequency_model.jl\n  test_evaluation_suite.jl\n  test_evaluation_analysis.py\nreports/local/\n  mp-evaluation-suite-v3-*/   ignored memberships, rankings, factors and results\n\nReusable package code should calculate metrics and scores from in-memory canonical objects. Research orchestration should verify sources, rebuild split-local state, write complete artifacts and refuse overwrite. Python should independently read the saved schema and recompute results; it should not call Julia metric code. Plots and narrative tables belong to generated analysis output, not src/. Notebooks are unnecessary for the core workflow.\n\nAvoid modifying fields in existing schema-v1 reports. Define a new result schema with, at minimum:\n\ndataset ID/version/retrieval metadata and hashes;\naudit/preprocessing algorithm and implementation hashes;\ncode commit, dirty flag, patch/source-tree digest and relevant file hashes;\nprotocol ID/hash, split algorithm, split membership hashes and label policy;\ncandidate-generation/filter definition and exclusion counts;\nmethod/baseline/ablation configuration, seed roles and coverage policy;\nmetric algorithm/version, K values, grouping rule and denominator counts;\nJulia/Python versions, dependency locks, OS, architecture, CPU, threads and optional accelerator details;\nhashes for every deterministic output, with runtime/host measurements clearly separated.\n\nA deterministic run ID can be the SHA-256 of the canonical scientific configuration and input identities. Wall-clock timestamps and output paths must not enter that identity.","title":"F.3 Repository integration"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#G.-Implementation-sequence","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"","title":"G. Implementation sequence"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Milestone-1-—-Reusable-metric-kernel-and-system-diagnostics","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Status: implemented 14 September 2026 as the additive experimental recovery_metrics API with a metric contract and independent Julia/Python hand-worked checks. Existing frozen reports and pu_metrics were not changed.\n\nObjective: extract a rigorously specified evaluation kernel without changing any frozen result. Implement composition Hits/Recall/enrichment, optional binary NDCG, distinct/system recall and effective-system diagnostics.\nLikely changes: src/EkaCompositions.jl, docs/api-stability.md, test/runtests.jl; do not edit frozen protocols.\nNew files: src/ranking_evaluation.jl, docs/evaluation-metrics.md, test/test_ranking_evaluation.jl, test/test_evaluation_metrics.py.\nTests: hand-worked rankings; no hits/full hits; ties at and across K; duplicate canonical candidates; missing relevance; empty relevance; K>N; K=N; input-order invariance; system grouping; one system with many stoichiometries; exact Julia/ Python agreement; no mutation.\nExpected output: a versioned in-memory metrics object and deterministic TSV/ JSON row schema, exercised only on synthetic fixtures and existing saved rankings.\nDependencies: none beyond current Julia/Python standard libraries.\nScientific question answered: are current conclusions robust when recovery concentration across chemical systems is made visible?\n\nMilestones 2 and 3 are now complete. The next implementation milestone is Milestone 4, the prospective v3 protocol freeze and missing-pair ablation.","title":"Milestone 1 — Reusable metric kernel and system diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Milestone-2-—-Uniform-provenance-and-result-schema","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Status: implemented 22 September 2026 as the internal schema-v1 EvaluationSuite writer/validator and docs/evaluation-bundle-schema.md. It has only synthetic coverage; no frozen report reader or existing result was changed.\n\nObjective: make a result independently traceable and restorable without experiment-specific knowledge.\nLikely changes: research writers and validation utilities only; preserve old readers and schema-v1 bundles.\nNew files: src/evaluation_suite.jl, a schema document and synthetic golden bundle under test/fixtures/ if small enough.\nTests: schema validation; missing/extra/wrong-typed keys; source, split, config and output tampering even after internal checksum rewrite; clean and dirty tree identities; fixed-seed rerun; runtime excluded from deterministic equality; no overwrite and partial-output cleanup.\nExpected output: one run.toml, complete metrics.tsv, rankings and a deterministic inventory with explicit environment metadata.\nDependencies: Milestone 1.\nScientific question answered: can an evaluator establish exactly which data, code, split, metric and configuration produced a number?","title":"Milestone 2 — Uniform provenance and result schema"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Milestone-3-—-Direct-pair-frequency-control","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Status: implemented 22 September 2026 as the internal PairFrequencyModel. Its synthetic suite coverage verifies the direct target, training/candidate isolation, system-holdout zero-score property and deterministic ties. No real result has been run.\n\nObjective: formalise the unfactorised target matrix as a baseline.\nLikely changes: the new suite only; leave src/element_pair_model.jl and prior evidence unchanged.\nNew files: src/pair_frequency_model.jl, test/test_pair_frequency_model.jl.\nTests: hand-counted pairs; canonical duplicate rejection; permutation invariance; same-system stoichiometry ties; unseen-pair zero; system-holdout all-zero property; candidate/label isolation; deterministic cutoff ties.\nExpected output: complete rankings and coverage/tie diagnostics on synthetic splits.\nDependencies: Milestones 1–2.\nScientific question answered: does low-rank factorisation add cross-pair generalisation beyond direct observed-pair frequency?","title":"Milestone 3 — Direct pair-frequency control"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Milestone-4-—-Freeze-v3-protocol-and-missing-pair-ablation","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Objective: prospectively define the real comparison before inspecting any new result. Specify system holdout primary, composition control, all carried label policies, raw pair frequency, the main converged/fixed-compute model and the alpha=0 ablation.\nLikely changes: add a new protocol pin in src/mp_recovery.jl; never edit an old protocol or reuse an old ID.\nNew files: docs/mp-evaluation-suite-v3.md, test/test_evaluation_suite.jl and scripts/run_evaluation_suite.jl.\nTests: training-only preprocessing, outer membership equality with v2, split-local fitting, alpha-only configuration difference, complete candidate coverage, common-candidate score invariance, and frozen grid completeness.\nExpected output: signed-off protocol bytes/hash, synthetic preflight and a no-results design freeze.\nDependencies: Milestones 1–3 and a decision on whether primary optimisation means convergence or an explicitly fixed compute budget.\nScientific question answered: is the next real run capable of separating marginal frequency, direct pair memory, low-rank generalisation and the weak-zero assumption fairly?","title":"Milestone 4 — Freeze v3 protocol and missing-pair ablation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Milestone-5-—-Execute,-independently-validate-and-report","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Objective: run the frozen suite once on the retained snapshot, preserving all favorable and unfavorable branches.\nLikely changes: no scientific code after freeze except a separately versioned correctness fix with affected reruns.\nNew files: scripts/analyze_evaluation_suite.py; local ignored result and analysis directories; an attributed aggregate tracked summary only after review.\nTests: Python reconstruction of metrics, raw pair scores, ranking order, system diagnostics, factors/objective where applicable and schema/inventory; complete same-environment rerun comparison.\nExpected output: per-split rankings/metrics, macro summaries, system-aware concentration results, runtimes and explicit negative-result retention.\nDependencies: Milestone 4 freeze and artifact-specific sharing review.\nScientific question answered: does the element-pair model add robust value under unseen-system recovery, and is that value dependent on missing-pair penalisation or clustered stoichiometric hits?","title":"Milestone 5 — Execute, independently validate and report"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Milestone-6-—-Reproducible-performance-characterization","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Objective: connect theoretical complexity to measured phase costs and only then optimize a demonstrated bottleneck.\nLikely changes: performance-sensitive implementation only after baseline capture; scientific ranking order must remain byte-identical.\nNew files: isolated benchmark/ environment/scripts and a benchmark report.\nTests: golden ranking digests before/after, allocation ceilings where stable, and scaling fixtures separated from correctness tests.\nExpected output: raw benchmark data, environment metadata, profiles and a before/after comparison for any accepted optimization.\nDependencies: stable Milestone 1 API/schema.\nScientific question answered: what actually limits scaling, and which runtime/ memory claims are supported?","title":"Milestone 6 — Reproducible performance characterization"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Milestone-7-—-PyTorch-gate,-not-implementation-by-default","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Objective: decide whether a new representation and PU objective answer a remaining scientific question.\nLikely changes/new files: a feasibility document first; no dependency until the gate is positive.\nTests/output: if approved later, deterministic synthetic gradient/loss checks, negative-sampler isolation, CPU reproducibility, shared-split schema compliance and Julia/Python metric agreement.\nDependencies: Milestone 5 error analysis and appropriate feature provenance.\nScientific question answered: would nonlinearity or a richer representation, rather than merely a different optimizer, improve the same recovery task?","title":"Milestone 7 — PyTorch gate, not implementation by default"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#H.-Benchmark-matrix","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"The following is the recommended scientific grid. “20 splits” means descriptive overlapping holdouts; it is not 20 independent datasets. Every row uses K = 100 primary and K = 20, 50, 200 secondary, with Hits, Recall, enrichment and system diagnostics from the same complete ranking.\n\nMethod Split Policies Seed/repetition Runtime class Purpose\nAnalytical uniform random + deterministic hash realization System primary; composition control original, exclude mixed, unlabel mixed 20 split seeds; one declared ranking seed each Very low Calibrate prevalence and tie-independent chance behavior.\nElement popularity System primary; composition control all three Deterministic per split Low Test against marginal historical element frequency.\nMaximum composition cosine System primary; composition control all three Deterministic per split Medium, O(NT) comparisons Diagnose dependence on near stoichiometric analogues.\nRaw pair frequency System primary; composition control all three Deterministic per split Low Separate direct pair memory from low-rank completion.\nRank-4 element-pair model, prospectively chosen convergence/compute rule System primary; composition control all three Fixed initialization; independently refit per split Medium Main learned association comparator.\nRank-4 alpha=0 ablation System primary; composition control all three Same initialization and fit rule Medium Test dependence on weak-zero missing-pair loss.\nOptional leave-element-out stress test, all simple methods first Element cold-start policy chosen prospectively One split per adequately supported element or fixed declared subset Medium Expose transfer limits for unseen elements; not a leaderboard extension.\n\nDo not repeat deterministic models under arbitrary “evaluation seeds.” If initialisation sensitivity becomes a question, predeclare multiple initialisation seeds and report all of them as a separate stability experiment rather than selecting the best restart.","title":"H. Benchmark matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#H.1-Required-cross-cutting-tests","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"","title":"H.1 Required cross-cutting tests"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Metrics","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Hand-compute Hits, Recall, observed-label fraction, enrichment, DCG/NDCG, distinct hit systems, system recall and effective systems.\nCover K=1, K=N, K>N, unsorted K input and duplicate K.\nReject empty candidate rankings, duplicate canonical candidates, duplicate relevance, relevance outside the candidate universe and empty relevance unless a future API explicitly defines nullable denominators. Preserve pu_metrics's current strict behavior for compatibility.\nTest all-zero relevance scores, all-relevant rankings, no top-K hits and ties spanning K.\nVerify metric values do not depend on candidate input order once a ranking is supplied, and that metric computation does not mutate inputs.\nCompare Julia with an independent Python implementation on golden fixtures; include integer/rational expected-random values to avoid floating ambiguity.","title":"Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Splits-and-leakage","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Assert train/candidate composition disjointness and, for the primary protocol, train/candidate chemical-system disjointness.\nInclude proportional formulas, symbol permutations, polymorph records, mixed flags, unlabelled-only systems, unsupported records and unequal system sizes.\nAssert deterministic membership under input and seed-list permutation.\nChange evaluator labels and unrelated candidate membership while holding training fixed; fitted state and shared-candidate scores must not change.\nIf inner validation is introduced, assert outer candidates never appear in inner training, preprocessing, sampling or selection.","title":"Splits and leakage"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Baselines-and-ablation","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Check popularity and pair counts on hand-worked examples.\nProve raw pair frequency is zero for every candidate in a valid system holdout.\nVerify maximum similarity against direct pairwise similarity and a Python calculation on a small fixture.\nVerify alpha=0 changes only the missing-pair weights/configuration; observed targets, regularisation, initialization, stopping rule and membership must match.\nTest complete finite score coverage and explicit cold/unseen states.","title":"Baselines and ablation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Ranking-and-reproducibility","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Golden-test split, random and tie SHA keys, including exact-score tie blocks at K and synthetic hash-collision fallback logic where feasible.\nReject duplicate candidates rather than silently retaining one.\nRe-run with reversed inputs and perturbed global RNG state.\nValidate result schema types, required fields, deterministic run ID and every output checksum; reject semantically corrupted membership/results even after checksums are rewritten.\nCompare two same-environment synthetic runs byte for byte except declared runtime/host fields.","title":"Ranking and reproducibility"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Performance","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Keep correctness fixtures small. Put realistic scaling and allocation checks in the isolated benchmark suite, not the unit suite.\nRecord compilation-inclusive and warmed measurements separately.\nUse a fresh process for peak RSS and end-to-end latency.\nVerify any optimization preserves complete ranking digests, not only top K.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#I.-Risks-and-open-questions","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Scientific target: Is the goal database completion, unseen-system transfer or actual experimental recommendation? The current data answer only the first two, using a provenance proxy.\nCandidate universe: There is no enumerator for absent formulas. Ranking already catalogued MP compositions cannot measure prospective discovery among genuinely unknown candidates.\nGround truth: theoretical=false is not literature-audited synthesis success; mixed groups show label ambiguity. Unlabelled is not failure.\nWithin-system dependence: The main model ignores stoichiometry and generates very large tie blocks. Composition hits can be concentrated in a few systems.\nOptimisation: The frozen primary model often stopped at its iteration cap. A future protocol must choose convergence handling before results, not promote the favorable checkpoint afterward.\nSplit dependence: The 20 hash holdouts overlap. They quantify sensitivity to membership but do not create independent experimental replicates.\nCross-design confounding: Existing system and composition pools differ in training size, candidate size and prevalence. Without a prospectively defined matched control, protocol differences are descriptive, not causal effects of chemical separation.\nChemical OOD: System disjointness does not prevent close periodic-family analogues. A family split needs an external, justified taxonomy.\nTime: The snapshot has retrieval time but no defensible first-observation time. Chronological evaluation is currently unavailable.\nDistribution and reproducibility: Aggregate attributed results may be  shareable, but exact public reruns still depend on local record-level artifacts  and final permissions review.","title":"I. Risks and open questions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#J.-CV-/-README-claims-unlocked","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"Claims should be tied to completed evidence, not planned work.\n\nCompleted milestone Defensible claim Claims still not supported\nExisting repository “Implemented deterministic positive–unlabelled composition-recovery benchmarks with composition-safe and chemical-system-grouped holdouts.” Prospective discovery, synthesis success, independent replicates.\nExisting repository “Benchmarked element popularity, maximum composition similarity and a nonnegative element-pair factor model on identical candidate pools with independent Python validation.” Superiority to published state of the art or reproduction of Seko.\nMilestone 1 “Implemented reproducible fixed-budget recovery and system-aware ranking diagnostics with Julia/Python cross-checks.” Chemically meaningful diversity unless a chemistry-aware definition is added.\nMilestones 2–3 “Added a traceable result schema and an unfactorised pair-frequency control to isolate low-rank generalisation.” Model value until the frozen real comparison is complete.\nMilestones 4–5 “Evaluated a nonnegative element-pair model and missing-pair ablation under leakage-resistant unseen-system recovery, reporting every split and label policy.” General materials discovery or causal effect of system separation.\nMilestone 6 “Profiled the ranking pipeline and reduced [named phase] runtime/allocations by [measured amount] on [named workload] while preserving ranking digests.” Generic speed or peak-memory claims without the corresponding measurements.\nMilestone 7, only if approved and completed “Compared Julia and PyTorch models under identical splits, candidates, filters and metric implementations.” A PyTorch claim merely because a toy network exists; superiority without a prospectively controlled result.\n\nThe existing README can already credibly claim reproducible ranking evaluation, leakage-resistant grouped evaluation, baseline comparisons, label sensitivity, algorithmic determinism and same-platform exact reruns. It should not claim an “ablation study of element-pair interactions” until the raw pair control and/or missing-pair ablation is executed under a new protocol.","title":"J. CV / README claims unlocked"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/evaluation-roadmap/#Final-direction","page":"Evaluation roadmap: rigorous recovery, controls and scaling","text":"The best next investment is not a larger model. It is a small evaluation kernel that makes system-level concentration impossible to overlook, followed by the raw pair-frequency control. If those results show that the factor model's apparent system-holdout advantage survives system-aware evaluation and truly comes from low-rank cross-pair generalisation, a richer stoichiometric model becomes a well-posed next question. If they do not, the negative result is scientifically more valuable than adding PyTorch or tuning rank until one branch improves.\n\n","title":"Final direction"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pu-evaluation/#Verified-positive–unlabelled-recovery-evaluation","page":"Verified positive–unlabelled recovery evaluation","text":"eka benchmark-pu is separate from the existing binary eka benchmark. It verifies saved split bundles, then evaluates every declared method at every declared budget: random, training-only element popularity, and maximum training-composition similarity. Joshua Corbett is the author of this implementation and documentation.\n\nAll three primary methods declared by the protocol are implemented. The full real pilot has now completed after the implementation freeze, with all scientific outputs reproduced exactly from a clean checkout. Results remain local; see the evidence and restore guide. External scores are not a method here; the external score review records that decision. The frozen protocol remains the unchanged pre-evaluation record; its statement that the CLI was not yet available describes the freeze date.","title":"Verified positive–unlabelled recovery evaluation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pu-evaluation/#Offline-end-to-end-example","page":"Verified positive–unlabelled recovery evaluation","text":"Run from the repository root with installed Julia dependencies. No Python, MP client, API key, or private data is required for this core pipeline. Every output directory must be new; change the names when repeating this example.\n\nmkdir -p reports/local\njulia --project=. examples/mp_recovery/make_snapshot.jl reports/local/pu-example-snapshot\njulia --project=. bin/eka audit-mp \\\n  --snapshot reports/local/pu-example-snapshot \\\n  --output reports/local/pu-example-audit\njulia --project=. bin/eka split-mp \\\n  --snapshot reports/local/pu-example-snapshot \\\n  --audit reports/local/pu-example-audit \\\n  --output reports/local/pu-example-splits \\\n  --synthetic --budget 1 4\njulia --project=. bin/eka benchmark-pu \\\n  --splits reports/local/pu-example-splits \\\n  --snapshot reports/local/pu-example-snapshot \\\n  --audit reports/local/pu-example-audit \\\n  --output reports/local/pu-example-results \\\n  --synthetic\n\nThe example has 20 splits, three methods and two budgets, producing 120 metric rows. Every split contains eight training positives, two held-out positives and four candidates. These arbitrary fixtures test software, not physical plausibility. All three methods use identical candidate membership within each split.\n\nThe CLI accepts --splits, --snapshot, --audit, --output, and explicit --synthetic mode. Budgets and all three kinds of seed come from the verified bundle; the evaluator does not offer overrides to select favorable subsets. Synthetic mode must match both the bundle and the original snapshot. Real mode is pinned to the preserved real v1 snapshot and all its declared splits/budgets. Keep real outputs local and do not run them before the experiment freeze.","title":"Offline end-to-end example"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pu-evaluation/#Verification-before-scoring","page":"Verified positive–unlabelled recovery evaluation","text":"load_mp_recovery(bundle, snapshot, audit; synthetic=false):\n\nCaptures and verifies the bundle manifest, frozen protocol, original snapshot and audit provenance, and copied provenance files.\nVerifies the expected split list, counts, algorithm, scope, seeds, budgets, implementation-copy hashes, split-manifest hashes, and membership hashes.\nReconstructs every expected split from the verified original grouped records and compares the exact training/candidate/holdout/label file bytes.\nCompletes validation of all splits before any ranker can run.\n\nUpdating a corrupted label file and its checksums is insufficient: reconstruction still detects disagreement with the frozen source. Missing splits, altered seeds, noncanonical/duplicate memberships, train/pool overlap, and extra manifest file paths fail validation. Only fixed allowlisted filenames are read from manifest structures; stored source code is never executed.\n\nCompatibility is based on schema v1, the fixed split algorithm, verified original inputs and complete membership reconstruction. Historical wrapper/CLI code need not equal the current evaluator. Preserved source-copy hashes prove internal integrity, not the authenticity of an unknown producer. Reconstructed membership and the pinned real source provide the scientific validation; synthetic bundles make no real-data authenticity claim.\n\nThe loader returns separate inputs and evaluator-owned evaluation fields, plus captured provenance bytes. This is a software interface boundary, not an OS sandbox for arbitrary third-party code.","title":"Verification before scoring"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pu-evaluation/#Ranker-and-metric-interfaces","page":"Verified positive–unlabelled recovery evaluation","text":"using EkaCompositions\n\ntraining = [\"CaTiO3\", \"BaTiO3\"]\ncandidates = [\"MgTiO3\", \"SrTiO3\", \"CaZrO3\", \"MgAl2O4\"]\nranked = pu_rank(training, candidates; method=\"similarity\",\n                 ranking_seed=10000, tie_seed=20260901)\n# Arbitrary synthetic heldout example; evaluator data is not passed to pu_rank.\nmetrics = pu_metrics([r.composition for r in ranked], [\"SrTiO3\"]; budgets=[1, 4])\n\npu_rank accepts only training/candidate compositions, method, ranking seed and tie seed. No labels, provenance flags, stored scores, source IDs, or bundle paths are accepted. Duplicate canonical compositions and train/pool overlap fail.\n\nPopularity counts each element once per training composition and scores c as sum(training_frequency[e] for e in species(c)) / (length(c) * training_count). Counts are rebuilt per call; unseen elements contribute zero. Oxygen contributes a constant 1/3 in this ternary pool. Scores are finite Float64 values, ordered descending with the frozen score-independent hash tie policy.\n\nSimilarity scores each candidate by the maximum cosine similarity of its reduced element-count vector to any single training composition: max(dot(c,t) / (norm(c) * norm(t)) for t in training), clamped to [0,1]. It is the pairwise cosine Eka's similarity already computes, maximised over the training set; the tests assert exact Float64 equality with that function rather than a tolerance. No reference is chosen using held-out labels, there is no atom weighting, learned embedding, oxidation feature, fitting step, or search over similarity variants, and the stored-score tie breaker of the SQLite SimilarityRanking path is not inherited. Ordering uses the same frozen score-independent hash tie policy as popularity.\n\nReferences are rebuilt from the training argument on every call, so per-split training isolation holds by construction and a candidate's score depends on the training set alone, never on the rest of the candidate pool. Comparisons are streamed one candidate at a time: peak working memory is proportional to the number of compositions, and no candidate-by-training matrix is ever allocated.\n\nRandom ranks by the ascending exact SHA-256 key from the protocol, then shared tie key and formula. It has no fitted numeric score: the saved score cell is empty, and random_key records the exact ordering key. The popularity and similarity random_key cells are empty. All three methods retain tie_key, and none of them consults evaluator labels.\n\npu_metrics accepts a complete, duplicate-free ordering and a nonempty held-out subset. Every holdout must appear in the ordering. Invalid budgets and zero holdout denominators fail explicitly. It reports:\n\nField Meaning\nhits Held-out positives among the first k candidates\nobserved_label_fraction hits / k; not synthesis success rate\nheldout_recall hits / total holdouts\nobserved_label_enrichment (hits / k) / (holdouts / candidates)\nrandom_expected_hits k × holdouts / candidates, shown numerically\nrandom_expected_hits_numerator, random_expected_hits_denominator The same expectation as an exact reduced fraction\n\nUniform-random expectation is analytical sampling without replacement. Individual hash-based random runs need not equal it. Unlabelled candidates are never assigned confirmed-negative labels. No specificity, false-positive rate, ordinary negative accuracy, significance test or population confidence interval is computed.","title":"Ranker and metric interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pu-evaluation/#Reports-and-reproducibility","page":"Verified positive–unlabelled recovery evaluation","text":"benchmark_pu(bundle, snapshot, audit, output; synthetic=false) saves:\n\nArtifact Contents\nconfig.toml Protocol and input hashes, methods/budgets/seeds, runtime versions, source/dependency-file hashes, deterministic output hashes\nmetrics.tsv Every method × split × budget, with raw counts and metric denominators\nsplit-XX/random.tsv, popularity.tsv, similarity.tsv Complete ranks, canonical formulas, scores/keys, and labels attached after ranking\nreport.md All metric rows and interpretation limits; prominently identifies synthetic status\nruntime.tsv Ranking seconds for every method/split, including any first-call compilation\ninputs/ Captured bundle manifests, membership and provenance used by the evaluator\nimplementation/ Relevant current code, Project.toml, and Manifest.toml when present\n\nThe writer reserves a new directory exclusively and never overwrites an existing path. Failure removes only the new directory created by that invocation. Input bundles and source snapshots are never modified. Exact rerun comparisons exclude only runtime.tsv; scientific outputs and deterministic configuration should match under the same source bytes, inputs and environment.\n\nThe report distinguishes split sensitivity from independent experiments. It does not claim success merely because one method wins. The paired primary comparison (similarity minus popularity hits at k=100 on each identical split) is computed during analysis from these raw per-split rows; benchmark_pu deliberately writes the inputs to that comparison rather than a summary verdict. The implementation freeze, real experiment, paired report and clean-checkout reproduction are now complete locally. scripts/analyze_pu_pilot.py independently validates saved outputs and generates the predefined paired and descriptive summaries. The data handling restrictions still apply.\n\nThe follow-on label-sensitivity workflow is separately versioned and does not change this v1 runner or its scientific outputs. Its original-policy controls verify exact compatibility with the preserved pilot.","title":"Reports and reproducibility"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pu-evaluation/#Runtime-and-memory","page":"Verified positive–unlabelled recovery evaluation","text":"The frozen protocol implies about 35.2 million candidate/training pairs per split (4,288 training positives x 8,218 candidates). scripts/benchmark_pu_similarity.jl measures that size on generated formulas only; it reads no snapshot, split bundle, MP record, or label, and produces no recovery result.\n\njulia --startup-file=no --project=. scripts/benchmark_pu_similarity.jl\n\nMeasured on Julia 1.12.6, macOS, Apple silicon, 31 August 2026:\n\nStep Time Allocated\nStreamed maximum similarity, 35.2M pairs 0.132 s 3.2 MiB\npu_rank similarity, including tie keys and sort 0.141 s 21.8 MiB\npu_rank popularity, same pool 0.015 s 20.3 MiB\npu_rank random, same pool 0.015 s 20.9 MiB\n\nA full candidate-by-training Float64 matrix would need 268.9 MiB per split; the streamed maximum never allocates one, and most of pu_rank's allocation is the per-candidate SHA-256 tie and random keys shared by all three methods. At about 0.14 s per split, all 20 splits cost a few seconds, so no batching tier, caching, or approximation is needed and none was added. These are one machine's numbers, not a target or a performance claim; record fresh timings alongside a real run.","title":"Runtime and memory"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/mp-pu-evaluation/#Tests","page":"Verified positive–unlabelled recovery evaluation","text":"julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\npython3 -m unittest discover -s test -p 'test_mp_export.py' -v\n\nTests cover hand-calculated hit/recall/enrichment cases, no hits, full recovery, exact random expectation, invalid denominators/budgets, golden hash keys and ties, training-only counts, evaluation-label independence, corrupted bundles including rewritten checksums, complete split validation before scoring, complete rankings, CLI behaviour, no-overwrite and deterministic reruns.\n\nSimilarity additionally has hand-calculated cosine values, exactly tied scores resolved by the shared tie policy, agreement to the last bit with Eka's existing similarity over a generated pool, evidence that the score is the maximum over training rather than the first or last reference, per-split training isolation (widening and narrowing the training set, and scoring a candidate alone), score invariance under changed evaluation labels, untouched global RNG state, repeated identical reruns, and an allocation bound proving no pairwise matrix is built. GitHub CI also executes the Python fixture → Julia audit → split → PU evaluation workflow on Linux.\n\n","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/production-validation/#Production-database-verification","page":"Production database verification","text":"The upstream recommender-2024-07-01.sqlite was inspected locally on 2026-08-30 with Julia 1.12.6 and SQLite.jl 1.8.2. It remains outside the repository, is explicitly ignored by Git, and was opened read-only. SHA-256 before and after verification:\n\n9ee6f6a81f80cab74d967b70d4b0ad5d61c55ecf70e6b689c8a2c59e489a58fb","title":"Production database verification"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/production-validation/#Schema-and-full-audit","page":"Production database verification","text":"Table Scope Rows Invalid/unsupported under this package's contract Score range\ndata2 Binary 208,299 5,793 0.01–1.0364\ndata3 Ternary 1,474,012 16,135 0.01–1.18551\ndata4ionic Ionic quaternary 2,588,412 0 0.001–1.12303\ndata5ionic Ionic quinary 465,828 0 0.001–1.17031\nTotal  4,736,551 21,928 \n\nSQLite quick_check passed. The streaming record audit accepted 4,714,623 rows and reported 21,928 rows with unsupported element symbols. Example: Dy1D2 contains D, outside the MVP's 118-element symbol set. All audited rows in the two ionic tables passed the formula/score/redundant-column checks. These are software-contract checks, not validation of scientific plausibility.\n\nStrict validation stops on the first unsupported row. Report mode counts all affected records without modifying them. Queries remain strict and can fail on an unsupported candidate; they do not silently filter out such records or equate isotopes with ordinary hydrogen. Explicit isotope support requires a separate type/filtering decision. Consequently this package does not claim complete compatibility with every production record or every original query.\n\nThe unsupported symbols observed in the source columns are:\n\ndata2: Bx, Cx, Cz, D, Fx, Hx, Ix, Nx, Ox, Px, Sx, T, Vx.\ndata3: D, Hx, Kx.\n\n20,120 rows contain D or T (counting each row once). The remaining 1,808 rejected rows involve other unsupported labels. No meaning is assumed for labels such as Bx or Cz; their source conventions must be established before designing an adapter. Simply accepting every token as an element would break the validated composition contract.","title":"Schema and full audit"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/production-validation/#Reference-query-comparisons","page":"Production database verification","text":"scripts/verify_production.jl uses a separate streaming reference matching the earlier Julia prototype's element-set and strict-threshold semantics. It canonicalizes supported formulas for comparison and applies the new deterministic tie-break; it does not claim byte-for-byte output parity. It also verifies that unsupported queries fail explicitly.\n\nElements Nary Stored score constraint Result\nAl, Si, O 4 > 0.01 All 1,106 results match\nN 4 > 0.01 All 4,434 results match\nMg, Zn 2 > 0.01 All 83 results match\nMg, Zn 2, 3 > 0.3 All 6 results match\nN 5 > 0.3 Both return zero results\nMg, Zn 2, 3 > 0.01 Original returns 1,762 rows, including 5 unsupported formulas; package rejects explicitly\n\nThe last case includes D1Mg1Zn1; supporting the table layout alone does not make those isotope-containing candidates compatible with the element-only Composition type. The verification script reports this as an expected limitation, not successful parity.\n\nThe original script uses score > threshold. The package preserves its MVP default of >=; --strict-threshold or inclusive=false selects original semantics. For example, Al–Si–O with nary 4 returns 1,108 rows at >= 0.01, versus 1,106 at > 0.01.","title":"Reference-query comparisons"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/production-validation/#Reproduce","page":"Production database verification","text":"julia --project=. scripts/verify_production.jl /path/to/recommender-2024-07-01.sqlite\njulia --project=. scripts/verify_production.jl /path/to/recommender-2024-07-01.sqlite --full-audit\njulia --project=. bin/eka validate -d /path/to/recommender-2024-07-01.sqlite --report\n\nThe verification script passes its supported-query and expected-rejection assertions; that is not an assertion that the full database is supported. The standalone validate --report command exits 2 for this database because unsupported records were found.","title":"Reproduce"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/production-validation/#Ranking-example","page":"Production database verification","text":"With binary Mg–Zn candidates above an inclusive stored-score threshold of 0.3, similarity to Mg2Zn produces:\n\n# Composition, Score, Similarity\nMg2Zn1   0.37147   1.00000\nMg3Zn1   0.39168   0.98995\n\nThe stored score is unchanged; similarity is a separate untrained mathematical measure. This is an example of reordering existing predictions, not new scientific scoring.\n\n","title":"Ranking example"}]
}
